        <p>The tgilliant collection of solid Brass Bedsteads, having tha- appertrance of a mountain of gold. Hundreds of exquisitely finished brass bedstetds at incredible low prices are oo show from Us 6,1 up to £2.5. Two thousand bedsteads from 5s 6d may be inspected. besides many wondetful bargains in. Second-hand iledsteods. For instanee, a magnificent mas- sive solid brass be stead, which east £25 when new, is shown far £4 los. I must now pass on to describe the enormous Beafroom Suite Showr, urn, which is the lafgesr in Manchester, tnd contains 150 bedroom suites from 35s to £65. The suites ' are shown in corridors. with plenty of width and seaCe I- lowed for in:pectic:rt. The great feature itt " 1 t,e Pantheon" Is that there is plfnty of room, and a btilliant light enabling you to see what you art: purchatring perfertly. In this riepart- ment you have splendid opportunities for securing a bargain. I notieed one, a fine handsome majestie6 feet Bedroom Suite, In richly figured wood. al solid; wardrole, 6 feet, dressing table 5 feet, waslista,el 4 feet 6 inches, three chfurs.tewel rail,. and pedestal, in cot-Mitt:on quite equal to new. This suite is fitted with every convenience, and cost from £7O to £3O. The• pike is only 24 guineas, the tact being that Worsley's must buy a tremendous quantity if goods awfully cheap. and, simply ask a smell profit or it, no matte, haw littie the cost. I have seen bargains worth three ant four times the money asked for them. The best plan to adopt to prove this is to, take the trouble to pay a visit to "The Pantheon" and see- for yourself. Ac I said before you are not asked to buy; you can walk in and walk out. with perfect freedom, experiencing only f 10UfaliSY AND POLITBNESS. / To give a full account of the vast stores cr( " The Pantheon " would occupy the whole et this paper. I may, however, just mention one fact, and that is you cannot ask Worsley's for any article of furniture without being quickly shown ss htt you require.