        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The marriage of Princess Maud of Wales being fixed to take place in July recalls to mind the fact that, for weddings, this particular month has always been a favourite one with the Royal Family. The late Princess Alice, Princess Christian, her daughter, Princess Louise; Princess Henry of Battenberg, the Duchess of Fife, and the Duke of York, were all marl ied in the month of July. It does int appear to be generally known that the Duke of Marlborotigh first met his future wife at Blen- heim, when she came to England with her mother to be presented, and stayed at the palace, as a guest of Lily, Duchess of Marlborough. The young Duchess is by no means fond of a gay town life and it seems probable that the greater part of her time will be spent at Blen- heim. By the way, there are two coming marriages in the Vanderbilt family, that of Cornelius, brother to the young Duchess of Marlborough, and that of Miss Gertrude Vanderbilt, who is cousin to her Grace. Cornelius Vanderbilt is about to marry Miss Grace Wilson,a young lady very well known in the most exclu- sive circles of what is sometimes styled the aristocracy of the United States, while Miss Gertrude Vanderbilt who is prospective heiress to about £5,000,000 is to marry Mr. Harry Payne-Whitney, a fine athletic-looking youth with dark hair, dark skin, and a shy manner. Be is the only son of ex-Senator William C. Whitney and prospective heir to the Payne-Whitney millions. Those of us who have enjoyed Miss Maud Jeffries' almost ideal representation of Mercia in the" Sign of the Cross," will note with regret that her stay amongst us is shortly to come to an end. About the commencement of June she returns to America, and bids farewell not only to her English admirers, but to the profession as well. Like her distinguished countrywoman, Miss Mary Anderson, she has grown weary of the labours, anxieties, and triumphs of stage life. Apropos, it is a strange coincidence that the well- known actress Miss Kate Rorke, who takes the part of St. Hulda, the beautiful Protestant in the new play at the Shaftesbury Theatre, London, is a member of the Roman Catholic Church. Miss Rorke is highly esteemed in Roman Catholic circles, and frequently organises and gives entertainments in aid of the charitable work done by her co-religionists. There is no doubt that the dramatic profecsion is a model to every other for its esprit de corps and genero- sity and enthusiasm for good causes. A Nursing Home is now being established for actors and actresses, at which the charges will be suitable to poor as well as rich members. Some time ago an effort was made to start a home of this sort for literary men and women, but it fell through for the want of co-operation that is so noticeable amongst this profession. What with the army of struggling women journalists and novelists such a home is urgently needed,and placed upon a self-support- ing basisonight be of equal advantage to young literary men, a large proportion of whom live alone, and are in sad straits when attacked by illness necessitating nurs- ing. In connection with the proposed Orphanage where the children of actors and actresses may be brought up and trained to earn a living,a large and novel bazaar is to be held in London in June next. One of the new features is to be a collection of dolls, which will be dressed by four hundred actresses in the characters they like best, and which will be sold by auction on the last day by a well-known comedian. Another feature will be a com- petition amongst twelve smart young actors upon a practical subject usually left to women. This will, un- doubtedly afford immense fvousement and merriment to the feminine spectators and judges. In some clubs it is a tradition that at all risks our sex must not be permitted to enter their precincts, and among old clubbites the bare suggestion that members should occasionally be able to entertain their women folk within the sacred walls, rouses frenzied horror. Quite lately however the National Liberal Club saw an innevation of this sort. A social dinner was arranged, at which a limited number of members were enabled to offer hospitality to their women relatives and frienda,and between sixty and seventy sat down to a rigidly simple meal. The billiard and card rooms were not disturbed, and the smoking rooms were not invaded, nevertheless some of the members are exceedingly wrathful and there will probably be a huge discussion over the ques-</p>
        <p>The year's summary of the membership of the Wesleyan Methodist body furnishes a number of very interesting hints regarding the condition of the rural population. A decrease in the member- ship is shown by the report of 3,718 in certain ,:istricts, and an increase of 1.443 in others, both figures being no doubt the result of migrations !nerely of the Wesleyan population. But. rather ore than 2.000 of the number disappear, and can- not be traced. The decreases chiefly occur in the agricultural counties of East Anglia, and in the mining districts of Cornwall. A number of the for- Tiler may be supposed to have been swallowed up 2n town life, and have not yet resumed connection with the body; and some may be seeking better :ortune in the colonies. The increase:a have been Aiefly in the northern counties of England, and have been in a very much smaller proportion than I he loss was in Cornwall,which amounted to 34 per- -1 000, or in East Anglia, where it amounted to 20 pr 1,000. What has happened to Methodism has pr,liably happened to other religious communi- ies also, and thus. WP stumble upon traces of many ri village tragedy for which agricultural and indus- trial depression is responsible.</p>
        <p>Two or A KlND.—Sitauchin : "If I had your money I know what I'd be." Glosefist : "What would you be?" Sitanchin : "Just as mean as you are." SING OE PLAY ?—Miss Amateur: "Are you musical, Professor Bisten ? " Professor Bisten : " Yes ; but if you were goiug to play anything don't mind my feelings." BOILING IT Dow.—Perry: "Do you spend much time reading the papers ? " Berry: "No, I skip the criminal news.' "Who is the master of this house ? " asked the agent of the man who answered his ring. "Well," was the cautious response, in a resigned tone, "I am the husband and father." _ - -</p>
        <p>tion a little later on. Two lectures on fearsome subjects have recently been delivered. One of them under the auspices of the Royal Meteorological Society dealt with " Dust,"and the other, under the auspices of the Gaelic Society, with" Bag- pipes" although at firet sight it seems improbable, there are certain analogies between the two topics. The lec- turer was able to shew that dust is practically übiquit- ous—Dust particles have been found on the highest mountains and on the surface of the ocean thousand of miles from any coast. The bagpipes too, are everywhere, their sound has gone oat into all lands. Dust, again a matter out of place, a remark which in the opinion of the unsympathetic Southron, applies equally to all the bagpipes South of the Tweed. Kilted chiffon bodices are likely to be more worn than ever this year,by fashionable women, the only difference being that all the newest are enchanced by a zouave, or braces, of some glittering material. I would however warn those of my readers who are not possessed of an unlimited dress allowance to beware of this particular material. In appearance it is all the heart of woman can desire, but its wearing powers are exceedingly limited and it is only suitable for evening blouses. Grena- dine is almost as pretty, and far more durable. Elastic belts are en evidence again. They were intro- duced last year and were popular enough on the Conti- nent, but English women seemed doubtful of them. In white, these belts need to be worn with discretion. I remember one woman of ample proportion last year who accentuated her own lack of waist by a wide zone of white elastic. The vision is still to me a haunting memory. In black elastic, embroidered with jet and steel beds they are better however, and the very wide ones are. peculiarly becoming to slender figures. The fashion in hairdressing is once more undergoing a change, but the process is slow. Indeed all our fashions change slowly. Novelties, all in a rush do not suit our taste at all, and we always wait (until our Parisian sis- ters have almost worn out a mode before we decide to accept it. Just now, our hair to be correct, must be arranged loseiy, setting outwards round the nape of our necks. Then it must be coiled neatly and worn rather high on the head. French w:men are piling their hair in a knot or coil on the top of their heads and form a resting place for the latest hats which are tilted right on to the eyebrows, but it will in all probability be some months before we get as far as this. 'the Rontgen rays which have been a little quiet lately have a very startling sensation for us again just now. It seems they are going to kill the bacteria of cholera, diptheria, pneumonia, influenza, glanders, typhoid, tuberculonis,and anthrax. This news hails from America. It all seems to me a great mystery. What happens to the bacteria ? Do they die of fright ? Lord Rosebery once called the Home Rule Bill a leap into the light. Evi- dently such leaps are to be quite fatal to bacteria. Those of us who happen to be possessed of white gloves that decline to have any more cleaning operations performed upon them, will doubtless be glad to hear of a method whereby they may be dyed a pretty tan. Take some water in which a lump of saffron has been boiled and leave the gloves to infuse all night. In the m]rn- ing squeeze them in a cloth and hang them out of the sun, but in the wind to dry. Here is a delicious receipt for Welsh rabbit. Take one cupful of crumbled cheese, one of milk and one egg. Melt the cheese in the milk, then add the egg, well beaten, and pepper and salt to taste. Pour over hot but- tered toast and serve immediately.—Madge.</p>
        <p>MODERN LOVE.—" I love the sea," she said to the poor young man, as they sat on the beach. "I don't ee why," he responded, dejectedly ; "it hasn't got any money." AGRICULTURAL NOTE.—Amy Belle: "Don't you think she has a cultivated voice ?" Sue Sharp: ." yes: it certainly is harrowing." Young Chip (who is just recovering from a bicycle experience) : "Pa, did the martyrs of the Middle Ages ride bicycles ? " Old Block: "Why, no; what gave you that idea?" Young Chip: "Well, I read about so many of 'em getting broken on the wheel." IT Looki:o THAT WAY.—Nodd : "I've had. plumbers in my house for a week." Todd: "What are they trying to do?" Nodd : "Judging from appearances, I should say they were trying to make my cellar into an aquarium." CONFESSED HIS FAULT. Surburban Boy: -"Mamma, th' train I always take to go to school ran off the track, and ever so many got hurt." Mamma: " Merciful ! How did you escape?" "I played truant." "My precious darling ! '</p>
        <p>The Curates' Union has secured the countenance and possibly the championship of Archdeacon Sin- clair, who read to the members assembled on Tues- day afternoon at the Shuttleworth Club an admir- able paper suggesting certain remedies for existing injustices. Several curates, as well as a vicar or two, subsequently spoke very much to the point. One reverend gentleman declared that certain euracies were "death traps" and certain vicars "nigger-drivers." One incnmbent was declared to have changed his curate regularly Avery eleven months, while another had had thirteen assistants in three years. It was suggested that a "black list of incumbents "should be made, although it was admitted that probably the roll would not exceed fifty. The clergymen present evidently meant business, and it was determined that as soon as the membership of the Union became sufficiently numerous a definite programme should be formu- lated.</p>
        <p>No ESCAPE FOR BlNKS.—Winks:"How is it Binks doesn't get along better?" Minks: "Poor Binks ! He's enslaved for life." " ? How ?" "When he was young he let a good many people do favours for him, and now it keeps him poor pay- ing 'em back." "Mn. ALLILLs was terribly frightened about his wife, and sent for a doctor in great haste." "What was the trouble ? " 'His wife complained of feel- ing perfectly well." Oh, Mamie, why did you marry again after that terrible experience with your first husband?" "I heard they were saying that I was old enough to know better." Too BAD !--Mrs. Beresford Vanderbillion sat alone in her 'parlour in upper Fifth Avenue. "Theodore," she remarked to one of her flunkeys who had been summoned into her majesty's presence—" Theodore, have you sent out the cards for the tea ? " "Yes, madam." "No disappoint- ments ? " "No. Mrs. Stevedore, Mrs. Vanaster, Mr. and Mrs. Vincerbilt, and all, with one ex- ception, have promised to attend." "Who is the exception, Theodore ? " "Mrs. Van Houser. We have just received word of her death." "Then she will probably not be present," muttered. Mrs. Vanderbilliou, languidly. "How irritating 1" RESEMBLANCE.—" DOR% you think the baby looks like me, dear ? " asked the proud father. " Y-e-e-s " replied the mother, cautiously, "when it's getting ready to cry." PLENTY OF MONEY.—Susie : "My sister has got a new piano. It's awful fine. Pop bought it. It's the best make, and it's a parlour. grand, too, and it cost a thousand dollars—just think of that I" Billy: "Let's go round an' hear 'er play on it." Susie "She ain't at home now. She's gone down to the music store to get another copy of 'Sliding Down the Cellar Door,' cause the old one's worn out." .</p>
        <p>One of the Liberal leaders in the Frome division, where a vacancy has been caused by the succession For Viscount Weymouth to thelpeerage, assures ma o,fit the prospects of a victory are excellent. Mr. : Barlow, who represented the constituency as a i Liberal from 1892 until the last election, has main- tained his association with the district, and has .ousiderably heightened his personal popularity. I While this is so, the local electoral machinery has been strengthened. The Tories of Frome, which, it • hould be remembered,is one of the brewing centres )f the South of England, have already shown that they desire to make local veto one of their chief caras in the fight. However, the constituency is -trongly Nonconformist in character,and the feeling which ha.s been aroused over the Education Bill will make that, so the Liberals believe and hope, one of • the dominant influences in the contest. Mr. Barlow . has fortunately all the Nonconformists with him. and his attitude towards the*Miners' Eight flours' Bill has quickened the sympathy of the miners and quarrymen, of whom there are a considerable num- ber in the constituency. The Longleaf, influence, which really secured Lord Weymouth's victory, ; will in this election necessarily be to some degree ,lormant.</p>
        <p>I If the strike in the building trade, which was postponed early last winter to the spring of this year, is, to take place at all, it will come on almost immediately. The time is favourable from the workmen's point of view—when labour is in the i full tide of its spring activity. ,No arrangements ; have yet beet' come to between the masters and men ; and both are, up to the present, unyieiding in their attitude to each other. New streets of houses are being run up on every unoccupied plot of land in I the suburbs, and in fact it may be said without ! exaggeration that new towns are being added con- tinually to all the London suburbs.. The demand for small houses of six or eight rooms is enormous, and a house agent in the north of London told me i the other dav that he could, let such dwellings by I hundreds and by thousands if they were only to be had. The strike, which , will be bitter awl pro- tracted if it ever begins, will arrest building. It will affect 30,000 workmen, who, if they are sue- cessful.will transfer from the miters to themselves upwards of £300,000 a year. It will be in vain for the master builders to say- they cannot afford it, for most of them have a wonderful habit of growing I rich, not a few having begun life as working men nnd speedily reached to considerable wealth or even to affluence.</p>
        <p>"DON'T tell me that you're at the office every night till twelve o'clock," sobbed the young wife, hysterically. "But my dear"— said the young husband, racking his so-called brain, and wishing the inventive lobe thereof were as fertile as Edi- son's. "Don't but me, sir 1" she interrupted. "But really "—" Don't really me ! " "But will you"—" No, I won't !" • Then won't you" —"No !" "My dear, wouldn't you " ' No, wouldn't ! "--" like to do a little shopping to- morrow with this sovereign ? " The sobs ceased, silence succeeded, and the star-eyed goddess of reform was herself again. TREMBLING with excitement, Louise stood in the parlour and waited for her lover. It seemed an age since he had gone to see her father in his .office. The young man was so impulsive, so easily betrayed into a hasty action, and papa was so grave and stern. At length the door opened and Louise's sweetheart stood before her. He was un- harmed, his cheeks glowed, a strange expression, gluanied in his eyes. "Have you spoken to papa?" she asked, in quivering tones. "Yes, my dearest." "And what did he say, Hans? Quick—tell me what he said. He said 'No' to thee. Oh, I see it written in thine eyes. But I will be thine—am thine! His cruel harshness has no terror for me. I—l. will Ily with thee." He gazed down at her face in a dreamy sort of way. "Now speak, do ! " :she impatiently exclaimed.• "I brook no delay. Was his manner repellen , insulting towards you? What did be do? What did he say p' Hans Muller took a deep breath, and then softly replied: "He only said, Thank goodness 1' and went on with. his writ ins!.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>TRIPLE 3117RDER AND SUICIDE.</head>
        <p>TRIPLE 3117RDER AND SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>; A Teignmeuth lodging-house keeper. named Henry Thine 4 killed his three children, aged respectively ten months, three years. 'and four- years, on Monday. A ' little Ipter he killed himself. It is reported that the man and his wife did not live happily together. There is rea- son to believe that Haines intended killing his wife also as he ran upstairs. where Mrs. Haines was dressing. The ' murderer was about 40 years of age, and had been twice married. A son by the first marriage he sent out ; the children of the second marriage he killed. The servant ' 'lir% named Bertha Newland. was in the kitchen at the 'me af the tragedy, but neither she nor Mrs. Haines •ioard anything.</p>
