      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CITAI.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CITAI.</p>
        <p>All rights teserved. THINGS A WOMAN WANTS</p>
        <p>TO KNOW.</p>
        <p>I THINK one of the most delightful parts of spring is the feeling that one can discard winter wraps, and instead take to those of light weight and colouring. I suppose we are fickle, for there is also a charm in taking to winter things when the days shorten and the cold winds begin to blow. However, it is with summer things that we have to interest ourselves now. A summer dressing-gown is, I think, a necessity, and there is no material prettier than a white brilliant or delicately-flowered cambric I am giving you a sketch of an exceedingly good model for one of these useful garments. It has a square yoke in the front, the fulness being gathered below, and the back is box- pleated down the centre. A wide ribbon with a bow on the left side confines the fulness at the waist. The large sleeves are in the bishop form, being gathered into a deep wristband, a full ruffle of lace or embroidery falling below. The yoke is also edged with a frill which is carried across the sleeves somewhat in epaulette fashion. There is a slight novelty about this, for the frill, instead of being gathered on to the edge of the yoke on the shoulders, is brought on to the !sleeves about four inches below the shoulder-seam.</p>
        <p>A Summer Dressing.gown. No. 2056. Paper Pattern, To be had from PATTERN DEPARTMENT, 18, HENRIETTA STREET, LONDON, W.C.</p>
        <p>Some ladies find these wrappers very con- venient for morning wear, and I am sure that one made after this model could not fail to give satisfaction. There is an immense range of cotton materials being shown this season; cotton crepes, grass muslins, cambrics, muslins, one and all, are equally popular, and as they are all more or less inexpensive, one can have a variety of them. Organdie and grass muslins are two of the most fashionable materials at the present time, and really there is nothing prettier. A grass muslin blouse with a wide collar of the same material, handsomely embroidered, is very chic.</p>
        <p>, Pique coats and skirts will be very much worn this year, and, provided a good pattern be obtained, they can easily be made by the home worker. The coats are fairly simple in construction in that they require no lining; the collar only is faced, and the seams finished with a binding. The skirts are also made unlined, but should have a deep help, so as to give an importance and weight to the edge of the skirt. The return of cotton and other light materials should be a great pleasure to the home worker, for they are much easier of manipulation than the heavier makes of cash- ii ere tnd cloth, in faA there are many ladies who make all their summer things themselves, and yet fight shy of fashioning their own winter garb. They need not really do so, but still I must confess that cotton blouses are far more easy to make than any other kind.</p>
        <p>A New Sleeve. No. 2057. Paper Pattern, 4icl. To be had.from PATTERN DEPARTMENT,IB, HENRIETTA STREET, LONDON, W.C.</p>
        <p>I know that there are many people who are more interested in renovation than in the making of new garments, and as the sleeves at the present timls have considerably changed from those of last year, perhaps a new pattern will be appreciated. I am, therefore, giving you here quite the latest model of an up-to-date sleeve. You will see that it is made with an under. sleeve, which is perfectly tight-fitting, while the top is composed of a graceful puff—a change indeed from any style we have lately worn. Notice, too, that the sleeve is very long; it fits tightly at the vtrist, and is then sloped out again in a point over the back of the hand. I know there are many who will regret the departure of the full sleeve, not only because it was picturesque, but also because it was so easy to make. The tight-fitting sleeves cer- tainly require a little more skill, but when once the difficulty is mastered I think you will find them as simple, even more go, perhaps, than our old and much-loved style. I dare say many ladies at the present time are looking at their white sailor hats of last year, and wondering how they can be made up-to-date again. Let me tell you they are by no means difficult to renovate, provided, of course, the straw be good and the brim perfect. Proceed in this way and you cannot fail to be pleased with the result. Take a teaspoonful of flour of sulphur, and stir it into the juice of a lemon, making a very liquid kind of paste. Dip a tooth-brush into this mixture, and with it rub the hat well, having, of course, pre- viously removed the band. In this way all traces of sunburn will be effaced, and all that now remains to be done is to place the hat under a gentle stream of water to remove the sulphur. When it is perfectly clean, put it into a current &amp; air to dry; when almost dry lay it on a table, cover the brim with white paper, and put heavy books on it so that it may be a good shape when required for wear. • Another piece of ribbon on the hat and a fresh flannel lining shbuld render it equal to new at the cost of a very few pence. Sometimes, after being cleaned two or three times in this way, the natural gloss Of a hat seems to disappear; this may be remedied by brushing it over after it is dry with the white 'of an egg.</p>
