      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>A MN WHO" COULD' NT BF AR TO HEAR THE CLUCK TICK.</p>
        <p>INTERVIEW BY A REPORTER.</p>
        <p>Such were the words which Mr. George Clarke of Aiwaltem, a village five miles from Peterborough, made use of to a "Peterborough Standard " reporter. "Some four years back," qr. Clarke said, by way of explana- tion, " I met with an accident in the gravel pits, which resulted in my right wrist being broken. A year after that I had a stroke of paralysis. I got up one morning, and should have fallen head over heels downstairs hadn't it been for the bed. Our doctor attended me for five months.' _</p>
        <p>Mr. Clarke had none of the looks of a paralysed man, and the reporter., astonished at his bale and sound appearance, asked— " Did the stroke leave no after4ffectB P" "It left me .without a nerve. I was like a child. I</p>
        <p>couldn't bear to hear that clock tick. If I went across the ro,m I used to tremble like a teal. But all that has passed away, tbauks—"</p>
        <p>"Thanks to what ?" "Thanks to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. I was just like this, whet: a friend, who used to drop in to cheer Tee. read in your newspaper about some of the wonderful cures Dr. Williams' Pink Pills had been doing. He read it out abut a man—a blacksmith, I believe— who had a stroke, just like vie, and recovered from it, and my miscue, ho had been listening, bought a box." "Did the first box do you good then?" "Yes ; I felt a benefit from the 'first. There is nothing to beat alum. I was able to resume work, and have not lost a day since. My nerves are equal to anything. I can eat well, and never felt better." This opinion was strongly upheld by Mrs. Clarke, who said she had recommended the Pills to several neigh- butirs, who were deriving benefit, from them. From this it is evid-nt that Mr. Clarke had a narrow escape of lifelong lv-Ipl,ssness, and that he was only saved from it by Dr.Wiliarnsi' Pink Pills for Pale People, which have already saved from so-called " incurable's diseases more than five thousand persons 1 Paralysis, which is heralded by numbness of the limbs or arms, often attacks strong and apparently heathy persons. It is not only in cases of parals Sill and subsequent nervous breakdown, however, that Dr. Williams' Pills are so invluable. They have cured locomotor ataxy, rheumatism, and ,sciatica .; also all diseases arising from impoverishment and vitiated humours of the blood, which cause scrofula, rickets, chronic erysipelas, con- sumption of the bowels and lungs, anemia, pale and sallow complexion, general muscular weakness, depres- sion, loss ef appetite, palpitation, shortness of breath, p tins in the back, nervous headache, early decay, all forms of female weakness,and hysteria. They invigorate the blood and system when broken down by overwork, worry, and indiscretion of living. These Pills are not a purgative. They are genuine only with the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and are sold nv chemists and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, / Holborn-viaduct, London, E.C., at Ls. 9d. a box, or six i for 13s. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>LITTLE SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>LITTLE SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held in the Wesleyan Schoolroom,Sankey Bridges,on Thursday evening week. There were present :—Messrs.R. Bond Matthews,Walter Brough,Samuel Williamson,Benjamin Grin3shaw,William Jackson, :Dior-11as Greenwell, Frederick Mas ey, and Mr. Smith (clerk). Mr. Williamson proposed the re-election of Mr. Matthews as chairman. He did not think they could choose abetter one.—Mr. Massey seconded the proposi- tion, and it was unanimou:ly agreed to.—On taking the chair,Mr. Matthews thanked the members for re-electing him tothat posiiisn. Ile had taken a great deal of in- terest in the affairs of Little Sankey and had tried to do his duty to the whole place as far as he could. In the future he should endeavour to do so. They had been a harmonious parish hi herto, and he hoped they would continue so to the end. He did not see any reasoa why they should not. He w.is very much obliged to them for re-appainting him aq chairman.—On the motion of the chairman,secondeti by Mr. Williainson,Mr.G,eenwell wab re-appointed vice-chairman.—Mr.Greenwell thanked the members for electing him vice-chairman for the second time. The 'Chairman proposed the re-appointment of the overseers, 'viz. Messrs Greenwell,Grimshaw,and Corra.- dine.—Mr. Jackson seconded, and it was unanimously agreed to. The!Chairman was re-elected waywarden without op- position. He stated that he attended nearly all the meetings of the'Highway Board. In the position of way- warden there was very little that he could do other th.,n seeing that the township was well watered. He man- aged to get a lot of paving done and a footpath made and put into decent order. If there were any others re- quiring .attention he would do what he could la the matter. :(Bear, Lear.) Mr. Smith exphined th overaeer's accounts for the half-year ending Slat !inter 29th. l'he accounts for the half-year ending in Marcn amounted to £674 14 91. The expenditure had been : c unty rate call, £259 ; ex- penses Rural District Council, .£25 19g. 101.; Highway call, £1244 school attendance,.-£6 113-3. D.; costs before justices, .65.; expenses of Parliament Iry registra ti0n,212 3s. €l.; salary assistant overseer,,£lo ; rate hooks and other expenses,Zl 155.8,1.; making a total of £440 a: 5 1. This left a balance against the overseers of £234 12. 4(1. On ne rtehze na a2n43tsa.rev cts.p, na etxhpee nes call sll apcacindu 00theieraevcieni t as balance in hand of £22 81. 81.—The ChairmL2 That is a 51. ratel—The Ci rk : Yes. The Chairman said it was very satisfactory that an arrangement had been made by .the Rural Council with. be Borough of IV arrington for the treatment of infect- ous diseases. It was a very serious matter when the agreement was broken, and he was very glad the Rural Council had,a,gain,come to terms with the borough in the matter. He believed the very day the arrangement was re-made .a case went into he hospital from Sankey Bridges. That showed that the action they took was</p>
        <p>necessary.. The Chairman said they would remember he was anxims that a good meeting should be held to return thanks to Lord Lilford for his handsome gift to the township. The Meyor and other gentlemen at Warring- ton seeing that the township was likely to be joined to the borough wished to give a strong expression of thanks to his lordship both on behalf of Warrington and Little Sankey.and the Mayor would attend any meeting which was called for that purpose. He (the Chairman) thought it would be advisable to postpone the meeting until it was definitely settled whether they were to he joined to the borough or not. In the meantime he proposed that the Council silo Id pass a resolution of thanks to his lordship for what he had done. Although they might go into the borough it was really a matter for Little Sankey as they would receive the whole benefit. An ex- pression of thanks from that Council would.therefore.be more fitting, and suitable than one from the whole district of Warrington. He proposed " That this Coun- cil for itself and inhabitants generally respectfully ten- ' ders to the Right Honourable Lord Lilford, its heart- ' felt thanks for his generosity in giving a playground for the neighbourhood, and ler his kindly appreciation of the requirementeof the locality." He felt sure they woald agree with him in stating that his Lordship had handsomely appreciated his high duty as a landowner wheel he recognised the wants of the people, and dealt with the matter in rhe liberal and handsome mariner he had dome—eMr.Grimshaw said that as one of the largest tenants of Lord Lilford, he was glad to second the reso- lution.—The Chairman said he thought they had a' agreement with the Corporation to lay the playgreend out if they at Little Setikey toiled the land. If they went into the Borough that would be done.—Mr. Greenvrell supported the resolution,and said the playgroue d would be of great benefit to the township. It would relieve the streets of the children who nevepla,yed in them to their great danger.—The reeolutioe was unanimously carried. Mr. Brough said their thanks were due to the chairman for the able way he had undertaken the duties ef inter- viewing Lord Lilford and obtaining that piece of land. He (Mr. [trough) thotieht a great deal was due to the Chairman for the WA.3, Lewd Lilford had responded to the wishes of the Council. and had it not been for the influ- ence of the Chairmen perhaps they would not have fared rio well. He therefore prepeeed that the best thanks of the meeting be given to the chairman for hi work in connection with the playground.—Mr. Williamson seconded, and the vote was unanimously carried. —The Chairman said he, was very much obliged for the vote of thanks. It had been his wish to do what he could for Little Sankey,and he hoped it would not bw the last thin! that the Council would be able to do for it. The meeting then terminated.</p>
        <p>THE EXANIINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1896.</p>
        <p>respect. We are now making a prefit, and hoping to do better in the future.—Colonel Ducat : In some places they have a difficulty with the Railway Company who will not carry it.—The Town Clerk: We have no trouble in that respect. The farmers fetch it from the works.— Councillor Monks explained the method of removing the material to Longford by means of the intercepting depots.—Colonel Ducat : Do you receive no complaints from the residents in the vicinity of the depots ?—The Mayor stated that the collection of the pails now took place in the night-time. There were complaints when the collection took place during the day.—On Colonel Ducat asking if anyone present objected to the applica- tion of the Corporation, Mr. William Diggle was seen to rise. He said he considered the proposed outlay on the Longford works unwise. He said many complaints of the nuisance which existed had been made. Very recently two deputetions from the Rural Sanitary Auth- ority had attended meetings of the Sanitary Committee of the Corporation, and complained of the nuisance at Loneford,stating that if it was not remedied they would endeavour to obtain an injunction againet them to stop it. The chimney at the Longford works had been raised but the residents still complained. THE EXTENSION OF THE GAS WORKS AND NIGHT SOIL DEPOT. With regard to the sixth application to enable the Corporation to borrow additional money for the pur- poses of the gas undertaking and the night soil depot, the Town Clerk quoted a number of statistics with re- ference to the gas undertakings stating that the Cor- poration asked for an additional sum of £20,000. This sum was needed for extensions amounting to the great inarease in the consumption of gas. They were com- pelled to make prov:sion for the making of 1,000,000 cubic feet of gle per day. In 1887, the consumption of Ras wao 97.461,000 cubic feet, in 1894 it had increased to 216,000,000, and for the 12 months ending 31st March 1896, it had risen to 255,347,00 —Colonel Ducat: You estimate that £20,000 will be required during the next ten years ?—Mr. Haddock : We shall require more than that, but the act stipulates that we may borrow not ex- ceeding £20,100. That stipulation must he removed be- fore we cite ask for £3o,ooo.—Mr.Burd: It would be better to apply for toe larger sum under the prov;emnal order. —The Town Clerk: Then we will ask for £3O 000. The work in hand will reqeire that amount.—Mr. Bard : Is this tn be spent at the Lengford works ?—Yr. Had- dock: Yes. We wish to close one worke. We are work- ing two at present.—Mr. Haddock and Mr. Hepherd then explained the plans of the proposed extension at Long- ford gas work.—The Town Clerk afterwards proceeded to explain he mcpirements of the night soil depot, and said the Local a ee•rnment Board had sanctioned the borrowing of £5,000 annually. In the application to the Board the Corporation had net the total amount re- quired to be borrowed at £15,000, but they wished the sum increasing to £l6 439.—C eonel Ducat : How long will the money carry you ?—The Town Clerk : From ten to fifteen years. Mr. Rue): Are the works not begun yet. The TOWN CLERK : S ‘me part has been done. Mr. Rue D: Ie there no chance If further expense? The Tows CLERK: No But lam advised to apply for £20,000 so that it will allow of a margin.—Colonel Ducat drew attention• to the fact that in the notice issued no mention was ma le of the Corporation's inten- tion to borrow a specific sum. It simply stated hat th y were applying for power to borrow money for cer- tain work, but did not mention any sum. He thought it would be necessary to give a fresh notice shewing the sum they intended to borrow, which would prebably form the subject of another inquiry.— rhe Town Clerk said it had been thought that the notice covered that.— Mr. Burl : The present position of affairs is that the notice you have given is not wide enoue,h to give every- body an opnortunitv of jedeing what you intended to apply for. People would say that it was not fair to take it into consideration now, but that it should be heard at another inquiry. It. think it would be well, therefore, that as soon as you get the sanction of these powers, you should give notice of the amouet you desire to borrow. The Town Clerk The Warrington Rural District Council, which is the authority outside the borough, quite approves of the proposed extensions. We have had no complaint of any nuisance from the intercepting deeots 'tr. four or five years. However, under the cir- eumstances they would withdraw the application for a specific sum as it was ruled that the notice given had not been suffilient—Colonel Ducat : All I can say is you are very lucky.</p>
      </div>
