      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD INQUIRY COM PANY. AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD INQUIRY COM PANY. AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE NEW AGREEMENT.</p>
        <p>The following are the chief points in the new agreement entered into between the Warring- ton Corporation and the Ship Canal Company.</p>
        <p>THE NEW WHARF. The agreement is dated 9tn of April, 1896, and the company undertakes to commence within six months of that date the construction of a new wharf of 300 eet long near the entrance of the Warrington dock from the Sllip Canal and to com- plete the construction within twelve months.</p>
        <p>MUNICIPAL IMPROVEMENTS. At the Town Hall, Warrington, on Tuesday morning, Colonel W. M. Ducat, R.E., and Mr. E. P. Burd, Local Government Board inspecZors, opened an inquiry into the application of the Corporation to amend certain local acts, chiefly dealing with gas and water financial arrangements; for power to borrow additional monies fr purposes of the gas undertaking and night soil depot; and to make provisions as to the powers of the officer in charge of the Corporation Fire Brigade when ' attending at firen in the borough, including provisions as to the penalty for obstructing or hindering any officer of the brig.de in the execution of his duty. There were present—the Mayor kCouncillor James Fairclough), Aldermen Francomb, Blackhurst, Wright and Sutton; Councillors Lee, Ellison, Evans, Duncan, Bennett, Plumpton, Rogers, Woods, Hepherd, Monks,Dr. Cannell, Shaw-Green, Furness, Roberts Baird and Tinnion ; Messrs. J. L. Whittle (town clerk), Stanley Wilson (assistant town clerk), John Fairhurst (borouth treasurer), W. El. Woodcock (assistant borough trea- surer and collector), Joel Smith (assistant collector), John Sutcliffe (assistant borough surveyor), Herbert W. Longdin (assistant borough surveyor), James Deas, (water engineer, Szil.), W. S. Haddock (gas engineer), Fred Taylor (secretary of the Gas Works), Dr. G,rnall (medical officer of health),Luke Talbot (chief constable), and Messrs. William Gill, William Diggle, C. Jackson Holmes, John Hartley. Curran and others. The Town Clerk opened the proceedings by, reading the notice with</p>
        <p>reference to the inquiry. PROPERTY OWNERS AND THE PAYMENT OF RATES. The first clause of the application was to increase the limit of rateable value of properties within which the owners of property, hastead of the occupiers, shall be rated by the Corporation to rates levied for municipal, sanitary, or other purposes.—Tri support of this the Town Clerk stated that the Local Act of 1854 prescribed the amoant at,£6 per annum, upon which composition might hc allowed. The Corporation had for some years been making a composition on all property of £8 and under, and a doubt had been created in his mind as to the legality of the matter. The Corporation, therefore, asked the Board for legal sanction. The arrangement had never been questioned by any one except h There was not the slightest objection to it in the town.— M:. Burd How many houses will be affected as be- tween the £6 and £8 P—Alderman Wright: Very few. —The Town Clerk : It is almost impossible to tell. The arrangement had been going on for years.—Mr. Hurd : Can you give me an idea how many houses are affected ?—The Borough Treasurer: Over a thousand. —Mr. Burd : How many houses are there in the borough P—The Town Clerk: 11,284.—1 n answer to ferther questions by Mr. Burl the Town Clerk said the improvement rate was 2s. 81. in the £ and the sewers rate 4d. one year and 21. another. The Borough rate was is. 2d. There eas no School Board rate, but there were expenses of the School Attendance Committee. The Library rate was IV. There was only one differential rate and that was tor tenants of Lord Lilford an t only on land.—No objection to the apptication was made. THE PAYMENT OF GAS ACCOUNTS. The second point of the application wes to enable the Corporation to render their gas accounts to consumers either half-yearly. quarterly, or at the end of periods of seven months and five months in each year. and to adapt the provisions of Section 41 of the Local Act of 1877 to meet the case of such accounts being renderei otherwise than half-yearly.—The Town Clerk stated that for the convenience of a large number of their customers, the gas accounts were rendered quarterly, at their re- quest.—Colonel Ducat: Yeu wish, as in the other case, to legalise what you have been in habit of doing ?—The Town Clerk : Yes. sir.—Mr. Hord : What discount is al- -1 ,wed on the gas accounts.—The TJNVE Clerk; Discount is allowed in every case where the acceunt is paid within one month from the delivery of the bill.—Mr. Burl: And it works well.—The Town Clerk: Very well—Mr. Burl . What is the rate of discount ?—The Town Clerk: Sixpenee per thousand cubic feet —Mr. Burl: Wnat does the total disenunt amount to P.—The Town Clerk : About £7,000.—N0 objections were raised. SUCCESS OF THE GAS AND WATER UNDERTAKINGS. The Inquiry proceeded to the third clause of the ap- plication, viz :—To make the net surpluses of the gas and water revenues respectively remaining after carry- ing out the various nhjects mentineei in Section 43 of the Local Act of 1877 and Section 78 of the Local Act of 1890 severally applicable in aid of the improvement rate fund instead of the borough fund.—The Town Clerk sail the Corporation acquired the gas undertaking in 1877. The price of gas was 3R. 61. per thousand cubic feet. It was now 2s. 101., and they had established a reserve fund of £7,717 19,. 21. They had reduced the cost of street lighting from 541. to 403. a larrp,which on 4,500 lamps amounted to £l,OOO a year. They had re- duced the meter rents to almo,t one half, and paid to the credit of th Borough tunes net of the surplus fund the sum of £2,000 in the year 1877. Last year eller made a rebate on the account of street lighting of £625 or about a penny in the £on the improvement rate. No charge had been made on the borough rate in aid of the gas undertaking. They had paid eft £52,374 of the capital debt, and they had in hand £2,510 ewieds a further re- duction. The nrieinal debt was £239 951 12s. It new ' stood at £187,578. They purchased on a basis of twenty- five years purchase. In 1890 they took power to redeem the annuities at market price and added four years to bring them to market value. Proceeding to speak of the water works undertaking, the Town Clerk said it wee purchased in 1890,and the revenue at that date was £8.025 ner year. Fee the rear ended 31st March lad the revenue wag £10,626. They obtained 60 years for the repayment of the money, hut the sinking fond was str. nended for five years. That period expired in October last. The sum that would he required to pay the prin. cipel,interest and annuities was £10,359 in equal annual payments of principal and inierest at 21 per cent. The income was now more than suffieient to meet all de- mands upon it. They had now an accumulation of pro- fits of £5,600 which was tised as working capital.—Mr. Berd : You really want some authority to use the pre- fits as working capital.—The Town Clerk: Strictly speaking we ought to close the Recounts and hand the balance over.—Mr. Burl: Ycu may us well have It authorisied.—The Town Clerk: Then we f, enmity apple for authority to deal with the capital in that war, and we also make the Same application with re- gard to the gas works. There had been no charge on the rates in respect of the water undertaking sinee it had been Required. Mr. Berl : What is the reason for the charge P—The Town Clerk: We have pressing upon us many matters of im- portance which will he a direct charge upon the im- Provement rateemeti as the widening of our streets, the Provision of smallpox hospital, the extension of the Longford Depot, and the purchase of land for sewage werke. These undertakings are required more in the capacity of a sanitary authority than in that of a murP- cleat corooration.—Mr. Burl : The Act will require cr n- -1 siderable alteration. The word " borough " rate occurs in mane places.—The Town Clerk : There is no practice! objection to it. PROVISIONS AS TO NEW BI7ILDINOS. Proceeding with the application IC to confer (in gee:- stitution for existine provisions of the Loeal Act of 18791 powers nn the Corporation to fly a line for new buildings to be built in streets and to define the term "new hiiild- ings" in this connection. the Town Clerk stated that the Borough Surveyor was engaged in L,ndnn, under the Snefiker'm Order in ctinneetion with a Bill before a com- mittee of the House. P was proposod to strike out clause Bin the Act of 1879 which regulated the line of buildinga in their streets becenee they frnind it innnera- tive. The section had been quite easy to evade and ad- vantage had been tal-en of that fact. They asked the Board to give them the came powers as the Rrighnuse Improvement Act—Mr. Burl pointed out that they (raid not alter the Public Health Act by a provi- sional order. His own impression was that it was hirdle worth while, that was, so far SS it affected Meal Aerie There was another matter in connection with the Brie- house Act. They would find it gave the power of anneal from any decision of the local authority to the next Quarter Seesionse—The Town Clerk seid they would not, object to that. There were other clauses in the Brighouse Act which the Corporation would like to get, if possible.—Mr. Burl Which are those P—The Town Clerk : From eeetion 52 to 79.—Mr. Burl said many of them were worthless. —The Town Clerk said he would be satisfied to leave it with the Inspectors to grant what they thought would be of value to the Corporatine.—Mr. Burl Is mulch building going on in the town P—The Town Clerk : Yes, very ex- tensively. At the last meeting of the Town Council we passed plans for 96 new cottages and houses besides works.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED EXTENSTONS AT LONGFORD DRPoT.</p>
        <p>The C,lrpnration applied f,r permiasion to repeal sec- tion 19 of the Local! Act of 1879,authorizing the acquire- ment be agreement of not exceeding two acres of land for nightßoil depot purpnses.—The Town Clerk amid he wished them to repeal and remove are dont t as to the power of the Corplratien finding more than two Item; of land for the depot. The Cnrporation had already four acres of land in tre.—c.,,ienel Ducat: Can more be pur- chased at the Paine place P—The Town Clerk: I think so, although we have not yet approached the owners. Colonel Ducat: Are you going to carry the depot on when you cet your sewage arrange- ments completed ?—The Town Clerk: We :ntend to continue the work at Lnneford. We look moon It as a snuree of revenu-..—Cnlnnel Ducat : Your profit is a very small one. The depreeiation of Plant, materiPl'l and horq.a mu.t exceed £2OO. Why don't you put the material into the sewers P—The Town Clerk We have only about 500 water cl.aßets in the town.—Mr. Burd : We shall have an application from von shortly to make Warrington into a water clneet town when you find the wondreul effect it has in Wigan.— The Town Clerk: I hope not. We are very well Ratio. fied with our arrangement. at the present time in this</p>
        <p>Cocoa ta more thso mere timulatig end re'roehing drink, it is also a nutriti us feo 1. and one of the most prec- ious gifts of nature—sn.taining and invigoret ng the system prftblv mo a than any other beverage. The Lancet refers to Cadbury's Cocoa as "the standard of highest purity at present attainable in regard to Cocoa." • No alkalies used.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>THS DOCKS.</head>
        <p>THS DOCKS.</p>
        <p>If within one month from the date of the agreement the Company has net commenced the construction of the Warrington dock the powers of the company to construct the dock shall be suspended, but the Corpora- tion shall not call upon or compel the company to con- struct the dock. The Corporation upon the expiration of the above period may at any time introduce into Parliament a Bill for conferring on the Corporation power to construct the dock. The Corporation in con- nection with the construction of the dock, shall seek power for the widening at their own cost, of River-road, and for the construction of a new bridge over the river diverrion. The wharf to be constructed by the company is to be considered part of the dock. The entire manage- ment and control of the dock is to be vested in a com- mittee consisting of five members of the Town Council and three appointed by the Canal Company's directors. It the Corporation fail or decline to construct the dock within a pericd of ten years from the date of this agree- ment, the a.,treomPnt shall be null or void. TEE NAVIGATcON OF THE RIVER. The Company shall forthwith, at their own cost, dredge the bed and banks of the river Mersey (meaning thereby a channel having a minimum width of fifty feet at the bottom), and for ever after maintain the same dredged so that at all times there shall be a depth of eight feet of water at low water of Spring tides in the said channel, between the western boundary of the works of Monks Hall and Company, and Howley leek. The company also agree to maintain a depth of eight feet of water at low water of Spring tides in front of all wharves existing at the date of the agreement, between the western bound&amp; y of the works of Monks, Hall and Company and Howley lock and also in front of any future wharves which may, within a period of five years, be constructed on the north side of the river bp- twcen the two points already mentioned, or on the sonth side between the present western boundary of the borough and Howley lock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>TOLLS</head>
        <p>THE TOLLS</p>
        <p>It ig provided that traders and manufacturers at or rear Warrington shall have the Tight to use the canal between Warrinr,ton dock and Eastham for vessels of not more than'2so tons, upon payment of a toll for each j lumpy up or dawn not exceeding 31 per ton of cargo; and if toll to the amount of not less than £1 the vessel shall be entitled to return without any cargo free of toll.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WYOLIFFE CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening at the second of the free and oven monthly services at the above place of worship, there was evidence in the largely augmented congrega- tion that the idea is becoming a popular one. The pastor (the Rev. John Yonge) again conducted the ser- vice and took as the subject of hie discourse "Sunday," selecting as a text " The Sabbath was made for man," Mark 2, part of verse 27. At the commencement the preacher said those words were a distinct affirmation of the purpose p-if what we now commonly call "Sunday," from the lips of the Great Teacher himself. The text declared that the day was made,designed, separated and set apart "for man. In his brief space of time that night he did not wish or intend to enter upon any of the vexed, debateable questions relating to the observance of Sunday. It must be, however, to any thoughtful and religious mind a subject of the deepest concern that Sab- bath observances in a religious sense, was apparently suffering serious decline. A recent census of attendance at public worship in Bolton,even alio wing for some very considerable inaccuracy,served to show that the absence of the people from the worship of God was becoming terribly general. Several years ago a similar census was taken in Warrington,revealing then a very large absence from public worship. If such a census were taken now the rev, gentleman feared that the revelation would be much more serious still. Even among religious people it was to be feared there was a relaxed regard of the value and the duty of public worship. The subject of" Sunday "was one that needed to be thought about. Be apprehended that none of us desired that shops and works should be open and going seven days a week. We found that the week of six days was quite long enough for work and toil, and it would be a terrible blow to us to learn at any time that a Parliamentary majority of whatever political colour had created a law, opening Works,shops, ani factories, with the same ease that the Parliamentary majority had recently authorised the opening of public museums and picture galleries on Sundays. Ele fancied if any attempt were made to take our weekly day of rest from us it would precipitate a revolution. Mr. Yonge also condemned the use of Sun- day solely as a day of leisure and suggested that in a non-religions use of Sunday, people were really doing serious injnry and great injustice to their truest welfare. Sometimes men said they could worship God in the fields, on the sea shore, and hill side, but because they could do so was no justification for their ab enting themselves from worshiping God al-Arg with their fellows within the walls of the sanctuary. There were vast numbers of people ton who were worse for their Sun- days. If a man could not go to work on Monday be- cause he had spent Sunday in dissipation, he at any rate was the worse for his Sunday. Neither could it be said that those people were better for their Sundays who went off in loaded waggonettes to spend Sunday in the country returning in the evening in conditions the reverse of religious or Sabbatic. Often the companies were composed of husbands who regularly left their wives at home to struggle with the children while they went off to "enjoy" themselves. Those excursionists too, gave no thought to the fact that to receive their pleasure they made men and horses work seven days a week and far more than 'eight hours day. Moreover they would have the puhlichouses kept open for their reception and so Precluded the publicans, their families, and servants, from having any leisure or ever finding their way to the House of Worship. Mr. Yonge also alluded to the bad example set by parents who were-, 'particular about their children observing Sunday religiously but who themselves abstained from attending public worship. Those parents who made Sunday a day of pleasure and social intercourse need not feel surprise, if the young people broke loose at the earliest opportunity. The rev, gentleman concluded by pleading with all for a better use of Sunday, a more earnest and spiritual use% for the old couplet that A Sabbath well spent Brings a week of content."</p>
        <p>migl-t be more thAn V&gt;ccasionally tr-u—e-in its literal sense. They should all keep the Divine Day for the purpose which God had given it. During the evening Mr. A. Griffiths pave an excellent rendition of Sullivan's solo "The Chorister."</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>During the week, which was the 15th, of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered : Births.</p>
        <p>Deaths. 14</p>
        <p>Warrington 34 Latchford 3</p>
        <p>Total 37</p>
        <p>The excess of births over deaths was 22. The death rote from all causes was equal to 134 per I,ooo,a,gainst 19 4 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 18 the erirresponding week of last year. The death rare from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 2"r per :,000, against 21 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 09 the correspnnding week of 1895. The ages of mortality were— () 1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 6 4 3 2 15</p>
        <p>The deaths included-1 from scarlet fever. 1 from diphtheria. 1 from diarrhoaa, 1 from old age, 4 from brain disease, 3 from lung disease. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Szarlet fever, 2 cases, dhotheria 1 ease, enteric 1 case. Total number of patients in hospital on Saturday, April 11h-12. METEOROLOGY. —The mean temperature was 49.3 Fah., and therefore 8.6 degrees higher than the precedinc, week. Tim maxiMtain temperature 58F., was recorded on the 10'h inst., and the minim= temperature 41F., on thP 11th inst. The Rainfall measured •30 inch. The general direc- tion of the wind was N.W. The reading of the Anemo- meter denoting 1,905 miles. JOHN H. GORNALL, '4.edieal Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
