      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>VIRE BRIGADE.</head>
        <p>THE VIRE BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>No. 7 clause was an application,to make provision as ta the powers of the officer in charge of the Corpora- tion Fire BrigadP when attending at fires in the borough including provisions as to a penalty for obstructing or hindering any officer of the brigade in the Qxecution of his duty.—Tie Town Clerk said the oiThet was very ob. Vintl9, and it was desirabTe that they should have the powers. The clauses were simply those granted in previ- ous Acts of Parliament.—Mr.Burd : Ts there a fire bri- gade P—The Town Clerk: O'i, yes,there is a very efficient fire brigade un3er the control of the C-mpnration.—Mr. Bird : Vk ith a responsibl. Supprinten3Pnt?—The Town Clerk: Yes.—Mr. Burd : A paid officer?— The Town Clerk : Yes. It is in the nature ot a volunteer tire brigade, and the Corporation pay the men so much as an annual salary and also for practices and fires.—Mr. Bard: How many stations are there?—The Town Clerk: Only one.—Mr. Burl: What is the population ?—The Town Clerk : 57,000, hut in about a month, if we get the extension, it will be about 60,000.—Mr. Bard : Have you ever found in the cases of fire any diffilulty with the public P—The Town Clerk : It is as well to have the power to deal with people who interrupt.—Mr. !Turd : IDoes the superintendent of police or the superintendent of the brigade assume command P—The T ,wn Clerk: The superintendent of the Fire Brigade.—Mr. Bard: I suppose the superintendent of police and fire brigade are on good terms ?—The Town Clerk intimated that he thought they were.—With regard to the statement of Mr. Diggle, it was there mentioned by the Town Clerk that there WAS no nuisance from the destructor at L3ng- ford depot. The inquiry then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WHAT A WEAK BOY DID.</head>
        <p>WHAT A WEAK BOY DID.</p>
        <p>Consumption is too often traced to weakness in child- hood. When from any cause the child is unable to isiimilate nourishment necessary to make a healthy growth, there is almost at once impoverishment of the blood. This condition is dangerous,not only to growth, but to life itself. No child can hope to have solid bones, fat rosy cheeks, and vital strength, if its food supply is cut off frcm the blood. The son of W.D. Ray,Head Master of Plaxtol National Schools, Sevenoaks, Kent, at four years old showed a w.-akness of the chest. What his condition was, and what his physician recommended is told by Mr. Ray, the Head Master,in the following letter,dated January 15th, 1896 : _</p>
        <p>_ " I take pleasure in acquainting you of the great hew*: Scott's Emulsion has given to my little boy. He is four years old, and althou,lh a good big boy, has at times shown a weakness of chest, which we have been afraid would develop into serious lung trouble. For the last two winters he has had severe attacks of congestion of the lungs, and has been, up to the last two months, peculiarly susceptible to catching cold. In October, 1895. he had another grave illness—conges- tion and bronchitis—and he was reduced in strength and appearance to a very low ebb. Our doctor ordered him to take Scott's Emulsion. He has done so for the last two months, and the benefit to him is remarkable. His appetite, which before was capricious, is now good and steady, and he has increased in weight to a consider- able extent.. By the aid of Scott's Emulsion, solely sad entirely, he appears to be rapidly getting into a s mnd, healthy tate. I have every faith that by its continued use he will soon outgrow that weakness of the lungs which had shown:itself in him. I have begun to take it myself as a tonic, and have goDd reason to hope that it</p>
        <p>will prove of equal service to me. " You are at liberty to publish this, and I shall be pleased to answer any questions respecting the case, either from you or from anyone to whom you might quote me as a reference." Scott's Emulsion certeinly has a wide range of useful-</p>
        <p>ness. Food is designed to supply the body with every. thing needed by Nature ; but, Snott's Emulsion has the advantage over food of acting directly upon the throat and lungs, healing sore places and relieving all local irritation, and at the same time feeding the blood with the most ersential nourishing properties of food. It is easily assimilated, and this enables the patient to gain fl-sh and strength, even though weakness may have im- paired the digestive organs. For all who are pale and thin, and weak. Scott's Emulsion is almost a necessity. Its value is attested by the high esteem in which it is held by the medical pro- fession, which recommends it in nearly all cases where there is decreased vitality. It is peculiar to itself, and cannot he imitated. The genuine Scott's Emulsion is easily distinguished by the trademark of a man carry- ing a big fish on his back, on the wrapper. 10.</p>
        <p>The members of the principal trade unions among the men employed in the London building trades threa- ten t come out on strike on tbe Ist May for an advance of a halt-penny per hour in wages and a new code of working rules. It is computed that a general .triko in the building trades of L ,ou will inv iVf• 50,000 me 9.</p>
        <p>p UPTURKS.—REY N OLD,' Pdtent lw percept,' ble Infilarubber Stin Pa..-1 Tilll-48. Suitable in all e.aaes. perfect Fit Ensured. Write or call for particulars.— RHYNOLDS, 10, Slat., -treat (a fLvi ,re Boln-street), Liverpool. Open on Saturday until? on.— trivti</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>"?'</p>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>The Paris corre‘pondent of the " Telegraph " states that a little girl in a school at Tilly-sur-Seulles, in the Caen arrondissement, suddenly exclaimed to her teacher, "Look out of the window, madame; there is the good Virgin." The nun and her fifty pupils turned their eyes in that direction, and declared that they beheld what ap- peared to be a statue of the Virgia,with the infant Jesnb in her arms, at the foot of a tree, surrounded by a bril- liant halo. Last Sunday upwards of 1,200 crednlous folks travelled to Tilly-sur-Seulles for the purpose of visiting the spot, and every evening fully 500 persons gathered round the tree and picked off the bark to make relics with it. The natural explanation of these pheno- mena is that they are to be attributed to the phosphore- scent effects produced by limekilns, which are very numerous in that part of the country.</p>
        <p>At the beginning of the year and perhaps not without some impetus from the rumours of wars which then filled the air,the Institution of Electrical Engineers ap- pointed a committee to consider how best the experts in their ranks mi•_;ht assist in the scheme of national de- fence. A circular was issued to all members in Great Britain, and in response a large number of replies have been received expressing a desire to j tin any electrical engineer corps which may be formed. The matter is now under the consideration of the War Otfice,and, con- sidering the important part which electricity now plays in warfare, its decision is not likely to be adverse to the patriotic impu:se of the Institution.</p>
        <p>An ingenious person, by way of winding up the Asso- ciation football season, has compiled comparative statis- tics of the attendances at League matches during the last eight years. Within this period the average" gate" at first League matches has risen from '4,400 to 6,800 ; in the Second League from 3.000 to 4,800; and in the. English Cup from 6,000 to 12,800. The year's Leag,iv matches have drawn a total attends, ce of 2,985,000,anl the grand total for the 3.036 matches which have been played within the last eight seasons amounts to 17,750,000.</p>
        <p>The "Times" Berlin correspondent telegraphs :—With regard to the question of the German Emperor's visit to Cowes this year, the Berlin " Neueste Nachrichten " publishes a statement which, it alleges, comes from Vienna, and for which it is pleased to claim high auth- ority. The otlect of the document is to show that England has been making strenuous offorts to secure the Emperor's presence at the Regatta. These exertions appear to have been completely successful, for it asserts that the Queen has already received a letter from His Majesty from Venice announcing his intention of visiting Cowes, and that this result is in no small measure due to the Emperor Francis Joseph. with whom" the Queen seriously di4cussel the Anglo-German relations Lt Nice." Such a cons Ammation would, of course, run directiv counter to the wishes of those circles which the Bis- marckian organ represents, and it declares that the statements are incredible " in view of the shameless attempts of the English press to make the German Emperor pass through the Caudine Forks. It is likely enough that exertions are being made in England in this direction, but people over there know perfectly well that the success of their efforts would be irreconcilable with the national dignity of Germany, and that it is therefore impossible. Germany cannot pursue a family policy, but only a policy compatible with her prestige and her interests, which is out of the question if she follows in England's wake."</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the decreased consumption of coffee in this country, to which the Chancellor of the Ex- chequer referred in his Budget speech, the production of the fragrant berry in British Cenral Africa is in- creasing by leaps and bounds. The single plant which was taken out to Nyassaland a few years ago by a Scott- ish gardener is now the parent of many millions of trees, and it is estimated that this year no less than three hundred and twenty tons ,A coffee will be available for export.</p>
        <p>Mr. Eugene Carter, the well-known American billiard player, has paid a second visit to Manchester this week, and has given his exhibition of American (or French) billiards and fancy strokes. The game is played on a pocketless table, each cannon counting one, with balls considerably larger than those used in English billiards. Mr. Carter claims to be "cushion cannon champion "of America; but that game, which provides that the striker's ball most hit the cushion before cannoning, is almost, if not entirely, unknown in this country. Mr. Carter's mastery of the American game is, however. sufficiently interesting to anyone with the slightest knowledge of billiards. His adroit employment of the masse stroke, so seldom seen in English billiards, has a special interest at the present time, when the profes- sional player finds himself face to face with the problems of the new push-barred game. Mr. Carter playes this stroke successfully with English balls and a cue of aver- age weight, so that, if the crusade against the push. stroke should continue.it is probable that the masse will be much more frequently played by the experts.</p>
        <p>The Paris corresondent of the " Daily News" sends to that journal" another monstrous case of Algerian military discmline." The victim was a soldier named Cheymol, brother of M. Paul Cheymol. He was sent, for some breach of rules, to a disciplinary company. This !mans a scat of penal servitude of the many fearful kinds that have survived the Revolution. Tbe name of the sergeant set over Cheymol was Perrin. To hum- ble him he ordered him to be tied by the wrists to a horse's tail, which was to be kept going at a brisk pace until the sergeant cried "Enough." After a long spell of this exercise Cheymol fell. The horse nevertheless was given rein and whip, until it was evident that it was dragging not a living man, but a corpse. A com- plaint has been sent by the brother to the War Minister, but as there was no breach of rule,he will probably wash his hands of the affair.</p>
        <p>An inventiv" ...us has given to the world an im- prnv°' ii. It is arranged on a long rocking seat, co tho. the usually irksome duty of churning can be com- bined with a pleasant rock and perusal of the latest novel. It is said that by the time the " plot begins to thicken" the cream follows suit, and before the point is reached where "they marry and live happy oder after" the butter is ready to remove to the cooler.</p>
        <p>This is an age of exneriment, useful and otherwise. The greatest experimentalist is probably Lord Kelvin, but his latest experiments have not their usual humane interest. He has been endeavouring to discover what the effect of a cannonade of guiclufiring guns would be on board the vessel firing and the ship subject to the fire. He finds that after fifteen minutes' firing the survivors of the crews of both vessels would be reduced to a erste of mental, if not physics!, in- capacity, owing to the concussion of the projectiles on the sides of the vessel and the noise of the guns.</p>
        <p>A great auk's egg was sold by auction in London on Monday and fetched 160 guineas. Six or seven years ago there were only 68 n.eimens of the egg recorded, The highest price of £3OO was paid for a duplicate for the collection of Baron d'Hamonville.of Meurthe,France. two years ago. Shortly after this event two very good specimens were detected among a collection of eggs pur- chased at a sale in the country for 305., and were subse- quently sold in London tor 275 guineas and 185 guineas respectively. A third specimen came into the auction. room during last s,ason, and fetched 180 guineas. A few years ago a number of exceedingly clever forgeries lof the egg were manufactured.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gladstone has often pointed with pride to the fact that brane.hes of hi,4 family have flourished in each of the four parts ef the United Kingdom. An article by Miss Florence Gladstone in the "Scottish Review," which gives an account of the pugnacious Gledatanes of Cocklaw Cas`le, shows that Mr. Gladstone might have gene :1 little further afield and included France and Sweden. A William de Gledstanes who accompanied the Earl of Douglas to fight against the English in France behaved so well that he was created a knight thinnerrt on the field of Potiers in 1356 It Was this same member of !he family who in the next year formed part of the sad procession of French captives on the occasion of the triumphal entry of the Black Prince into London, and who snent several months in the Tower. It was a Herbert Grl 4datnnn who Was most prominent among the Scottish offieers of Gnats vus Adolphus. In 1647 —only seren years after he had first entered the service—He:- belt Gladstone! WA raised to the Swedish peerage, and founded the noble house of the Gladstens, whose sons al: followed the profe,sion of arms right down to the ex- tinction of the hraneh in 1747. In whatever part of Northern Europe the family has flourished there has al- ways been the one distinguishing trait of combativeness.</p>
        <p>A revolting case of desecration of a graveyard i re- ported from the Ca.tleishind district of Kerry. An old wehlfhw man, named O'Brien, a reputed miser, died, and was hurled in Cordal graveyard a week ago. The house where he died, ard several places which he used to fre- quent were searched for hidden treasure, but none was f-und. Yesterday the caretaker of tho graveyard found the cornse disinterred, and the garments' which had covered it dioarranged and torn, evidently by robbers in pursuit of the supposvd wealth of the miser.</p>
        <p>The "Daily NPIt'S " New York correspondent tele- graphs :--" A long correspondence between General and Mr. Willineton Booth IQ published here shr wing the origin of the quarrel. It makes an unfavourable im- pression. Mr. Ballington Booth's assertion that tile General said to him while on his visit heretl'm not going to let you suck all tke juice out of this plum' confirms th tvery'general new that the trouble has really grown out .4 the :14rge sums of money raised by the Army in America, and is far rnre mercenary than spiritual. Ind,Pd, the Americans view the quarrel with weariness, wrongly tinctured with a .uspicion that there is a good deal of humbug on both sides. There is little doubt that the nlt'rnate result will be the gradual lisappearance of the Salvation Army in th, countrY, both in the original and Mr. B4llineon Booth'. V,Aunteer form. Toe whole movement has become an object of ridicule, and notl-- ing which excites the American sense of the ridiculous can survive long."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>HOW MR. i %NELL WAS ARRESTED.</head>
        <p>HOW MR. i %NELL WAS ARRESTED.</p>
        <p>The " Windsor Maeazine " publishes, in its April issue, a most striking article by Tighe Hopkins on Kit- mainha is Prtsen, that gaol so famous in political annals. 'rife account of the arrest of Mr. Parnell is of more than ordinary interest. " He was Braying at Morrison's Hotel in Dasseori street, and it wee nere that he was asked for, at seven on the morning named, by a trusted officer of the Dahlin detective f Tee. The waiter who was first in- terviewed declared that Mr. Parnell had ' gone out for a bath.' It seem, d impathatile, and the effieer, disclosing his identity, gave the number of Mr. Parnell's room (No. 20. for rhe next curious visitor at Morrison's) and re- quested to be shown up there. Ile was begged to wait • just four or fie mieutes." Not a minute it you please,' was the officer's reply. It is very unlikely that Mr. Parnell, had he been warned, would have teken ad- vantage of the warning, but in tour or five minutes' a sempathetic waiter might easily have drawn toeether a sympathetic cr3wd rn rfee. street ; and the oftieer was single-handed. " Hewever he was shensevat once to No. 20. Mr. Parnell, who was still betesiten the sheets, presented himself at his door in a moment in nether garment and slippers The situation being explained to him he in_ speeted the warrantennd said he must have time to write two or three letters. For fit een minutes the offieer paced the corridor, ad then, as the crowd which he had feared was beginning to gather in she street,he ri quested Mr. Parnell to 'mire a hurried toilet. " Hurried or not, woe, he c ime out of his room five minutes later he was as scrupulously dressed as always. The officer led him out beldly by the front door ; there was no disturbance (to the chagrin, doubtless the the sympathetic waiter), arid they entered the cab wh was in waiting. Mr. Parnell relieved threueinu: with admirable dignity and composure, otily for one m enent showieg sins of annoyance. Ha had written tete° letters which he asked to be allowed to east whh his tiwn hind a request which was repeated severel tim- s. Pies etly sir,' said the officer, biding his tirne. For the -•ffi-.er it was a journey of some neryousnes4. He was carryeng to orison, under the fiat of a Gevernmen dete;te I op the strongest party in Irelat.d, the most p ,werful and most popular man in Ireland, and he wa-4 unsupported by any kind of escort A. word fr .m Parnell is he wee being taken thareugh the streets, and it would have been, a hard matter to arrive with him at Kilinainham. There were a number of persons ga• hired about the Kings- bridge Station, and had he merely shown his face and said, am under arrest,' the cab woad have been wrecked. He said nothing, aid sitting well I shk hi the vehicle seemed anxiime that no one should reeeg- niee him. Just beyonu this p iint a company of the Guards turned out of the Royal Hospital and marched behind the cab. It was here that the prisoner, for the first time, vented a word cc two of tetneer. • Yen said that I should pest my letters,' he said to the officer beside him ; ' yeu are decrying me." You Filial pest them in s moment, Mr. Parnell,' was the seaswer. Kit- maiahern was reached almsst iinmecliately, and in the pillar-box against the prison Mr. Parnell posted his letters. Some dozen r twenty hawkers, labourers, and car drivers recognised him here, and seeing that he was under arrest pressed fmward t touch and speak to him. He drew hack, and would give his hand to no one as he passed into the courtyard of the prison. With no hests heueur he entered the prison itself, and st flirting erect in the inter hall scarcely conitescended to recogniseth sae of hie acquaintances amongst the suspects who advanced respeetfielt to greet him. Iniieed, from the first day to the liter, the' Chief ' was as unapereachable in 'film tin- hero, by the rank and file of his party imprisoned with as he had alvvays been in the Lobby or dining- reem of the Heine of Cemmons. Within a few days of his arrival in fact there came to be an • Upper' and a • Lower House' in the pris in. The Upper Howie was the pertion in which Mr. Parnell and his few associates met and tick their exercise, and rarely indeed did ens from the Lower House venture unbidden within the privileged confine."</p>
        <p>T'. "toyal Laundresses say Critchley's " STARCH GLOSS nneo..l.lled for. imparting a beautiful finish to starched. inn &amp;c., either cold or boiling vftec starch. Mr.—. C rn- wall. says : My wif3 will not finish our linen without your " Starch Gloss' " From Mrs —, Bradford :—Dear sir,- 7. an, oloasecl to inform you th It I have tried many so-called StArels atsZe3 ,nd Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It sh-mid he used in every house. Sold at 14., 3d., and 61., by most stirch s,llcrs. If not in stock will procure it. T. CutTattLEY. Blackburn Proprietor.—Advt.</p>
