      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURPAY, APRIL 2,5 189</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURPAY, APRIL 2,5 189</p>
        <p>THE country is now rapidly Landlords Relief. realising what Tory Govern- ment means. The present Government has been placed in power with such an overwhelming majority that it has a freer hand in dealing with national affairs than any other Tory Government has had for more than half a century. The result is to be seen in the kind of legislation that is being submitted to Parliament. The Liberals have often been accused of a partial- ity for "class legislation," but in their case the class for whose benefit the legislation has been designed is the working class, the most numerous and the most necessitous class in the country. The Tories are evidently deter- mined not to lay themselves open to any charge of helping the helpless or of succouring the needy. At the same time their policy so tar as it has yet been disclosed is one of class legisla- tion of the worst kind. The section of the com- munity upon which they propose to shower bene- fits is one composed of rich landlords—a section which has been in the past and is now very well able to take care of itself and which is certainly not in need or deserving of any special favour. The landlords are being endowed with a lavisk hand. Practically the whole of the handsome surplus provided for by Sir WILLIAM HARCOURT left after the augmentation of the Navy Es- timates is to be handed over to the landlords. Last week the CHANCELLOR Of the EXCHEQUER gave the landowners a round £lOO,OOO by reducing the land tax; this week it is proposed that another million should be absorbed in reduc- ing the rates upon agricultural land. In future years this million will be increased to two millions. The Agriculturafl Rating Bill which was brought into the House of Commons on Monday by Mr. CsArLIN provides that the rates paid on agricul- tural land shall be reduced by one-h all. This seems to be an easy and a summary method of reducing the rates, but as a matter of fact it does not mean that the local authorities who have received these rates are to lessen their expenditure by one-half. All that it me ans is that the mil lion or two [millions hitherto derived from land will be replaced by the granting of an equaleum from the Imperial Exchequer. In other words, it is simply the transference of a burden from the shoulders of one portion of the com- munity to those of another,!for, as has been often pointed out, by far the largest portion of the im- perial taxes is derived from residents in towns. The plain meaning of.,the:Government in propos- ing the Agricultural Rating Bill is, therefore, to tax town residents for the benefit of landlords. The ostensible object of the Bill is, of course, to "relieve agriculture," but its real effect will be to check the fall in rents. The landlords do not care to relieve agriculture at the expense of their rents and they have succeeded in persuading this Govern- ment to give the required relief at the expense of the taxpayers. That the Government are thinking of the landlords rather than of the tenant farmers was very clearly shown on Wednesday when the Revision of Rent Bill was before the House of Commons. The passing of this Bill would give more relief to agriculture than the passing of twenty such measures as that of Mr. CHAPLIN, but then the relief would be given by restraining the power of landlords to increase rents upon tenants improvements and by giving some security of tenure, and this was enough to rouse the strongest Tory opposition. The Bill was therefore destroyed by the votes of the reaction- ary majoritv. It was, however, during the discus- sion on this Bill shown very clearly how little is the relief that can be given to agriculture by tinkering with the rates compared with what the landlords might themselves give by remissions of rents. Mr. Loo &amp;N calculated that whilst Eng- lish farmers only paid five millions a year in rates they paid no less than forty-six millions in rent and another four millions in tithes. Yet in the opinion of a Tory Government the fifty millions of rent and tithes must not be touched, not even to relieve the greatest of our industries, while the already over-rated towns- people are to be called upon to subsidise the land- owners to the extent of another two millions a year. As a matter of fact urban ratepayers have us setancoemteh ar tpleioe awbh eet live in claim thef oer ui emir eyr idails tarsiseitss-. In almost all towns, and especially in large towns, there is a steady growth in the demands upon the</p>
        <p>rates. On the other hand there has been al decrease in the rates in many rural districts. On Monday, Sir HENRY FOWLER illustrated this fact by referring to the connties of Norfolk and Hamp- shire. The county rates in Norfolk have declined in the course of the last twenty years and now stand at 2s. 4d. in the pound, but in the county town, Norwich, the rates are 7s. 4d. in the pound, and in Great Yarmouuth ss. 2d. In the county of Hants the rates have fallen from 3s. to 25., but in the towns of Southampton and Portsmouth in the same county they are ss. 6d. and ss. Old. Yet so dominated by landlord influence is the present government that the lightly rated counties are to be still further relieved at the expense of the heavily-rated towns. When taking these things into account it is easy to believe the report that the Tory repre- sentatives of borough constituencies fear the effects of the Agricultural Rating Bill upon their constituent s.</p>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN pal d a rather Lord Salisburv's " Flrm Hand. awkward d compliment to Lord „ SALISBURY in his speech at the Constitutional Club on Wednesday. He rejoiced, he said, that Lord SALISBURY'S "firm hand" was at the helm, and he believed that the ship of state would be successfully piloted through the" strong seas and narrow channels" of international politics. It would have been interesting if one of Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S auditors had asked him why our foreign relations could be likened to "strong seas and narrow channels." These strong seas have all risen since Lord SALISBURY took over the reins at the Foreign Office; and, what is more serious still, his "firm hand" does not seem to be able to bring the vessel of state into calm waters The country will notice with alarm that some warning notes are again being sounded with re- gard to the Venezuelan question. The " Times correspondent—a well-known and accomplished American journalist who has the command of ex- ceptional sources of information—sends word that not only is this dispute not on the eve of settle- ment but that the negotiations between our Government and that of the United States are at a standstill. This is, to say the least of it, rather disturbing news. The people of this country are practically unanimous in believing that a war with the United States would be one of the worst calamities that could happen in international affairs, and a comfortable reeling had spread abroad that the dispute was in a fair way for settlement. It will, therefore, came as a disagreeable surprise, that Lord SALISBURY'S diplomacy has been no more success- ful in this affair than with regard to Armenia. It will be remembered that Lord SALISBURY, soon after he had taken office, spoke out with com- mendable force and freedom with respect to the SULTAN'S treatment of the Armenians, and that about the same time there were some suggestive movements of the Mediterranean fleet. The opinion was widely entertained that our PREMIER was about to settle the Armenian question with a "firm hand," but, alas, not many weeks had elapsed before it was known that English policy had completely failed at Constantinople. The Armenians were deserted, and their last state was worse than their first. Is this recent chapter of history to be repeated in connection with Vene- zuela ? The war feeling having almost completely subsided in the United States there has lately been a clear field for judicious diplomacy, and if Lord SALISBURY has not made the best use of his opportunity a heavy responsibility will rest upon him.</p>
        <p>In Runcorn there seems to be nothing more talked about than the deplorable condit io of trade in the town. For some time now, the authorities, the tradespeople, and the town folk, have been crying poverty, crying it so loud indeed that the condition of the town has not only become a sore point with the inhabitants generally,but has also in its alleged depth of poverty become a bye word with outsiders of the commercial 'class. Whether the town has become so .commercially bad as has been lately pictured is open to question, though beyond doubt trade is very stagnant and a revival would be benefical to everyone. Amid all the hope - less expressions so often heard of late it is pleasant to see Mr. Jones grasp the question and put before, the leading body of the town a suggestion which judicioasly carried out ought to bear good fruit. It is even more pleasant, to hear MI. Handley in a vigorous speech bid his townmen cease their cry of poverty and look with more hopeful eyes towards the future.</p>
        <p>There • was rather a strange sight in the Warrington police court one morning this week. Usually on entering the dim interior of this court of justice, there is nothing particularly noticeable beyond the afore- mentioned dimnese,but on Thursday morning every eye was immediately struck with an unusual gay- ness near the witness box. Closer approach dis- closed the fact that:a draper's stall had :apparently been established along the solicitor's desk, for that piece of court furniture was covered from end to end with a large anvaried collection of milinery, etc. This, it turned out, was but part of the pro- ceeds of a series of systematic robberies from stalls in the market by one woman du ring the past nine months. Several of the numerous peculations from the Market have been brought home to the per- petrators of late, but this is the most important of any owing to its extent, and the police are deserv- ing of commendation for their watchfulness. The magistrates also did the right thing in sentencing the woman to a quarter of a ye ar's hard labour, as a warning to others who, there is no doubt, still carry on nefarious practices in the same direction.</p>
        <p>Durin,</p>
        <p>THEATRII ROYAL. the week Messrs. Crompton and Forster have I</p>
        <p>_ been apparently before good houses in their drama" A Man's Ambition." The piece is well :staged, a plunge into real water being very realistic. The characters ire well played by a strong and capable company. For NAME BROOCREs, solid silver name brooches 100, (any name) Government 'tamped. Presents or Aracles for yourself, you may visit DEBERT'S, 10, CHURCH-STREET, LIVERPOOL, where you can get hundreds of articles such as Watclr2s, Clocks, Jewellery, Electro-Plate, Cutlery, Purses, Pocket Knives, etc., at -wholesale prices. ~ee advertisement (Illustrated Chron- grap Watch, 6s. 11d.,_et.c.), on pige 7 f thii Paper. -Advt</p>
        <p>DEILTR OF MRS. RIGBY, RUNCORN. On Thursday evening a highly respected Runcarn lady pas.;ed cplietly away at her residence in Greenwa3-road, in the person of Mrs. James Rigby. The deceased lady was 83 years of age and the widow of the late Mr.:ames Rigby, well known as a joiner and builder in the town. The Rigby family is perhaps one of the oldest in Run- corn. The deceased leaves two sons and two daughters one of whom, Harry, is the captain of the " Hawarden Castle "of the famous Sir Donald Currie line.</p>
        <p>RIT NCO RN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening a meeting (If the Parliamentary and General Purposes Committee was held at the Town Hall. Mr. Speakman, J.P., C.C., was re-elected chair- man and Mr. James Pritchard was appointed vice-chair- man.—At a meeting of the Technical Instruction Committee, also held on Thursday evening. Mr. F. Wiswall was again elected to fill the chair for tlae next twelve months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>RUNCORN AND wiDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND wiDNES.</p>
        <p>DEAF AND DUMB.—DRUNIE AND DISORDERLY. On Tuesday at the Runcern Police Court. before Messrs. F. J. Norman and S. Taylor, Michael Finnegan, of 18, Lock-street, a deaf and dumb man was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Lock-street on the previous night. P.C. Nicholls proved the case and stated that he was creating a disturbance and ill-treating his wife.—A fine of se. including costs was inflicted. RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE. The usual fortnightly meeting of the above society was held on Saturday night, the chair being occupied by Mr. George Holt. On the cemplesion of the routine business the remainder of the evening was devoted to impromptu speaking, in which the following took part. Messrs. Thomas Cooke, Thomas J. Holt, Edward Key, George Holt, Thomas Clarke and Daniel Bisbrowne. SERIOUS ACCIDENT AT RUNCORN. A somewhat serious accident occurred at Runcorn on Monday evening. Three girls aged about 14 were play- , ing on some bogie trucks lying on lines at the back ofil Parker-street, when one ot them, Mary E. Faulkner, of Parker-street, tell off one of the trucks, the wheels of which were pushed over her legs. Assistance was immediately rendered the untortunate girl but not before serious injuries had been inflicted. The child was taken home and Dr. Rubinson was called and on examination found one of the child's legs to be badly lacerated and , bruised. The bones were not broken. I BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY, RUNCORN. The concluding meeting of the session in connection with this society was held on Wednesday night in te Brunswick School, Runcorn. Tea was served during the early part of the evening,to which about sixty members sat down. After tea,a short musical entertainment was held, songs teeing given by the Misses Eder., A. Taylor, Simitin, and Crosby, and Mr. Morris. The chair was occupied by Mr. T. Simister, and the meeting was ad- dressed by Mr. W. Millington and Mr. J. D. Jones. The , remainder of the evening was spent in games and exer- cises in the gymnasium. RUNCORN CYCLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The Saturday section met at Runcorn on Saturday and travelled to Newton Lake, where the pleasures of that popular haunt were thoroughly enjoyed. Tea was served at the Oak Tree Hotel. On Wednesday afternoon a good number of members started from Devonshire- square and rode to High Leigh where they met the members of the Manchester Telegraghists club. Sides were formed and e. game of association football monopo- lised most of the afternoon, the Runcorn men winning a pleasant game by 5 goals to nil. Tea was provided at the High Leigh farm and Runcorn was reached about 9.30 in the evening after an enjoyable outing. SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY AT RUNCORN. The anniversary sermons in connection with the Cam- den Sunday schools were held on Sunday in St. Paul's Chapel, Runcorn. Both at the morning and evening services the Rev. D.A. de Mouilpied delivered impressive and appropriate sermons. There were good congrega- tions throughout the day and collections were taken on behalf of the school funds. On Monday evening tha Rev. D. A. de Meuilpied gave his popular lecture on the " Edict of Nantes." The chair was occupied by Mr. Robert H. Posnett. At the commencement an organ re- cital was given by Mr. William Cooke. At the conclu- sion of the lecture a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the lecturer on the motion of Mr. Speakman,seconded by Mr. Harrison. The collections realised EQ. MANCHESTER PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY. A meeting of the members of the Parliamentary Com- mittee for the Runcorn Rural District Council was held on Thursday evening, the matter under consideration being a communication from the Local Government Board re the provisional order for the above Pi Ts,. In it the Council were strongly recommended to withdraw their objection to it, for otherwise it would be necessary to apply for an Act of Parliament, and any injustice which might arise would be far more easily remedied by a provision l order than a Parliamentary statute. In the former case the Local Government Board would be able to make alterations if found necessary. The letter was considered at length, and it was decided to approach Sir J. T. Brunner, Bart., M.P., through Mr. Speakman, to get him to use his infinence with the London autho- rities with a view to having the order altered as desired or considerably modified. ST. MICHAEL'S SUNDAY SCHOOL, RUNCORN. On Tuesday evening a concert in connection with this school was held in the Primitive Methodist new school- room, Greenway-road. The programme was of a mis- cellaneous character comprising songs by Miss J.Linaker Miss E. Timmins, Mr. J. acd Mr. C. E. Linaker (duet), Mr. J. W. Pritchard, recitations by Mr. C. Chaatler, duet, violin and piano, Messrs. C. H. and A. C.Timrnins, and humorous sketches by Mr. H. J. K. Henderson. In the second portion of the programme Rhomberg's Toy Symphony was introduced, the performers being as fol- lows :—Miss Jessie Cameron,ldiss Kirstie Forrester, Miss Eva Gregmy, Miss Nellie Johnson, Miss Ida Kirkham, Miss Nora Lea,Miss Ada Pritchard,Mins Mabel Pope,Miss Gerty Raynor, Miss Maggie Smith, Miss Cissy Stokes, Miss Chrissie Sutton, Miss Taylor, Miss Timmins, Miss Florrie Timmins, Miss Pattie Timmins ; piano, Miss Crosby and Miss Pierpoint ; first violins, Mr. W. B. Kaye and Mr. C. Ditchfield ; second violins. Mr. G. Frith and Mr. H. Handley ; violincello,Mr. W. Simkin ; conducted by Mrs. W. B. Kay. Mrs. W. B. Kay accompanied throughout. A vote of thanks to the performers brought the entertainment to a close.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY COMMITTEE. The monthly meeting of this committee was held on Tuesday night in the Town Hall, Runcorn—present, Messrs. W. Handley, S. Pope, W. Hankinson, W. .1. Cunningham, T. Riley, Rev. J. Simpson, and Dr. Gastaldi. Mr. W. Handley Was re-elected chairman and Mr. S. Pops was made vice-chairman. It was decided that the Book S.-lection Committee should consist of the whole of the Council as before. The financial statement for the year presented showed an increase of £226 19s. I.ld, made up by balance in hand £7 83. 84.; fines, £8 us. 91. ; tickets, £6 15s. 44.; calalpoves, £1 59.10.1.; papers, and waste paper, £2 143. 9d.; lost and damaged books 3s. Id,; members' subscription3,£l 25.; donations, £5; sundties, 3,614 free library rate, £193 14s Toe expenditure was made up by salary of libratian £6O ; new books, £54 10 24. ; magazines and newspapers, £4l 55.; book binding, £ll 44. 104.; printing and stationary,£l6 8..; fittings, £3 6s. 61.; rent on library premises,£36 ; advertisements,£l 195.; sundries,£l 194.; total, £226 16s. 81.; leaving a balance in hand of 3s. 31. It was announced that a meeting of the librarians of the Mersey district will be held at Runcorn on the 22nd of May when it is expected there will be an interesting gathering aq the society purpose visiting Halton to see the famous Sir John Chesshyre's library.</p>
        <p>SEAMEN'S 111ISSION RUNCORN. On Monday night, a performance was given in the Town Hall Runcorn by the " Dusky Diamonds" bPtter known as members of the Liverpool Kentucky Darkies Amateur Minstrel troupe. The concert was arranged under the auspices of the Mersey Seamen's Mission with a view to helping to liquidate the debt existing on the Seamen's Institute at Runcorn. The programme sub- mitted by the burnt cork artistes followed closely on the lines associated with" nigger ministrels. Opening with the usual song and chorus the first portion of the enter- tainment was quickly gone turough. The individual efforts were good and the well balanced chorus proved very effective while the exertions of eight cornermen who were continually working to raise a laugh were greatly appreciated. Amongst the,singers were Messrs. T. Davies W. Wallace, R. R. Mothersole, W. It. Miller. R. H. Philips, T. Costello, W. Hughes, C. W. Cooke, F. Price, J. Jones, J. Arthur and C. J. Waring, and items were contributed by Mr. F. Merry jun., (fairy bells) and by the mandoline and banjo band. Mr. 'l'. Davies was an efficient interlocutor, Mr. Theo. Irwin acted as musical director and accompanist and Mr. W. M. Newman,acted as harmoniumist. During an interval in the proceedings the Rev. T. Lambert proposed a vote of thanks to the entertainers and also eulogised the work of Mr. Shaw and the Seamen's Mission. Mr. Shaw seconded the reso- lution which was cordially passed, and Mr. T. Davies. president, responded on behalf of the troupe.</p>
        <p>DEATS AND FUNERAL OF .9.N OLD RUNCORN LADY. On Thursday afternoon the remains of Mrs. Eliza Johnson, aged 80 years, latterly of 80, Westcliffe-road, Southport, were interred in the Parish Churchyard,Run- corn. The deceased lady will be known to -nany in the town as the widow of the late Mr. John Johnson, J.P., who up to about 20 years ago resided in Bank Chambers, High-st. He and his brother established the Runcorn Soap and Alkali Works and the Chemical Works at Weston, now the property of the United Alkali Company. To old Rnncornians the family were par- ticularly endeared, and remembered for their charitable aztions. The two brothers built and endowed the Runcorn Holy Trinity Church. They were both religi- ously inclined and a Sunday school was founded by them in connection with their works which was carried on with great success. Mrs. Johnson was a member of the Dorcas Society, and other kindred organisations. Before the funeral party left the deceased's late dwelling.a short service was conducted by the Rev. F. J. Gough, and amongst those in attendance was the Rev. E. Marsden, late vicar of Aston-by-Sutton. The remains were con- veyed by road to Runcorn, and reached the station shortly before three o'clock. The mourners present were Mr. J. Alcock Johnson, Southport, son ; Mr. Bateman, Bedford,grandson ; Mr. T. E. Reece Johnson,St. Aspath, nephew,and the Rev. Canon Hiemett.Ringway, nephew. At the station the cortege was joined by several well- known Runcorn gentlemen as well as a number of old employees of Messrs. Johnson. These included Mr.J.W. Lightburn.J.P., Dr. Meamgall,Messrs. J. T. Speakman, J. Fliggins,Rushton, Rigbey,Radley, R. Norman,E.Clare, W. Rowland ,Howard, Hayes, Barker, and E. Brazendale. The body was borne to the r:rive by Messrs. S. Parting- ton, T. Kirkham, W. Kirkham, and P. Brazendale 47TH YEAR IN SCOTLAND-ROAD, LIVERPOOL. where the last sad rites were performed by As will be seen in another column,Niessrs. Georga the Bev. (Senor)! Hignett, and the Rev. W. and Co. of Scotland-road, Liverpool, an eminent and Li. Staples of many beautiful wreaths sent we noticed of the Runcorn Parish Clergy staff. old-established firm, announce their 92nd half- amongst t tributes from the following—Miss Bateman, Mrs. Bate- yearly stocktaking sale of the season's accumula- man, Mr. C. Bateman, Mr. U. B. liateman' Miss W. B. don, of tine gold and silver watches, genuine Bateman, Miss Sladen (Blackheath), Mrs. Johnes-omith diamonds, marble clocks, &amp;c. Warrington and die- triet visitors to Liverpool will doubtless, befuic (Southport), Mrs. Johnes-Smith's children. Mrs. Mid- wood (Birkdale), Mrs. Brazi,, Mrs. Marsden (Southpurchasing, gladly avail themselves of this oppor- tunity, all goods being offered at from 55. to 10s. in 'port), Mrs. Giles, Miss Eggera (Chester), Mizs Maggie Clarke (Southport), M 3 M. Clarke's class, Mr. H. M. every £ reduction, and written guarantee given .Lawronea, Mr. and Mrs. Gamon :(Asliton Grange), with each article. and Mr. Thomas Kirkham.</p>
        <p>Widnes has never had a reputation for immunity from crime or criminals, and the proceedings at the Warrington Police Court on Thursday morning did not tend to better the reputation of the town—far otherwise. flier° were, on th at occasion, up for hearing two serious felony cases implicating four prisoners all of whom were given a three months' vacation in one of Her Majesty's gaols, with hard abour as a stimulant to existence. At the con- clusion of the business of the court it was remarked with a smile, but no semblance of astonishment that all the four prisoners came from Widnes,eithei: direct or within a short period of time. This was certainly an unfortunate coincidence so far as the reputation of Widnes is concerned, and it behoves the police of the chemical town to make renewed exertions to remove the stigma cast upon the town's "fair fame." Perhaps,were they to capture and convict say half-a-dozen Warrington" notorie- ties " it would go a long way towards making a neat revenge, and taking everything into account —especially the temporary disappearance of the " notorieties" —Warringtonians would not feel dis- posed to grumble.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>POULTRY STEALING AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>POULTRY STEALING AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. F. J. Norman and S. Taylor, at the Runcorn Police Court on Tuesday, Thomas Cross, Corn- wall-street, Bootle, was charged under a warrant with stealing eight pigeons and three hens, vat ,e £2 2s. 61., the property of Joseph Orme, Holloway, Runcorn, on October 24th, 1894. and also with stealing six pigeons valued at Bs. belonging to Henry Taylor, of Okell-street, on the same date. In the case of Taylor it was shown that the prisoner lived close to him and the pigeons were missed from his cote but afterwards returned to him by the police.—P.C. Butler stated that he was on duty in the vicinity of Orme's house early in the morning of October 24'h, 1894, when he saw a light in the hen cote. Thinking this unusual he was about to proceed to the place when he saw Cross with a dog. The former made off while the dog came towards him. After getting a good distance Cross called the animal away and though the officer tollowed, prisoner succeeded in making good his escape. Sergeant Lee and P.C. Gregory next morning visited Mrs. Cross' house in Okell-street and beneath one of the beds they found a basket containing a fowl which acuused's mother said belonged to her, she having bled it. Under the stairs was a hamper beneath a lot of wood and in it were 12 pigeons, some of which belonged to Taylor and others to Orme. With regard to the theft from Orme's the owner spoke to leaving his cote contain- I ing hens and pigeons safely securei, and to its being broken into.and the birds taken.—P.C. Wylie stated that he apprehended Cross at the Gas Works, Bootle, on the previous afternoon. In answer to the charge de- fendant said "I was drunk at the time and do not know how many there were."—Cross now admitted tak- ing the birds but said it was when drunk, and he did not know what he was doing.—Superintendent Okell expresed the opinion that the mother was net altogether , blameless in the matter. Prisoner was committed to gaol , for a month on each charge with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>W ESTON.</head>
        <p>W ESTON.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL MEETING. The annual meeting of this Council was held on Friday evening in the Cnurch schoolroom—present Messrs. Ball, Basnett, Guest, Tudor, Somerville, Joseph Ford, F. W. Clarke, T. J. Grounds, T. T. Sill, R. R. Clarke, James Collier (clerk.—The members signed the declaration of acceptance of office. Mr. Ball moved that Mr. Sill be the chairman for the ensuing year. He had presided on one or two occasions and had done so with ability and was conversant with the business.—Mr. R.R. Clarke seeonded.—Mr. Guest proposed the re-appointment of Canon Wood who he thought had discharged the duties of the offiJe with credit to himself and the Council. The amendment was not seconded and the motion was then carried, only Mr. Guest voting against it. Messrs. Thomas Ford and E. Aaron was re-appointed overseers. Mr. Basnett moved and Mr. Ball seconded that the names of the beneficiaries of the Orred Charity be posted at the Government offices of Weston and Weston Point. The motion was carried.—The Clerk submitted : ccounts amounting to £lO 7s. of which £lO 41. 10d. were the costs incurred by the election. Last year the cost was £2B.—The Clerk intimated that the balance in hand at the close of the year was £7 ss. 4d. Mr. Basnett proposed, Mr. Somerville seconded and it was resolved that a precept for £lO be issued on the overseers.-- This concluded the business. ' ALLEGED THEFT OF OLD IRON.—On Wednesday morn- ing at the Runcorn Police Court, before Messrs. P. Speakman and F. J. Norman, Edward Goodacre and Mary flaspell were charged with staling a quantity of scrap iron from the works of the United Alkali Co., and a quantity of iron from the Runcorn and Helsby Red Sandstone Co., recently.—Mr. Tudor, assistant manager of the Alkali Works, identifiod the iron, which he said had been taken away daily during the last four weeks. Thellprisoners lived in:one of the company's houses at Weston. Mr. Guest also identified a portion of the iron produced as belonging to the quarry company. Amongst the iron were two crowbars and a acrappling hammer. P.C. Morris stated that he was on duty near Weston Point on the 21st April when he saw the prisoner [(aspen coming from behind the old sulphur plant at Weston, carrying a bag on her back. He asked her what she had in the bag,and she replied that she had nothing in it but cinders. He searched the bag and amongst a lot of cin- ders found the piece of iron produced. He then accom- panied the woman home, and on searching the premises found 20 or 30 cwt. of iron in the yard, which the de- fendant Goodall said belonged to him. Mr. Tudor, the assistant manager of the works identified a quantity of , the iron.—Prisoners were remanded until Monday, bail being allowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH EXTENSION AND COMPLETION FUND.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH EXTENSION AND COMPLETION FUND.</p>
        <p>A meeting in connection with the above, for the pur- pose of forming a Building Committee, was held in the vestry of St. John's Church, Earlestown, on Thursday evening. The Rev. T. R. Matthews presided, and there were present, Messrs. Pimblett, Foden, Andrews' War- ing, Johnson, Cunliffe, Wild, Eaton and Rev. E. W. W Kaye. The Chairman said the obj.ct of the meeting was to form the nucleus of a Building Committee with a view to the completion of the church. Be did not know that , he need ge into the history of what was already built. Mr. Andrews said before going any further he thought, seeing that there was such a poor attendance, that it would be better to adjourn the meeting for a fortnight. Mr. Foden seconded. Mr. Waring as an amendment moved that they pro- ceed with the business. Mr. Pimblett seconded and the amendment wa trried. The Chairman said the first thing was to elect a com- mittee, and be would like to have some nominations. It was decided that the churchwardens and sides- men be included in the committee, and Mr. Pimblett submitted the following additional names, viz. : Dr. Mouneey, Dr. Pattison, Dr. Valentine, Messrs. Hewitt, F. J. Emmett, T. Robinson, N. Shim, Duckworth, Southern (Collins Green), F. A. Brown (chemist), J. Horridge, Muscroft, N. Siddeley, Marsden, T. Bowes,M. Winstanley, T. Waring, Gregory,Rylance,Sutton (Crow- lane), W. Tarbuck, A. Smith, Makin.W. Kay,W. Monks, J. Waddington, C. Conway, G. Slinn, Groves, Wild, Travis, J. Roscoe, J. Bunting, N. Johnson, and the Sun- day School Committee Mr. t'imblett moved that these gentlemen be asked to serve on the committee, Mr. Andrews seconded, and it was carried. Mr. J. Rvlance moved, and Mr. N. Johnson seconded, that Mr. Pimblett, and Mr. T. Molyneux be asked to act as treasurers. On the motion of Mr. Andrews, Messrs. H. H. Ander- ton, H. Tarbuck and T. Martindale were appointed secretaries for the fund. On the motion of Mr. Waring seconded by Mr. Pim- blett, it was decided to invest the food in the Manches- ter and Liverpool District bank. The Chairman said according to the plans the tower of the church would be in Sankey-street, and the nave and aisles would go right through to the West end, and there would be a small entrance in St. John-street. There was no doubt it would be a fine building when completed. The estimated cost of the extension was about £7,000, and the accommodation, when completed, not including the gallery would be 1100 on the ground floor, . when they remember that with a sitting accommodation for 600, and they often had over 1,000 people in, they would be able to see what they could do when they got sitting ac- commodation for-1,100. Still he did not think it w ould be at all too large for the population. It had increacied almost double since he came to Earlestown. In 1879 the population waq a little over 5,000,and now he was told it was over 9,000. The meeting afterwards dispersed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>PNEUMATIC TYRES.</head>
        <p>PNEUMATIC TYRES.</p>
        <p>AN EARLFSTOWN GENTLEMAN'S INVENTION. We understand that a local gentleman residing in Wargrave. Earlestown, has this week patented an un- puncturable pneumatic tyre for bicycles. So far the particulars of the inventi-in have not been made public:, but this much we know, that the air tube is fitted to the wheel rim without wire, an I is without an outer cover- ing. It is claimed to be entirely unpuncturable,and can be fitted to any pneumatic bicycle rim. Tht. inventor is perfectly sanizuine of success, insomuch that the tyre has withstood the necessary tests.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>NEWTON DIVISION CONSERVATIVE ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>NEWTON DIVISION CONSERVATIVE ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING. The annual meeting of the Newton Division Conserva- tive Association was held on Saturday afternoon at the Town Hall, Newton-le-Willows. There was a large at- tendance, over which Mr. Grimke Drayton, vice-chair- man of the council, presided, in the absence through ill- ness of the chairman (Mr. Thomas Pearson). Amongst' those present were the Hon. T. W. Legh, M.P. for the diviion, Cole net Pilkington, Alderman Sutton (Warring- ton), W. Holland Owen (hon. solicitor), C. B. F. Bor- ron, W. P. Skelton, Robert Stone, W. Collingwood, Thomas Walker (E •cleeton), William Richardson (Rain- ford), Rev. Mr. Monk kNewton), and others. The 10th annual report of the council recorled the re- election of the Hon. T. W. Legh, as member for the constituency by an increased majority,which was largely due to the united and energetic exertions of the various polling district officials. The Radicals did all in their power to secure the return of their candidate, and con- sidering the large influx into the division of collectors from a particular public company with which the defeated Radical candidate was associated, the coun- cil heartily congratulated the association on the decided and increased majority of their member. Hearty thanks were aecorded to the Liberal Unionists of the division for their continued valuable support, finan- cial and otherwise. The wcrk of the association was carried on with great economy and efficiency without in any way weakening the machinery or neglecting any of its interests, but notwithstanding the great assistance rendered by its usual bands of voluntary workers, the council thought it necessary to appeal for further finan- cial help. Mr.Borron,one of the hon.• treasurers, reported that the association was in a sound financial losition. There was a considerable balance in hand, the expendi- ture had been less than in former years.and the subscrip- tions well maintained. (Applause.) The Chairman briefly moved the adoption of the report and financial statement,coupling with the motion the congratulations ot the ineetine to Mr. Holland Owen on the occasion of his recent marriage. Mr. Jones se- conded,and the motion was passed with acclamation. The chairnan next moved the re-election of Lord GerarJ as president of the association. The eroposition was agreed to unanimously, and on the motion of Mr. Skelton. se- conded by Mr. Oulbridge,a vote of thanks was accorded to the hon. treasurers. Messrs. Stone and B rron, the hon. auditors,Mesers. Voysey, Worthington ,and Burden, and the hon. solicitor, Mr. Holland Owen. Alderman Sutton then moved the following resolu- tion :—" That this meeting welcomes the accession of the Unionist party to pov.r. and sincerely hopes that their domestic, foreign and colonial politics may tend to establish peace and honour throughout the world, and prosperity to the British Empire." Mr. Joseph Catterall (Eerlestown), seconded. The Hon. T. %V. Legh, M.P., in supporting the resolu- tion, congratulated the party upon their notable victory injuly last over the combined forces of Radicalism and the Prudential Society. (Applauee.) Proceeding to re- view the political situation, he said that the American danger in regard to Venezaela had temporarily, at any rate, passed away, but trumpery as the matter might have been at the time, and dim as their recollection of it might now be, the situation was yet one of consider- able danger, because, if he was not greatly mistaken, there was a large body of public opinion in the United States which was desperately hostile to this country,and only ton ready to seize an opportunity to quarrel with us. The only satisfactory feature of the affair was the way in which all politicians in this country united in the defence of their native land. Their opponents de- served every honour for their attitude, and as long as that spirit existed we need not fear a., regards our foreign policy. (Hear, hear.) The Transvaal difficul- ties were the direct inheritance of the mistaken policy ot Mr. Gladstone, which had resulted in establishine a situation for British inhabitants there which had be- come well-nigh intolerable. The declaration of British policy could not be long delayed. We could not wait inaefinitely for the goodwill of President Kruger and his Government. (Hear, hear.) We expected and had every right to expect, that the just demands of our countrymen in the Transvaal should be acceded to. He was mistaken if an attempt would not I shortly be made in the House of Commons to elicit a distinct expression of the Government's opinion on the matter, and he would te disappointed if that was not found to be thoroughly satisfactory. (Applause.) The "splendid isolation" of England was all very well in its war, but he was in favour of making friends. After the difficulties with the United States and Germany, it was evident that there was only one civilised Power with whom it would be possible for us to come to terms, and that was Russia, but the question of coming to terms with Russia or any other Power must be left to peeple in a more responsible position than he was, and more able to judge whether the friendship of Hassla or any other Power was worth the sacrifice which that friend- ship would entail. As regards Armenia,he had ventured in the Hoese ot Commons to speak against isolated ac- tion by England, and he had deprecated inflammatory speeches that had been made. He had been rebuked and his conduct had been denounced as heartless, but the conclusions he had formed had been entirely justified. (Rear, hear.) Dealing with the Soudan expedition, he said that it fell almost entirely on the Egyptians them- selves. who were naturally anxious to recover possession of their lost territory and to relieve the people that were groaning under the rule of the Khalifa. Speaking from his experience in Egypt, he did not think the task of smashing tin the Khalifa was so great as people imagined, and he hoped that Egypt would soon be free from permanent danger. In regard to domestic questions the outlook was more encouraging. Their op- ponents were probably geing to select the education bill as the field of battle in the House of Commons. Most of those present were no doubt supporters of the voluntary schools. (Hear, hear.) All of them were anxious to provide the best possible education for the children of , the country ; but they did not want to see their own , voluntary schools pushed entirely to the wall, and if the late Minister of Education, Mr. Acland, had remained in office much longer he was inclined to think that they would have disappeared altogether. They did not, as supporters of the voluntary system, desire to lower the standard of education What they wanted was to keep their own system up to the level, and be helped in doing it, and surely this was a very reasonable request to make. (Hear, hear.) The education bill was a great and important measure and it was quite clear that if it was to be passed in its entirety some other bill would have to be dropped. He had no doubt as to which bill should be dropped—it was the Irish land bill. (Hear, hear.) They had been engeged for years in passing land bills for Ireland.and it was only fair to ask Irishmen to wait one year whilst the English and Welsh passed a much needed education bill for themselves. (Applause.) Colonel Pilkington also supported the resolution, and urged the importance of strengthening the army and navy to such an extent as to place the empire in the position of absolute safety. He was not in favour of making alliances by money and concessions ; we should be in a position net only that we could do without others, but that others could not do without us. (Applause.) The resolution was then adopted. and subsequently , the hon. auditors were re-elected, on the motion of Mr. John Jackson, seconded by Mr. T. G,eg,ory ; while Dr. Handeside and Mr. Borron were reappointed represen- tatives to the Lancashire and Cheshire Union of Conser- vative Associations. Prior to the annual meeting. Mr. W. Holland Owen, honorary solicitor to the association, was entertained at luncheon by the members of the finance committee at the Central Conservative offices, and was the recipient of a handsome wedding gift from the hands of the Hon. T. W. Leah. The present took the form of a massive silver centre piece, and bore the following inscription : —" Presented to William Holland Owen Esq., hon. solicitor to the Newton division (county of Lancashire) Conservative Associationeon the occasion of his marri- age, 12th April, 1896." Mr. Owen returned warm thanks on behalf of himself and his wife for the ha a- some gift, and remarked that it was his earnest wish to continue his efforts in the Conservative cause in the division.</p>
