      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>CAMPANOLOGY AT ST. PAUL'S</head>
        <p>CAMPANOLOGY AT ST. PAUL'S</p>
        <p>On Monday evening, for practice, the ringers of St. Paul's Church, Warrington, rang a silent peal of Plain Bod Minor, with second as observation bell. The ringers stood as follows :—Treble, W. Oxley ; 2nd, J. Pomfrey ; 3rd, A. Oxley ; 4th, E. Edwardson, sth, W. Brammer ; tenor, F. Stout. Time 241- minutes ; weight of tenor, 16 cwt. This is the first silent peal accomplished by the whole of the above ringers, and the first on the bells.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>lathe House of Commons on Thursday Mr. Chamber- lain, answering Mr. T. Bayley, said the High Commis- sioner at the Cape had not received any complaint from the Matabele as to the treatment of native women at the hands of the Chartered Conipany's servants. With re- gard to the ownership of the cattle of the Matabele, he had directed that as soon as zircumstances permitted an inquiry should be held into the manner in which the law in that respect had been administered. Mr. CURZON informed Mr. Labouchere that negotia- tions with the United States in reference to Venezuela were still proceeding. It would not be advisable to make any statement with regard to them at present. Questioned as to the accuracy of the reports of his speech on the preceding night, Mr. CHAMBERLAIN said he had no doubt the reports were, as usual, substanti- ally correct. In his speech he alluded to matters of com- mon report with regard to the administration of the Transvaal, but though that did not constitute any special ground for urgency of discussion, the Govern- ment were prepared, if a considerable section of the- House desired to discuss colonial affairs, to put down a. colonial . vote. He was informed that the reply of President Kruger to the invitation to visit England might arrive at any moment, and after the situation had been considered by the- . Government he should lay on the table the whole of the papers with regard to the correspondence which bad been passing on the subject. Answering Sir E. A. Bartlett, Mr. CnaikinnsmaiN said the Boer Government had established strong patrols on the north-western and western boundaries of the Trans- vaal in order to prevent the introduction el lindernest. The rumour that a large number of German immigrants were landed the other drAy at Delagoa Bay had absolutely contradicted by the owners of the ship which was alleged to have clar.veyed them there. The House subsequently went into Committee of Ways anct Means. Sir M. E. BRACH replied that the cost of the Soudan expedition Would be borne by the Egyptian Government, while the Chartered Company would pay for the cost of the suppression of the Matabele insurrec- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL MEETING. The annual meeting of the Halton Parish Council was held in the Girl's School, on Tuesday night. Present : Messrs. John Ackerley, Jo.eph Astbury, Samuel Barrow, Bobert Hughes. John S. Jeacock, James Leoker, Joseph Manning, Alfred Povey, Joseph Pritchard, Joseph Stokes, and Mr. Woodier (clerk). Mr. Pritchard proposed, and Mr. Barrow seconded, that Mr. J. Stokes should act as chairman for the year. This resolution was carried.—ln responding, Mr. Stokes thanked them for the honour paid him, and trusted that they would work together and so make his duties com- paratively light.—Mr. Jeacock was appointed vice- chairman! on the motion of Mr. Looker seconded by Mr. R. Hughes.—The overseers, Messrs. H. Rowe, and J. Ackerley were unanimously re-elected, and a vote of thanks to them for past services was proposel by Mr. Looker seconded by Mr. Povey, and carried unanim- ously.—There was no other business of importance.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON OCCASIONAL COURT. At this court on Monday, before Mr. Bleckly, Ellen Smith, Rixton.was charged with being drunk at that place on the 19Ai inst., and was fined 2s.flu and costs, or seven days imprisonment in default,</p>
