      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>MAKE LIFE ENJOYABLE.</head>
        <p>MAKE LIFE ENJOYABLE.</p>
        <p>Loss of health means loss of all which makes life en- joyable; for, in addition to the bodily pain and suffering which it cause:, it entails a vast amount ot mental pain and anxiety, especially in the homes of the working classes. It brings in its train a host of discomforts which retard the recovery of the sufferer, and hinder, instead of assisting, Nature to prform her functions. When a working man suffers from any ailment which prevents his following his daily avocation, he is natur- ally filled with anxiety regarding the maintenanca of those depending on him for their daily sustenance. His very love for his family retards and hinders his speedy recovery, and his spirits become depres:ed at the antici- pation of the gloomy prospects before them in the event of a long continuance of his illness. It is well known that depression of spirits often Proves a serious bar to a speedy recovery.</p>
        <p>These results arise in addition to the various incon- veniences and discomforts which the sufferer and his family experience as direct results of his illness, such as additional expenses for luxuries for the sick, with in- creased difficulty to obtain those luxuries, or even the necessities ot life.</p>
        <p>Mmst of the various forms of disease which trouble the labouring classes of the country, and which are often attributq hie, more or less, to some unhealthly condition of their daily work, may be classed under a few loading beads.</p>
        <p>To avoid tha Oiseomforts arising from various forms of disease,every working man should seek the aid of</p>
        <p>THE VEGETABLE TONIC,</p>
        <p>Which has gained a world-wide renown as the most wonderful medicinal discovery of the age; and as un- failing remedy it is prescribed by many leading physi- cians in various ailments.</p>
        <p>For Bronchitis, Asthma, Coughs, and Colds. Shortness of Breath, N.uralgia, General Weakness, Low Spirits, Nervousness, Indigestion, Iniluenza. GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS</p>
        <p>s Sold In Bottles at 2. 9J. and 4s. ed. each. S.e the name Gwilym Evans on Llbel, Stamp and Bottle.</p>
        <p>Sole Proprietors: Quinine Bitters' Manufacturing Co., Ltd., Lianelly, South Wales. G WILYM EVAN'S QUININE BITTERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>9 EFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>9 EFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A THEFT FROM ORFORD BARRACHS.—THREH PBRUEDO /becalms.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. Sutton and Ridgway, at the Warring- ton R-rough Police Court on Tbursday, three men Patriek M'Cabe, Temple-st.. Widnes; John Townsend, 18. Travis•st., Widnes, and Patrick McCreece, 38, bugs- dale-road,Widnes,were all charg d on remand from Mon- day. with stealing or receiving knowing to have been stoien, from the receiving room at Orford Birracks,two jacketF, two vests, two pairs of boots, a silk scarf, a breast pin, and five photographs, the property of John Pickering and Michael Raftry, some time be;ween the 13th and 14th April.—All pleaded guilty.— The Chief Constable said the circum,tances were that, on the 13th Inst., the three prisoners presented themselvEs at Orford Barrails as recruits for the militia, and as such were as- signed beds in the receiving room at 9.30 in the ever- big; 22 or 23 other persons slept in the came room, al r.:.cruits. In the ordinary conrse of events they should all have presented themselves for medical examination next day. Lights were put out at 10 o'clock, and the prisoners were then there, but at 6 o'clock next morn- ing the Corporal who came to get them up found that the three prisoners had decamped in the night and had not gone empty handed. A number of articles, clothing, etc, belonging to the other recruits was found to have been stolen., and al,o barraekroom sheets, but with f gard to the latter he had reason to believe the prisoners knew nothing about them. Information was given to the police and a pretty accurate description of the men I circulated and also of the property. Sergeant Hunt of I the Northwich police observed the men in Northwich on I Saturday, and was suspicions that they were the men I wanted in Warrington. He located theni at a lodging I house, and after communicating with the Warrington Police, had them arrested. In M'Cabe's possession was fouLd the se.arfpin whieh was included in the charge. All three were handed over to detectives Meacock and Footer of Warrington,and it was found that the men had been eealing with the whole of the st•den property. Several of the goods were traced. Since their remand on Monday the men had made voluntary statem, nts to the polk.e.which made a clear breast of the whole matter and incriminated all the prisoners. The Chief Constable concluded his statement by expressing his thanks to S..r- geant Hunt and the Northwieb pAice for the assistance they had given him.—M'Cabe and McCreece had prf— Yious convictions against them, and all three were sent to gaol for three months each.—The magistrates also commended Sergeant Hunt for his sagacity and assist- ance to the Warrington police.</p>
      </div>
