      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.-" THE RELIEF OF LANDLORDS."</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday Mr. RITCHIE, answering Sir EiSincent,said he had no special Informas tion to the effect that arrangements were teeing made to bring large and increasing quantities of Westphalian coal to London at an inclusive land and water freight of about 63. a ton, owing to tke nominal rates charged by the German State Railways, bat he would make in- quiry through the Consul at Dusselchrf. Sir J. GORST told Mr. E. Grey that he was not yet able to state when the Government weuld introduce a Bill in favour of a State-sided scheme of superannuation of teachers in elementary schools. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN informed•Sir.E. A. Bartlett that he had received the names of the persons who were said to have witnessed the shooting of the man supposed to have belonged to Dr. Jameson's force on January 1. As to some of them he had been placed under such restric- tions as to disclosin 4 name as would prevent him from making much practical use of the information. He still hopes, however, to be able to get full Information as to the circumstances BP was not aware that law-abiding Englishmen in the Sauth African Republic were being subj cted to any maltreatment. Answering Mr. Arnold-Fost at, Mr. A. J. BALFOUR said it was not yet within the range of practical politics, in his opin on, to impose by law on the inhabitatents of this country so great a change as would be involved in giving effect to the recommendations of the Committee out he metric system. Mr. CURZON, answering a question put hy Mr. Steve- nson, said it was quite true that the terms on which Zeitun surrendered included the appointment of a Chris- tian to the office of Governor, and that the Turkish newspapers had announced the appointment to the post of a Mussulrnan. The British, French, and Russian Am- bassadors had protested against the appointment as i breach of faith, but no reply had so far been received from the Turkish Government. Sir M. HICV..BEACH atiled for leave to intreduce a bill to amend the Coal Mines Regulation Act, 1687. Mr. CHAPLIN afterwards asked for leave to introduce the Agricultural Rating Bill. He said the measure was one by which the Government hoped to do something to mitigate the severity of the existing depression in agri- culture. The proposal was to amend the law with re- gard to the rating of agricultural land in Eneland,but it wasinot therefore to be understood that the Government were not perfectly alive to the claims of other kinds of rateable property to an equitable adjustment of taxation also. They contemplated an early inquiry into that branch of the question eke, and he would take an early opportunity of stating the Government views as to the scope and method of that inquiry. The present measure, hetwent on to say, enacted that after the close of the financial year ending 31st March next agricultural land was to be assessed upon a propertion only instead of upon the whole of its rateable value. Be had been re- commended to adopt one-fourth as the proportion, but in consequence of the exceptionally heavy claims made upon the Chancellor of the Exchequer during the present year he had been obliged to adopt the proportion of one half. The result of the bill would be an estimated de- ficiency in the amount of rates raised hy local authorities of about £2.550,000, one half of which would Ise in- curred during the present year. The Government pro- I posed to meet it be a contribution from the Imperial ' Exchequer to the Local Taxation Accotant, which was I referred to in the bill as the annual grant. The first of these payments would be made in the six months preceding the 31st of March next, so as to pro- vide for the half-yearly payment whieh wonld be due to the local authorities on account of the first six months following thatrlate. Sir H. H. FOWLER, who followed, said it seemed to him that the Government had proposed no broad general principle dealing with the whole question, and it was his duty, on behalf of his friends on the Opposition side of the House to say that at the next stage, they would, on the ground both of its inelpediency and its injustice offer to the measure a most uncompromising opposition. Mr. G. WHITELEY, one of the Conservative members for Stockport. said he should oppose the Bill to the very hest of his ability. Sir W. HARCOURT said the question opened by the Gnvern- ment was by far the greatest financial question that had been onened in his parliamentary experience, and he was astonished at the recklessness and want of consider. ation with which the measure had been flung upon the floor of the House. The Bill was eventually brought in ana read a first time. TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>R.plying to Mr. J. C. Rtckett, Mr. CHATLIN said he availed himself of vPry opportunity of informing hoards of guardians that they had no legal right to suspend the nforcement of the vaccination laws pending the issue of the report of the Royal Commission.</p>
        <p>_ In answer to a number of questions, Mr. el! 14.3fISPIR. LAIN said it was true the Government asked the High Cnminissioner at the Cape to inform the President of the Transvaal that the Imperial G wernment had decided to send reinforcements to the Cape, and be believed there wet precedents for such a course. There had been of late so many inat (flees in which even official statementa had been magnified and distotted in South Africa that the Government thought it expedient,in order to prevent misapprehension, to publish at Capetown the exact nature of the movement they had decided to carry out, and at the same time to inform the president of the South African Republic of their intentions. His atten. tinn had been called to the importation of munitions of war into the South African Republic,but he would not be justified in interfering unless he had evidence to show that these preparations were intended for other than defensive purposes. He had not had any further com- munication from President Kruger in reference to his proposed visit to this country. The Helm afterwards went into Committee on the Military Marceuvres Bill, and considerable progress was made bef ore the sitting, was suspended. At the evening sitting' Mr. ARNOLD FORSTER moved a resolution con- demning the failure of successive Administrations to give employment in Civil departments to reserve or time-ex- pired soldiera and sailors of good character, and calling upon the Government to make larger provision for the employment of such persons,hy allotting to them prefer- entially such posts in the public service as they were competent to' fill. Captain NORTON seconded the mo- tion, which was eventually withdrawn in favour of an amended motion simply calling upon the Government to make all possible provision for the employment of re. serve and time-expired soldiers and sailors, by allotting to them preferentially such posts in the Dublin service as they are competent to fill. This was carried by 136 votes to 24, and the House shortly afterwards rose. WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday Mr.R. Palen moved the second reading of the Land Tenure and Rent Rovision Bill. He said the measure would undoubtedly mndify existing contracts between landlord and tenant, as well as to some extent restrict contracts to COMP. By it the compensation clauses of the Agricultural Holdings Act would be incroased,there would be a certain amount of security of tenure given.and there was a provision for tkies revision of the rent —Mr. BIIYNMOR Jos seconded the motinn.—Mr. VICTOR CAVENDISH Moved as an amendment that the House,whilst recognising the &amp;sir- ,.bleness of modifieations in the existing law relating to aerienitural tenar,cios, was of opinion that it had not neer, shown that the reversal of he general principles whieh had hitherto governed such tenancies was either d-sired by or was desirable in the interests of the van. nos classes engaged in the cultivation of the soil in Groat Witain.—Mr. VICARY Gum seconded the amend. ment.—Mr. Lowo said the Government had themselves givPn notine of a Bill for the amendment of the Agricul- tural Floldings Act, and the reason they had not yet in- troduced the measure was that they had received many representations,in which they concurred, that it was de- sirable they should know the mind of the Royal Commis- sion before dealing with a matter of such great import- anee. On behalf of the Government he opposed the Rill. The nontion for the seconding reading Was de- feated by 247 votes to 133,and the amendment was then agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>SHIP CANAL. CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL. CANAL.</p>
        <p>THE TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>During the week ending, Friday evening 86 sea-going vessels navigated the Ship Canal,in addition to a number of passenqPr steamers, tugs, and cargo barges. Of this number 49 arrived from and 37 sailed to other ports. Foreign tr,ffi, accounted for 17 arrivals and 12 derar- tures, whilst 32 vessels engaged in the home trade en- tered and 25 sailed from the canal in the same period. When the returns were made up there were nine vessels loading at Manchester for home and foreign ports, and 16 were discharging cargo. There was also a number on passage between Eastham and Manchester,and others tied up at various points along the waterwav So far this season 96,938 hales of American cotton and 60.409 bales of the wian staple have arrived via the Ship Canal, making a rAnti total of 157,347 hales.. The Levy is due shortly with 6.200 bates from Galveston and the Satur- nina is on passage with a further consignment from the same p:rt. The last opportunity for some time to come of shipping cotton from Galveston direct to Manchester is afforded by the Avona, now loading. Vessels other than regular liners are fixed and bound for the canal with gem-ral cargo from 14,,w York, timber from Baltic ports, west Bay, Mthile, and Apalachicola; wood nulp from tho Baltic!, fruit from Spain, iron ore from Huelva, and logwood from Jamaica.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>FOOTBALi,.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALi,.</p>
        <p>CABO. G. BU3SBY, &amp; CO., the lea-linz qu'irts Manufac- l...X terers of Lon'ion, 5.8., have published a most com- plete catslogne. It is bound to interest Cricketers, Golfers. Tenuisers, Croquet Players, anti th,slike. If you cannot ob- tain a copy from the local Dealer, send a post card to G. G. B. &amp; Co., as above.</p>
        <p>Uricketers should get the G. G. B. Diary. price 6}d. post free.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. WIDNES. These local rivala met on Saturday t WiCITIPS to play off the return match. The first meeting which took place during the first week in January was a very close</p>
        <p>affair, and was only won by a pecalty grml which Bur- ton kicked from near half way. The Widnesians ex• pressed themselves confluent of winning at the next encounter, but as events turned out, their predictions were woefully at fault. Neither side was represented at its full strenath on Saturday, for "Smack " Bats had not recovered from the injuries he received at the Wigan match and Hnughton did duty in his place. Broady and Nevins also cried off, and their respeetive places were taken by leAwless and Andrews. On the Widnes side. Drummond was ah•ent, and his place a‘ half was taken bv A. Peters. In Drummond's ahaenca, Barber captained the team, and it is therefore a little singular that the captain of each team hal the same name. The follow- ing were the nompnaition of each team, who were under the charge of Mr. Kennedy of Rrnughtan R 4ngers. War- rinoton Heesom, full hack ; Barber, Oros, Sutton, and Lawless. three quarters ; ll:mghton and Higginblttem, half backs ; Dakin. Thornton. Andrews, Fair- hnrst, Berry. Saunders. and Tiurrows.fnrwards. Widnes: Brennan, full hack; H. Peters, Rispan. Nevitt and Martin, three quarters ; Barber. and A Peters, half hark; ; .Tohnion, rower, E-nery. Handley, D•Innollv. T. eton, Wheelan,anci Dtthvshi-e forwards. Warrington had the advantage of having their hacks to the sun during the 111” st half of the game. The play was of a very open character at the cnmmencement and in fapt con.-inued. so to the close of the game. Barber</p>
        <p>(Wirinet kinked nil and after Cross's renly, A. Peters made his mark from which Flee;orn had to make a if, before the flrwards we on to him. The Wa.rrinmians had reanhod the half way flig. before they were checked end it was here that the first scrims maqe 1,71.1 fotand. The'" ChP115(11.18 " got the hall away well.ind Lawless was floored on the line. A hanky kick to the centre of the fiold EIPPM*Pi to have cleared the dan,zer,hnt Broon,n. the Widnes full hack, senored the ball, and ran riryht throolla the VlArrinnton taim '1 the line.and Ilnclinq Ressons in his way. parted to Foster who had no niflinnity in snnrinT, trhind the posts after</p>
        <p>two minutes' °lay. This was a splendid piece of Play by Brennan and he fully deserved the arinlause he received. The very easy place proved ton diffielilt for A. Peters who struck the uprights and the hall rehonndei into Widnes hafi not the lead for more than a minute for after Burton's restartea scrimmage was formed he- wind the half way line. and Houghton pinking up smartly dod-fed Barber in fine eyle and raced down the field with Iligginhottom &amp;nee 'behind him. On reaching Brennan. Ffnughton nassod to Higginbottom who after a good run got over and thus equalised, but Burton failed to kirk a goal. Immediately after the kink out, Lawless caused another minor to he raised by a futile drop at goal. From now to half time Wid- nes were thoroughly held in check and scarcely evengot to the half way line. Some brilliant passing after the above mentioned minor was witnessed ber,yeen Higginbotham. rrnsa and liarber.the latter being floored on the line. A similar piece of play on the opposite wing between Higginbnltom, Burtnn and Lawless, ended in Lawless being treated in a similar fashion to Barber. From the ensuing snrimm age, froughtnn got over, but unfortunately !oat possession of the hall. After some exchange kicking. Orrisa made his mark, and however Berry failed te score at the follow up is a mvaterv. was, however, evident that such a hot attack could not end without something tangible being scored, and at length Cross obtsinerl possession of the hall, and after a good run he kicked across the field to Lawless, ho snored at the corner. The difficult place 'Kick was siincessfrilly tak,n by Taylor and thus Warring- ton were five paint.' ahead. The game still went as strongly as ever in favour of Warrington, but nothing was scored further until half time. when Warrington led by a gnat and a try to a try. Taylor kicked off after the interval and Foster exchanged well. Dakin led away with a dribble to the Widnes line, but drennan just sa.ved in tim but a wild pass by Ffnughton gave Riapan an opportunity of get- tine' back to half way, and forward rushes took play right under the Warrington goal posts. This was I very ominous and was rendered still more so when I Thornton was severely cut over the eye and had to leave the field. linughton, however, arineciily relieved War- rinOon apnrehensinna, and his kick was followed well up by the Warrington forwards, with the reanit that a scrimmage took place within a few yards from the Wid- nes line. giggintinttnm got the hall from this scrim- mage and drooped a finegnal. Widnea tried hard to stem the tide of misfortune which was setting in against them and Rispan and Nevitt put in some useful runs. The Warrington hacks were, hnweirer,in tine forrn,and never gave their onnonenta much chance of making any head- way. liigqi.bottom was again en evidenee, and chiding his opponent, grit to the line, where passing to Dakin. the latter had hard line indeed in not scoring. Widnes now livened up a little, and by short stages they got to the opposite end. Disaster here almost befel Warring. ton, for they were penalised for off-side tactics right un- der the goal pests, but even this easy plan was not easy enough for Barker to kick a genalty goal, and only a minor resulted, From now to full time pretty even play followed. both &amp;dee missing opportunities. H. Peters had a prilden opportunity near close, for he got the ball past all opprisition, but some unaccountable fumbling eave Lawless an onportnnity of staying off the danger. minute or two from the end, Barber (Warrington) al- most got free,tant found Brennan an insuperable barrier, and when the final whistle blew. Warrington had won the game !Wile substantial majority of two goals one try at points) to one try (3 points). WARRTNGTON v. BROUGRTON RANGERS.</p>
        <p>The wijelete.voiniats of the Lancashire section were "at home " to Warrington on Monday evening'. and the match, which was not commenced until about 6.20, was witneased by 3.003 apeeatera.The field is not one which by any stretch of the imagination can be termed a grass field, for with the exeep'inn of one Small corner, there is not a blade of email to he !teen. It rejoices in the name of Wheeter's field, and only on one or two occa- sions have Warrington known defeat there. The Ran gers had been to Liversedge with a weak team on Satur- day and returned tome victorious by 22 points to five, and consequently with $l, stronger team they were confi- dent of beating Warrington on Menday.Their hopes were doomed to failure as the following account will show : —The teams were as follows:Warrington:Full baek,Hee- sem; Barber O'Brien,Burtnn and Lawlese,threequarter. ; Higgmbottom and Houghton, half-harks; Nevins, Fair- hurst, Taylor, Dakin, Saunders. Berry and Andrews,for- wards. Broughton angers:R Ward, ful: beck; Hey- ward, Berry. Barrett and Hacking, tbreeq rarters ; Mese senger and Oarrittv, half-backs ; Cleminson, Squires, Richardson, Robinson Deakin, Werthington, and Saun- derson, forwards. Mr. Boyden, of Leigh, was the ref- eree. Squires kicked off and Higginhottorn finding he could not catch the hall, kicked in touch. Clever forward rus'ies for a consider- able period caused Warrington to be hard pressed. and on several occasions Heelom just saved on the line. At length, from a scrimmage under the goal posts, Gar- rity sent the ball over the bar, and the whole of the Broughton team commenced to go to the centre. The alleged dropped goal was however promptly disallowed by the referee on an appeal being made to him,as it had been punted over, and only a minor was awarded. Im- mediately after the kick out,Garrity made his mark and Hacking essayed the goal kick, but it missed the mark and Lawless touched down. A good kick and follow tap by Higginbottom enabled Warrington to reach the centre line for the first time hut the Rangers again broke away a mark by O'Brien staving temporarily their progress. O'Brien's kick was caught in fine style by Berry, the Rangers captain, and he was only tackled by Heesom and Burton on the point of scoring ,Eechange kicking be- tween Heesom and Ward ended in favour of the former, and a grand dribble by Taylor, Berry. and Dakic took play to the Ranger's line,the latter being very unfortu- nate in not opening the scoring. The ensuing scrimmage was in favour of the home team and play was again taken to the centre, where a fine display of passing be- tween Higginbottom, Burton, and O'Brien, was moiled by the latter kicking instead of pass- ing to Barber who was waiting for the pass. For a time Warrington held the upper hand, and Lawless was tackled on the line after same good passing between Hisminhottom, Barber, and himself. G trritty and Messenger again gave their side relief,and Hayward almost ran over, Heesom, who was inlaying a fine game, just tackling in time. From now to close on half-time, Warrington had a very uncomfortable time of it, but defended wonderfully, and just before tbe whistle blew, they had reached the centre, and thus the interval ar- rived with no score, after a half hour slightly in favour of the Rangers. After the interval Warrington now made amends for the first moiety, for they pressed almost continuelly, the Rangers very rarely getting as far as the centre line. Taylorekicked off and elmest im- mediately after made his mark, which Burton, however, failed to titillate Good passing enabled Lawle.e Went</p>
        <p>to the line, but he failed to get over it. and during the next five minutes, Higginbottom,Houghton, Burton and Barber were each within al ace of scoring. At longth a cheer arose from the Warrington section of erp!ctators, when:Higginbottom getting the ball from a scrimmage near the quarter fiag,dodged past Messenger and Garrity, and running round the three quarters and full back scored a very meritorious try. which Taylor failed to raise into a goal. Squire's k;ck out was met by Tay- lor, Makin. Saunders, and Fairhurst, who had the mis- fortune of seeing the hall go in touch at the corner. The line out was utilised to advanta4e by Cleininson who sent the hall into far safer quarters, and it appeared as though for a time at least, the Rangers had got clear of danger. Great was their surprise therefore when ! Houghton passed to Burton near the centre of the field, and the latter steadying himself droppei a magnificent goal. Squires kickel off again, and Berry returned the hall well, and the Warrington forwards following up. Ward was tackled on his own goal line. Failing to play the ball he was pmalised, and Barber just missed topp- ing the Rangers goal by the place kick. Fairhurst made his mark after some even play and judiciously kicked in touch at the corner, but Warrington failed to score, and during the last five minutes Broughton made minor two dangerous spurts, which however were repelled by Heesom and Barber, and when the final whistle blew Warrington returned winners by a dropped goal and a try (seven points) to nil:</p>
      </div>
