        <p>MANCHESTER SHIP CABAL BILL.</p>
        <p>• TFIE TIDAL OPENINGS. EVIDENCE OF SIR LEADER WILLIAMS.</p>
        <p>The omnibus Bill promoted by the Manchester Ship Canal Company for .the closing of the tidal openings from the estuary of the Mersey, and for other purposes, was again under consideration on Friday last week by a aonarnittee of the House of Commons, presided over by Mr. B ake,.the other members tieing Mr. Fardell, Mr. J. Samuel, and Mr. W. H. Johnstone. The case for the promoters was continued. Sir Leader Wild woe, chief engineer of the Manchester &amp;lip Canal, said t tat after mature consideration he had come to the coin:in:don that it was necessary for the convenience of navigation of the canal that the precent tidal openings should be closed. He considered the scheme would largely benefit the canal without inflict- ing injury on any other interest. It would certainly benefir, the trade of Manchester and Warrington. Mr. Thomaq Longdin, borough engineer of Warring. ton, who extended upon a Speaker's warrant, obtained at the instance of the Mersey Docks,Board,wasexamined by Mr. Balfour Browne. -fle said he held the appoint- ment of engineer and borough surveyor at Warrington during the past 19 years, and bad made himself conver- sant with the estuary of the Mersey and the Manchester Ship Canal. It was a fact that since the opening of the canal th. tidal water level at Warrington had been re- duced by something like 14 inches.- He ascribed that to the tidal openings into the canal. The Corporation of Wlrrington had protection under the Acts of 1885 and 1890, and they preferred to exercise those provisions thein,elveq rather than tran.fer them to any other body. They were q uite able to take care of themselves. They had considred the present scheme, and had consulted with the eminent engineer, Mr. Wolff Barry, and With Mr. Carter Bell, the . county analyst of Cheshire, and the view they both arrived at, was that both from a sani- tary point of view and as re4ards the restoration of the• former tidal range the upper reaches of the estuary would te henAitectliy the scheme. Warrington was a very imp rtant town, with la population of 57,000. 'I heir se,ers were waterlogged for a period of about three hours in a 15ft tide. They had not experienced any ill effects from the waterloggin,g of the sewers, though in the ease of an exceptionally high tide some cellars were from time to dine fl. oded. But no complaints had been made upon that point.</p>
        <p>The c fllmittee adjlL-Irnel at this stage until Monday.</p>
        <p>The committee—Mr. Blake presiding—resumed on Monday the consideration of the Manchester Ship Canal Bill.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Farrington, inspector of nuisances for the Rural District Council of Runcorn, examined by Mr. Llncd,nn behalf of the District Council, said his district had a froltaqe to the estuary of about 13 miles, and the 'population was 24,663. The predecessors of the Council opposed the Ship Canal Bill of 1885, and obtained a preteeting their interests, and an agreement was drawn no The sewers draining into the canal were both ordinary sewers and for surface drainage. The Ship Canal Company had cirried out tho work provided in the iffreement,but he would not say perfectly. Elles- mere Prt hid not a proper system of sewerage, but an informal inquiry had been held, and it was anticipated that a system evotil:l soon be established. If the tide was retried to the extmt of one and a half hour it would seriously increase the waterlogging of the two principal sewers.</p>
        <p>Cross-examined by Mr. Geaham, for the promoters, wif.ness said in his opinion, if the tidal openings were closed,the canal would not he as w,ll flashed as now. He did not think the sluicing of water from Eastham to Latchford would have much eff ,ct, as the sewage from E..4ham wollthbe brought in. By Mr. Erskin Pollock, Q C.: The Runcorn urban sewers were syooned under the canal into the estuary, so that they w4uld not be affected.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Digale, surveyor of the Rural District Council of Runcorn, agreed with the last witness that to retard the ehh tide for ore and a half hour would cause increased waterloeging. It would be a source of great damage to the townships of Frodsham and Helsby.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ch tries FL Moe, civil engineer, Liverp-001, in answer to Mr. Polhck, Q.C., said he believed the retar- dation of the ebb during a heavy flood would be attended by serious results. Before the canal was made one of the principal sewers was waterlogged during certain tide by the Mersey for three hours. Since the canal had been made it was waterlogged by the corresponding ride f.,r 4i hours.and under the proposals now before the o.lmmee the waterlogging would be extended to six hours. It w4s not the mere raising of the water a few inches in the sewers that caused the damage, but the prolonged period of waterlogging,which caused deposits Mhe formed in the sewers. The'circulation of the water in the canal would not be improved by the closing of the tidal openings, hut the real effect would be to retain the sewage in the canal mid drive it backwards and forwards. F.r instance, the sewage of Ellesmere Port would be swept up the c +nal by the water entering at Eastbam and be carried beyond Runcorn. and back again. This concluded the evidence on behalf of Runcorn. Mr Freeman. Q. 0., then' called evidence on behalf of Widnes.</p>
        <p>Sir Alexander Rendall. civil engineer, said in his opin- isn the Widnes docks would ba injured by the closing of the tid 11 openings. The stg temente on behalf of the Ship Canal rompiny as to the effect upon the tide of closing the openings were purely hypothetical, and any altera- tions ought to be merely exoerimentll, so that if neces- sary the present state of things might be restored.</p>
        <p>Mr. L. B. Wells, engineer. Manchester. said the tidal openings were a decided advantacTe to Widnes as was shown by the levels of the dock sills and the position of Or? channels. The best thing for Widnes was to be let alone.</p>
        <p>Mr. Freeman said the opposition of Widnes was not factious. They believed that the proposal of the bill would wry Reriongly injure the entrance to the dock, which was vital to the existence of the town.</p>
        <p>Mr. Reader Harris, for the Marquis of Cholmondelev and 'other landowners, wan next heard. As to compen- sation, he said there might be a diffioultv,because if any disaster oneurred to the Ship Canal Company the Manche4er Corporation, who were mortgazees, might foreel,9o, and the damaged landowners might not get compensation. The Committee adjourned.</p>
        <p>A Committee of the Flouse of Commons—Mr. Blake presiding—on Tio,d2v further considered the Mane,hes- ter ship na nal Bill. with respect to that portion dealing with the prosewnt tia.l openinas Evidence was given on behalf of Lord Cholmondeley and other land- owners.</p>
        <p>Tlr case r,f the Salt Union was next opened by Mr. Freeman, Q."., who C...11Pd Mr. John Rigby, engineer tn the Union. Mr. Rigb7 said that at present the exigencies of the export salt trade were such that the Union's barges ran it aa close as ten minutes in setting into the Liyerp,•ol docks lrfPra they closed, and any interrup• of ttie present facilities would be of the greatest incon- venience.</p>
        <p>The opplaition of the Birkenhead Corporation and the Shropshire Union Canal was also heard by the Conimittee.</p>
        <p>In the cnurse nf th proceeding's it was mentioned that a cettlement had hem arrived at between the Canal Company and the Weaver trustees which was substan- tially to the effect that there shall he provided alterna- tive means of en'ranc as between Weston Marsh Lock and Weston Mer Arc Lock, with special provision for loading and unloading at the deep-water entrance to Delamere D -;ck.</p>
        <p>The Ceimmitt!.e presided over by' Mr. Blake met again on Wednesday, and rontinued the consideration of the n;11 promoted by the Manchester Ship Canal Company. Thia scla the seventh sittinn. Lard TC,lvin,who stated that hø had given evidence on various necasions on which the Ship Canal Company had peen to Parliament for the nuronse of dealing with the estuary of tho 53h4 he had lately made calculp.. Hong as to the velocities of the current which would he eAused if the tidal openings were closed. He estimated - that eighty minutes after the 14t. 21n. level was reached aed the rutting enened at Etstham the velocity through the Pool Hall cutting would be about 5,1 miles an hour. That was the mean. What about the outside velocity ? I believe that will he greater. hoegire Feveral condiions which I am not able to calculate have not heen_talten into account.</p>
        <p>What is the figureefor the 21ft, tide? About seven miles an hour. Mr. Ll'md then addressed the Committee en behalf of *assn. Rigby and Sc.na, corn merchants and millers, at Sutton, and Measra. frevwond and Massey, chemical unnucacturers, at FrAshim. His clients, he said, were upon the narural eatuary of the Weaver, and depended In it for their accean to Liverpool. The danger which they apprehended was that the closing of the tidal nneninv would increase the amnunt of silt in the canal It vumld reduce the amount of tidal flow and would retard the ebb. Tha estuary of the Weaver would afford a very handy pneket for the silt,which would he brought in at Eastham at a velocity which he was afraid to name, and there would he a loss of flow, and there- fore a loss of sconr. The result would be to injure the channel of the Weaver and the berths at his clients' works. They therefore asked for protection • and his auggeoinn was th*t the clAuse which Messrs. Rigby and San obtained in 1885 should he recapitulated, making it applicable to the proplsals of the bilLand that a similar protection should now be given to Messrs. Heywood and Massy The Committee again adjourned.</p>
