      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RUNCORN v. HALIFAX.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening these teams met at Runcorn on the Irwellel tne ground. Considerable interest was evinced in the match, which was the last but one of the engagemmts f the Halifax team, who had only to win tneir two engagements to secure the premier pesition of the Northern League. For the first few minutes play was remarkably even, though fast. A scrimmage near the quarter nag !t'ave Pendlebury his opportunity, and from his pass Warder gained considerable ground for Runcorn. SrnArt passing by the Runcorn threequarter ended in Myers looking dangerous,and his kick was only jest stopped in time. At this stage the Rencorn team were well within the twenty-five fiag,and Myers pickieg up smartly essayed a difficult drop at; goal, which just reached its destination. More fine back play in which Myers was several times conspicuous,aeain put Runcorn in the oppressors' position, and a long kick over the line Provided an exciting race, the.ball just being kicked dead by the Halifax back. After the kick- Rut Runcorn again returned to the attack,a,nd Robinson Butterworth anti Myers were cheered , for brilliant passing. A long kick by Keeping's took play into the Runcorn half, where Warder was collared in possession. Por some' few minutes through the exertion of the for- wards, Halifax kept play at the Runcorn 25, but Faulk- ner from a SCllintillge on that line kicked to the centre. Gareide being tackled, dropped the ball, and the home forwards just missed scoring from the dribble. The Halifax forwards by taking a home pass rushed danger- ously into the home line, and were stopped by a daring save by Warburton. 114 f-time was called with Run- corn, 1 goal (dropped), Halifax. nil. Runcorn restarted and Hali?ax playing with determination quickly reached Runcorn danger line, where scrimmage after scrimmage ensued. Relief was afforded by Houghton who kicked well past the half war flag, and shortly afterwards Halifax were penalised. following the kick came a scrimmage, and from the pass out by the Runcorn half back Warder punted to touch. Halifax again attacked but were penalized on the 25 line. After exciting play •in the home half, Jackson through clever passing sent the ball over the line and the forwards following up enabled Halifax to record a try in an easy position which was quickly converted. For the remaining few minutes play reached fever heat, .and Halifax were forced to defend. Several times the home team were very near scoring but by good defensive play were kept out. At the call of time the Halifax men were responsible for one geal(s pointe),against their opponents' dropped goal (4 points). WIDNES V. MANNINGEIAIII.</p>
        <p>Played at Widnes on Tuesday evening, before 3,000 speetators. Widnes had a full team with the ex,eption of H. Peters, but the visitors were short of Brown, Pickles,Williamaon,Lambert,and Wilson. The Yorkshire- men started, ard in two minutes Newton scored,Lorimer failinr, at the place kick. A moment later,VVidnes hav- ing made a temporary spurt, Barraclough ran halt thel I.mgth of the field and scored a try. L)rimer took the place and sent the ball over the bar, but Foster touched it in passing, and thus nullifif-d the goal. Rispan ran ' splendidly for Widnes, but fell when clear of his °poen- ents. Barraclough returned to the Widnes quarter, and passed to Clegg, who scored, Lorimer agaia failing to improve. Brennan got away to the Manningham quar- ter and WI Ines attacked strongly. Needham relieved with a smart run to mid-field, and 'working well toge- ther, Manningham forced Widnes back on their line. Barraclnugh passed neatly to Newton, who scored under the posts, Barraclough landing a goal. Widnes again reached their opponents' territory, but their faulty tac- tics lost openings, and the visitors defended splendidly and cleared their line. Another penalty kirk to Widnes at the Manningham quarter was fruitless, and half-'ime arrived with the score—Manningham, 1 goal and 3 tries (14 points), Widnes nil. Clegg was injured just on the interval, but was able to resume. Widnes forced play on restarting, and Drummond tried a shot at goal from a free kick, but without avail. Shortly afterwards Alford dropped, and the ball hit the crossbar and re- bounded into plAy; Widnes continued to press, and Johnson secured a try at the eqrner,but he failed to land a goal, For the remainder of the game Widnes were pressing,Manningham appearing content to defend their line, which they did with marked ability. Time after time Whine swooped down on them,only to be repulsed, and the game cloaed with a victory for Manningham by 1 goal and 3 tries (14 points) to 1 try (3 points).</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A " 1. WIDNFS "A."—The return match he'ween these clubs was played at Wilderspool on Saturday. The result of the match decided the Lan- cashire "A " team championship, and Warrington by losing has failed to secure the medals. The following were the trams :—Widnes : Full back. Grayson; three quarters.flampson, Taylor, Percival. Alford; half backs, Murphy, Douglas; forwards, Travis, Aapey, Murray, Peters, McCabe, Ford, Grace, E. Derbyshire. Warring- ton "A " : Full back. Ashurst ; threequarters, Scott, O'Brien, Isherwood, Foster; half backs, Clarke, Foden; forwards : Bailey, Wilcock, Freeth, Sudlow, Savage, Hughes, Rorie, Scholtze. Widnes kicked off, Hughes made his mark and the ball was sent . into the vivito&amp; territory. Several scrimmages took place ha one position until Clarke got off side and oas penalised, Mine!' entering Warrington quarters. From aflying kick by Tgherwood,W arrington were almost let in, Grayson failing to return. In a good position Warrington had a free kick which Foden faill d to utilize. The visitors then pressed and kicking the ball up &amp;Ad, Asteirst mulled and they secured a position close to goal. Passing along the three-quarters enabled Hampson to score and afterwards place a goal. This reverse did not waken up the homesters and more combined passing by Widnes eventn ly reached Percival ,and that player made a good run to the corner but was knocked into touch. fulf.time arrived with the score, Widnes 1 goal, War- rinetnn nil. On the restart Clarke kicked off and the ball was returned. After more desultory play the homes- ters commenced to press and Isherwood got over the line, hut the pass vras ruled forward. From this point Widnes were kept hard at work defending, which they did well. Twa free-kicks were granted Warrington from one of which Foden placed a penalty !!nal. A brief visit to Warrington q u rte!ta was made by Widnes but nothing came of it and maintaining their good defence they retired winners of the medals by 1 goal (5 points) to 1 penalty goal (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>FRODSRAM v. RIINCORN..—TiIi4 match wag played at Frodsham on We Inesday evening in very pleasant weather, this 'being the third time the teams had met this season ; the first game at Runcorn ended in a draw three earb ; the return at Frndsham ended in a decisive win for the home team by 6 goals to nil. So the visitors Cattle with the hopes of a victory as they hal the help of three Wirbv.s Parish Church players. The home team included the brothers Connor and Carter of Warrington. The home Captain having won the toss, the visitors started the game down hill and pressed for a few mom% ents, but E. Connor removed clay to the centre. Give and take play was the oder of the game for a length of time, until Browne got the ball and passed to B ,oth that player centring for Tomalin to score the first goal for the home team. Very soon after Stdby scored a second from a pass by Booth. Half time arrived with Frodsham two coals. linneorn nil. C. Linaker restarted the game for thehnmestera. an they had matters nearly all their own way Broome, 1. 0. Linaker. 1. Tomalin 2. scored goals for the home team the final result being Frodsham six goals, Runcorn nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>ST. THOMAS'S YOUNG MEN'S CLASS.—On SUIVIIar a fter- noon. Mr. Rthinson, of Fall River, United States of America. a eentleman who has had great experience of Sunday school work,both in this country and in America, addressed the members of the above class on "Sunday schools, past, present, and future." He showed that instead of them being" worked out," as some said,they had a great future before them, and were becoming more important every day. He impressed upon all the necessity of doing their best for their class and school and so help on the work of the parish. Afterwards Mr. Hallows, on behalf of Mrs. Hayes, presented every mem- ber, of whom there were 84 present, with a book on Church history being an account of the Church from its earliest days down to the present time. Mr. Robin said that as a dam they ought not to let such a kindness pass without showing their approval of it. I fie remembered Mrs. Hayes well, and the good work done in the parish by her husband ,their late Vicar.herself.and her sister—work that would never die. He proposed a vote of thanks to her for her generosity on this and other occasions. This was seconded by Mr. James, who thoroughly endorsed all the remarks made by Mr. Robin, and carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
