      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <p>Eaters to tin Obitor.</p>
        <p>AN APPEAL.</p>
        <p>Sir.—Will you allow me to appeal on behalf of two old Warringtonians who are now in distress in London? The man was educated at the Grammar School and sub- sequently was in Messrs. Rylands' employ. Nearly 30 years ago he came with his wife to London, where he found fresh employment until he Iy-came paralysed nine years agossince when he has only been able to do a little caretaking, for which he is no longer fit. His savings are spent,and as the wife is not strong and they have no children, they are dependent on charity. They enjoy a small pension from their church, and my Committee have decided to try to raise an allowance to supplement this,land to save them from falling upon the parish. A sum of four shillings a week is still required, to enable the couple to live in decent comfort, and I hope that some of your readers will be willing to send donations or small weelry contributions towards what is required. I shall be happy to give furtlrr particulars to the in- quirers.—Yours obediently, HERMAN W. MARCUS, Hon. Secretary.</p>
        <p>Lambeth Committee, Charity Orginization Soci-ety, 35, Lambeth Patace-road,London, S.E.,l4ch April, 1896.</p>
        <p>A LETTER OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>Sir.—Will you kindly allow me a little space in your widely read j iurnal to thank publicly all those ladies and gentlemen from the districts included in my stall, 4* No. 1 Newton-le-Willows and District," who so very gener- ously supported me either by gifts of money or goods or their personal assistance last week at the large Diocesan Bazaar for waifs and strays in Liverpool. It is impos- sible for me to thank each one personally but I wish to say how very pleased and gratified I feel at their united efforts to make No. 1 stall the success it was. There are a few more small sums to come in, as also a few more accounts to be paid but the money actually paid in by my hon. treasurer, Captain Royse, R.N., was £259 Us. 2d.. and we hope to pay off all stall expenses by subsequent sales of surplus goods to the supporters and promoters of two or three impending local sales of work. Any inquiries respecting this may be addressed to the Local Hon. Secretary, Mrs. Clare Rlyse, Church Hill House, Newton-le-Willows.—l remain, yours faith- fully,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>MARY L. GERARD. Garswood, Nowton-le-Willows, Lancs. April 19, 1896. .</head>
        <p>MARY L. GERARD. Garswood, Nowton-le-Willows, Lancs. April 19, 1896. .</p>
        <p>THE V,' PERS IN CYPRUS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Over the signature of "Robert Pierpoint " there is a precious bit of literature upon this subject in the " Examiner " of April 11th. As the document is dated from the House of Commons I presume the writer is the member for the Tory section of this community. If I am right in this surmise kindly permit me to express my astonishment that the said member should have passed over the solid phalanx of rich, Christian corn- patriots with whom he is in daily contact and sent his begging letter to Warrington. There are son os curiosi- ties in the composition, but I have only time to notice one of them. The plea put forth for a church for the lepers at Nicosia is the most remarkable thing of the kind I have ever met with. Here it is in the member's own words: "Is it strange that people so grievously affi.cted, who are by nature and training religious, are filled with a desire to have a church instead of a room to worship in, which those who have their sight can see and describe to those who are blind." But the begging letter of the Tories' member has fa.'len upon Warrington in an evil time. The Parish Church of the old town, which he loves so much,is in debt, if not in danger. In- stead of begging contributions for a place so far away as Cyprus let Mr. Pierpoint try his hand at getting up a big list for Mr. Willis and his wardens, and thus dm some- thing to prevent the former from having recourse to the drastic method of starving the poor and sick fund of the church in order to reduce the adverse balance on the accounts. In conelusion, let me humbly suggest to your corres- pondent the desirability of changing his tune, and treat- ing us o a little variety. We have had enough of Cyprus for the present. There is, unfortunately, much poverty and misery in his native town. What would Mr. Pier- point say, to taking up his parable against the drink traffic with a view to lessen the drunkenness which now Hoods the town with pauperism and disorder? It would be a far nobler work than begging doles for ecclesiastical buildings.—Yours truly, lONA.</p>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COU.CIL.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEEHNG.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Eirle.r.own, on monday afternoon the annual meeting of the Newton-in-MAketneld Urban District Council was held. There were present :—Rev. E. W. W. Kaye, Messrs. C. B. F. Borron, W. Coiling- wood, J. Simpson, W. Thompson, jun., T. Ormand, P. McMahon, J. W. Emmett, F. J. Wood, R. Stone, E. T. Evans, Beatty, Slee. Gilmour, and J. E. Worsley (law clerk). R. Brierley, (survevor).and Walter William Shir- ley (clerk to the Council).—At the outset, Mr. Coiling- wood was voted to the chair on the proposition of Mr. Borron, seconded by Mr. Ormond.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>SHARES OF EXPLORATION AND DE</head>
        <p>SHARES OF EXPLORATION AND DE</p>
        <p>VELOPMENT COMPANIES.</p>
        <p>If we examine the London Stock Exchange list of quotations of mining sharesove shall find that the shares of these companies which are devoted to the exploration and development of lands or gold mines stand at far higher prices than those of mining companies proper. The securities of these companies are at an immense premium, as also are nearly every one of the land and exploration enterprises whose operations are carried on in either South Africa er West Australia. Having regard to our limited space, it is not possible to enumerate them all. We may, however, mention a few of the West Australian Companies, most of which have been established within the last eighteen months, and now show a very large increase in value, having regard to the initial prices of their shares. Venture Syndi- cate shares, which now stand at En, became actively dealt in about six months since; Hampton Plains was regiftered a year ago last October—its shares are now qoJted at vi West Australian General Association was floated abo .t the same time and its shares are only pur- chasable at oi; Wept Australian Goldfields shares are at present quoted at £7l. Every one of the above shares is of the par value of £l. each. But these are not all the securities of this nature which are now standing at a heav,. premium on the London Etock Exchange. A few months ago a Company, entitled "The West Australian Gold Properties Development, Limited." was brought into existence, and its prosperity and success are now so -well assured that it is right we should draw attention to the low price, £.2 50., per share, at which its fully-paid shares are now selling. The Company has secured the right to prospect for gold and all o.;her minerals, and to purchase no less an area than 76,000 acres,freehold. in one of the best kn' wn and most important parts of West Australia. It should be noted that this great concession secures to the West Australian Gold Properties Development, Limited, the ppiviiege to acquire for mineral purposes freeholds for 76,000 acres,with valuable timber and water rights over the whole of the property, and all the timber thereon. Such rights as these may he formulated, hut it is im- possible to anticipate the immense profits to be reapod from a field of enterprise, which is practically inexhaus- tible. North and South of this vast tract of land,whie,h is well watered, well wooded, and has a perfect climate for residential and agricultural purposes, is found gold in large quantities. The Board of Directors, while emi- nently conservative and cautious, has been very enter- pris'ng.and already the West Australian Gold Properties Development. Limited, whose affairs have been con- ducted with singular ability, has grown to an important institution. The Company is interested in a large num- ber of gold properties, situated in the Menzies, Cool- gardie, and Flannan's districts of West Australia. The Company is furthermore interested in many other under- takings, more or less important, most of which are of an eminentiv lucrative character. The acquisitions of the Company are exhibiting a magnitude never in the first instance anticipated, and they are now in a position to reap the great advantag,s and profits arising from subsidiary companies formed to work portions of their large estates. If we investi- gate the merits of the various Land and Exploration Companies, and take into consideration the prices of their respective shares, we shall fled there is greater 'm for a big rise in the price of the shares of the West Australian Gold Properties Development, Limited, than in those of any other company whose business is on the same lines. The best advice that VIP have to give to investors and speculators is to buy s West Australian Gold Properties Development. Limited, shares, fully paid, at the present low price of £2 ss. per shareiat which they are now selling on the London Stock Exchange, as, now that large profits and good dividends are assured, there is every probability of their very shortly improving to many times their present price.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>RUNCORN POLICE COURT. TICIIRSDIY.—Berore Mr. 8. Tayior.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN POLICE COURT. TICIIRSDIY.—Berore Mr. 8. Tayior.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED HousliaßE &amp;MSG.—John Thorns, (hoes. an army pensioler. was charged with being found in possession o ce-tain implements for breaking into the dwelling-house of Hewv 'Hardier in Chaucer-street. on April 22n4.—Henev Hard'ey. rhaucer-street. employed at Bridgewater lock, said he was in bed about 2.30 in the afternoon when he heard someone try the back door. On getting downstairs he saw a• 1 ax hid been driven into the door and looking through the window he saw the prisoner who on seeing him .• bolted." Inspector Okell 'asked for a remand until Satur- day ani .nen'ioned that another house was broken into the same lay. —The remand was granted. ALL KGRD Wiyz ASUULT.—Thornas Gandy was eivoled with aasaelPinq his wife llen Gandy %.7 striking her on the head with a" jockey "bar on April 22nd. When Gan ly said her husband was a hoatman and I val. witik ksr in Grindrod- square. On Wednesday afternoon she saw her husband drunk She had been in the neighbourhood of King-street and Qneen-street and knowing his temper she went to the Police station for protection. She went home accompanied by an officer and on opening the door she found her husb^nd stan iinz behind it. He seized her by the head and dragged her ab nit in a shamefil manner but the constable ordered him to leave her alone and her husband promised to do so. The 'ffi.'r had scarcely left the house when .her husband be- gan to threaten her ant then picked up a peke., bar with vs-'which la struck her over the head causing a severe wound which bled profusely.—This case was also remanded until Saturday.</p>
        <p>The Marquis of Londonderry has doled two large pits in the county of Durham owing to the heavy logs entailowl in working them urder present conditions. A third pit in tho same ownership is also to be closed.</p>
        <p>EVERY CYCLTST ahnuld nes JOTIN S. GREEN'S NEW Ft OAD MAP,6O miles about WarringLon.l3.6d. nett, A 34, Bridge-at. and 23, Buttermarket-st.-4dvt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>CHAIRMANSHIP.</head>
        <p>THE CHAIRMANSHIP.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stone said he had much pleasure in proposing that Mr. Barron be the Chairman. He was sure every mem- ber ot the Council must feel that during Mr. Borron's last twelve months of office he filled the position of chair- man with dignity and ability. He had devoted a:1 his best energies in furthering the best interests of the town- ship, and he (Mr. Stone) had now great pleasure in pro- posing his re-election.—Mr. Tnompscia seconded the proposition. Mr. Emmett proposed an amendment that Mr. Ormand be the chairman. He said Mr. Orman,' was most fitted for that position. He was an oil Commissioner and understood the business of the Council quite as well, if not better, than most of the gentlemen sitting round that table. He (Mr. Emmett) did not ccncur with what had been said about Mr. Borron being a perfect chair- man. That gentleman had failed in his duty, and he (Mr. Emmett) did not go behind his back to tell him so. —Mr. Beatty seconded the amendment.—Mr. Gilmour said he had considered the question of chairmanship very fully, and he quite endorsod what had been said in favour of the election of Mr. Ormand as chairman. He ' considered that workingmen should occupy that position. He hadl known Mr. Ormand for many years and he thought he was a fit and proper person to occupy the position of chairman. He objected to Mr. Borron because it was an impossibility for an interested party to he fair and partial at their meetings.—Mr. Scone : "Question !"—lf they were going to make it a party question the Council could not be - a harmonious body. Mr. Herron was already a justice of the peace, and he thought someone else should have an opportunity of attaining to that honourable position.— Mr. McMahon eaid he had great pleasure in supporting the amendment. Mr. Ormand had done great service aq chairman of the Finance Committee and he had been very successful in what he had undertaken. No one could say anything against Mr. Ormand. He had been a pioneer in the work of the old Board of Commissioners and had done more than anyone else on the Council. On the amendment being put to the vote, four voted for it, viz. : Messrs. Gilmour, McMahon, Emmett, and , Beatty. For the proposition there voted :—Rev. E. W W. Kaye, Messrs. Simpson, St ,ne, Wo:,d, Thomuson, and Sloe (61.—Mr, Herron on taking the chair, thanked the members for again elect- ing him to that position. He would like to say that no one in that room could be a perfect chairman. Be did not pretend to be so, but he knew his own feelings on the matter thormaehly.fle had tried to be lenient with the menabersand to guide them as calmly and quietly as he could.knowing that it was the first year of theC mncil.le the forthcoming year, from their experience and know- ledge in public affairs, he hoped they would conduct the business without jealousy, envy, or malice of one an- other, so that the best interests of the district might be served. (Hear, hear.) He hoped to conduct the busi- ness in that spilt, and he should endeavour to smooth any irritation as far as he possibly could.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>VICE- CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>THE VICE- CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wood proposed that Mr. Collingwood be the vice- chairman.—Rev. E. W. W. Kaye seconded the prop,isi- tion, and said it was very wise to give Mr. Collingwood another trial. Last year was a very difficult year, and he thought this coming year things wculd go on more smoothly.—Mr. Gilmour took exception to the last re- marks. Last year was a very easy one.—The resolution was carried.</p>
        <p>THE COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour said he was of the opinion that all mem- bers of the Council should be on all committees. SPate- ments had befn made during the election with reference to the %mount of work which would have been done if it had not been for obstructionists. He could not under- stand how a gentleman connected with the maj-iritv of the Council could get on a platform, and state that more would have been done if it had not been for the minority. It was not obstruction that was the cause, it was the in- ability of the majority of the members to do the work. He thought they would do better if all the members com- prised the committees. There were many things which sul4-committees had been doing, of v hich</p>
        <p>many members knew nothing about. Ile wished to know what street making had been done durinc, the pad six months. There was also the ques- tion of sanitary conveniences, etc. Sub-committees had also been appointed to look after night soil and the water question, but what had they done? He moved that every member of the Council be on the committees. —Mr. Collingwood seconded.—Mr. Evans thought the members tried to do too much work at one sitting. It would be better if they met once a tortnight instead of once a month. He did not approve of the way they had been going on. There had been very little done. There were too many on the committees and they could not get on with the work.—Mr. McMahon said according to the last speaker, the more hands you had, the less they did. 11. thought the fault lay in many of the hands knowing nothing about the work. Instead of the Coun- cil working together there had been a great deal of hole and corner business. The speaker severely criticised the actions of the Water Committee,and state] that the Coun- cil had nothing to lose by interviewing Mr. Webb.—The resolution on being put to the meeting was carried.</p>
        <p>THE QUESTION OF EVENING MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour suggested that the Council should have evening meetings, and stated that he had never been more convinced of the necessity of evening meetings than at the present time, especially after the statements of candidates at the recent election. He thought it only right and reasonable that the workingmen should be able to attend and take some interest in the meetings. He wished to contradict the statement which had been made that the Council sat from three o'clock until eleven. He moved that the Council meetings in future be held at six o'clock in the evening on the first Monday in the menth. —Mr. Beatty seconded the proposition. He thought it only right that the working-classes should have an opportunity of attending the meetings of the Council. They could not do so when they were held at four o'clock —Mr. McMahon supported the proposition,but he would rather that the meetings be held at seven o'clock. He would he glad if the proposition could be altered.— Mr. Gilmour and Mr. Beatty consentei to the altera- tion from six to seven.—Mr. John Simpson moved an amendment that the Council meet at four o'clork as before. The evening meetings had been given a trial and they were found to be a failure. The members of the Connell did not appear to get on with the business very quickly, and if that meeting was a criterion for future meetings, he (Mr. Simpson) did not think they were going to do much in the future.—Mr. Themnson seconded the arnendment,—Mr. Gilmour said the Coun- cil could get through the business in three-quarters of an hour regularly. The work was all cut and dried hr- fore it cam- before the Council.—The Chairman said he was very glad that previous meetings had been got over qeickly. He had been -charged with ru: hing matters by some particular members of the Council.—On a vote being taken, seven voted for t:te amendment. (The Chairman, Rev. E. W. W. Kaye, Messrs. Sinapson.Wood, Stone, Thompson, See), and seven against (Messrs. McMahon' Emmett, Collingwond, Ormand. Evans and --- Beatty).Mr. Gilmour The Chairman has voted once. —The Chairman: I shall give my casting vote in favour of the amendment.—Mr. Gilmour : As mover—The Chait man: I wish you would not make any interruption. —Mr. Gilmour: As mover of the amendment —The Chairman: You have no right to !peak when the chairman is speaking. I call upon you to sit down. I shall be much obliged for you to sit do n.—Mr. Gil- mour then took his seat, but not without stating that it was an injustice to the working classes.—The Chairman said the reason he gave his casting vote in favour of the amendment was so that there should be no alteration. He was not in favour of it.</p>
        <p>THE TIME OF THE COMMITTEE MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Simpson moved that the committee meetings be held as before with the alteration of the time from 4 to 3.30.—Mr. Collingwood seconded.—Mr. Gilmour sug- gested that committee meetings be held at two o'clock, and moved a resolution to that effect. He stated that there was partyism in the Council from beginning to end. On being interrupted he asked the Chairman to keep order.--The Chairman: I will endeavour to do so.— Mr. Gilmour said whenever he had spoken he had been interrupted by some roughs at the bottom end of the table. Continuing, he said it was in the interests of the public that he moved that the Council meet earler when in committee. He contended that some of the members showed an inability to conduet business, and he wanted to teach some of the gentlemen present how to do business as workingmen representa- tivee.—On being again interrupted, Mr. Gilmour asked the Chairman if he would speak to the. roughs at the bottom end.—Mr. Simpson rose to a plint of order.— Mr. Gilmour said he was on his feet and was not goilg to be interrupted.—The Chairman said he must inform Mr. Gilmour that he (the Chairman) was conducting the meeting, and that Mr. Simpson had risen on a point of order.—Mr. Simpson contended that it was not right to call anyone a rough. It was out of order entirely.—Mr Gilmour: Have von finished sir ?—The Chairman: I rule what has been stated, especially those personalities, out of order.— Mr. Beatty said he did not think there should be any laughing or sneering at the meetings. He did not think it was gentlemanly.—Mr. Gilmour said he had seen the Chairman put his band to his mouth and sneer. He contended that anyone who interrupted him was un- gentlemanly. He wanted fair play and justice.—Mr. McMahon seconded Mr. Gilmour's amendment. He thought it would be better if the meetings were held at two o'clock. Where was the sense of letting a working- man like Mr. Gilmour come from his work after an hzur's labour to attend to their meetings,and afterwards to go back again? Let the members give the working- men a hit of privilege in the matter.—On being put to the vote the amendment was lost by six votes to eight.</p>
        <p>THE ORDER OF ROSINESS.</p>
        <p>In decidinz upon the order of the committeeq. ilmour moved that the Cemetery, School Attendance</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, APRIL 25, 1896.</p>
        <p>and Finance, should be the first three committees. He said when he got up to speak there was sure to be an uproar.—Mr. Stone said he was conferring with Mr. Wood as to the advisability of carrying out the sugges- tion. That was the uproar. He seconded the proposi- tion.—Mr. Collingwood said that it was impossible for them to have the Finance Committee before the others. —Mr. Ormancl spoke of the great attention paid by the Finance Committce to financial matters. Mr. Gilmour seemed to be casting a slur upon the committee.—The Chairman said so far as he had seen the finance accounts had been most carefully attended to.—Mr. Stone said he had second, d Mr. Gilmour's proposition under a misap- prehension. He wished to withdraw.—The Chairman said the major part of the afternoon had been taken up by Mr. Gilmour in irregularities.—Mr. Gilmour said he was very sorry if he had done so. The meeting then terminated.</p>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARy (EBENEZER LODGE.) The Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge held its weekly session on Tuesday evening in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown. Bro. A. Morris, C.T., presided and opened the meeting. There was a good attend- ance of members and visitors,including 32 members of Ashton Excelsior Lodge who gave the following programme ;—Melody by the members, " I love the cause of Temperance " ; address, Bro. Speakman; solo, Bro. R, IV hittle ; recitation, Bro. Chesworth ; solo, Sister Whittle.; recitation, Bro. Bowler; solo, Bro. Jackson; recitation, Bro. 11. Vint; solo, Bro. Duckworth ; address, Bro. Hulme ; solo, Bro. Ches- ' worth ; recitation, Bro. Speakman; glee, Bro. Whittle ; solo, Bro. Smith ; recitation, Bro. Fallon. A resolution in favour of Scotch veto Bill and Sun- day Closing was unanimously adopted. Refresh- ments were then partaken of. Thanks to .visitors brought the meeting to a close. CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The anniversary services in support of the Sun- day schools connected with the above place of wor- ship, were held in the church on Sunday. The pul- pit was occupied morning and evening by Prof. E. A. Armitage, of Yorkshire United College,Bradford, who gave a powerful exposition of the Gospel on each occasion before large congregations. At the morning service the choir sang the anthem "I will magnify Thee," and in the evening the solo was finely rendered by Miss M. Thompson. In the afternoon a musical service was given by the choir and scholars, when the following programme was ,capitally executed. Chorus, "Lo ! summer comes again," choir; chorus, "How sweet is the bible" children; song, "Calvary." Miss L. Randall; organ solo, 'Triumphal March," Mr. W. H. -Lhompsou ; anthem, "0. ye that love the Lord," choir; song "Jerusalem." Mr. Robert Owen ; chorus, " The world looks very beautiful," children; organ sol,o, " Ber- cense in D. Major," Mr. W. H. Thonipsou; 'solo, "0 rest in the Lord," Miss Mather; chorus, " Awake, thou that steepest,' choir. The collectioiii at the three services amounted to £24 is. BUFFALO CONCERT AT EARLRSTOWN.</p>
        <p>in connection with the No. 1. Hesketh lodge, Earlestown of the Order of Buffalo's, a smoking concert was given at the headquarters,Griffin hotel, Earlestown, on Tuesday evening. Bro. W. Pownall P. B. from N 0.2, Harinonic lodge,Haydock, presided and there was a large attendance of members, and visitors from the Haydock lodge. The concert was most enjoyable, the following programme being renderi,d :—Overture by the Banjo band; comic song, Mr. J. Eaton; "Told in the twi- light," Mr. J. Gibbons; piccolo solo, Mr. W. Pearson ; song, " Love's old sweet song," Mr. L. Bullock; banjo duet, Messrs. Eaton and Wickham; song "Parody on Little Alabama epon," Mr. Boughey ; song," Goodwin Sands," Mr. J. Richards.4n ; violin solo, Mr. Broadhurst ; song, "There's a flower that bloometh," Mr. J. Gibbons; song, "Laughing song," Mr. J. Eaton; song, "Gil- ligan," Mr. A. Massey ; song, "The man that came over from Ireland," Mr. J. Gibbons; selection by banjo ban ; song, " My Sweetheart when a bay," Mr. L. Bullock; song, "The little tin soldier," Dr. Rossiter, ijaydock BENEFIT CONCERT AT EARLES/OWN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening the Town Hall was well filled with an appreciative au Hence in support of a concert given for the benefit of Miss J. E. Roberts, a local singer. Miss Roberts has on many occasions willingly assisted various charities, and it has been decided to show appreciation of ber services by providing a fund to enable her to take lessons from a singing tutor. Several local artistes gave their services, and a very successful concert resulted. The programme was well chosen, those who took part being Madame Katie Williams, of Liverpool, who sang" Dear heart," and "Spring," and was re- called on each occasion. Mr. G. Kidd, organist at Burtonwood Church, who gave "King's Own," and "The Romany lass," his efforts being warmly ap- preciated ; Mr. J. Higham, who sang " The bugler," and "Vanderdecken," in his usual clever style; Mr. J. Howard, the local siffieuse, who whistled "Kil- larney," and another,and was vociferously encored; Mr. F. Mather, who efficiently executed a couple of violin solos; Miss Davies, who submitted "The songs the children sing." and "Miserere Domino," to the satisfaction of the audience, by whom she was loudly applauded; and Miss Roberts, who gave "The flight of Ages," and "Tell me, my heart." in a very careful manner. The humour was contributed by Mr. J. Ainsworth, and Miss Sudworth efficiently accompanied the singers. NEWTON DIVISION BOWLING ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening at the Pied Bull Hotel, Newton, a meeting of the representatives of the clubs comprised in the above Association was held, for the purpose of receiving at the hands of the Hon. T. W. Legh, M.P., the silver challenge cup,pre- seated by that gentleman for competition by the clubs. The cup has during the past season been won by the Nor*.h Ashton bowling club. Mr. T. Crompton, jun., presided over the proceedings, and besides the delegates there were present the Hon. r. W. Legh, M.P., Mr. Robert Stone. J.P., and Mr. J. Millington (secretary). The Chairman said he was pleased to note that Burtonwood had rejoined the Association, but he was sorry Garswood Hall and Perk Ltine had not seen their way to join.—The Hon. T. W. Lt.gh afterwards presented the cup to Mr. W. Welsby on behalf of the North Ashton club. The cup bore the inscription "Challenge cup pre- sented to the Recreation Bowling Clubs in the Newton Division by the Hon. T. W. LBO, M P., 1895." Mr. Legh next handed to Mr. F. Arnott, a set of bowls and jack, in bag, on behalf of the Gol- borne Cricket and Bowling Club, presented by Mr. Ralph Smith.—Thanks to Mr. Legh were accorded with acclamation. ORDER OF THE SONS OF TEMPERANCe.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Earlestown Grand Division was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earles- town on Saturday. There was a full attendance of delegates from Widnes, St. Helens, and Haydock. G.W.P. Burrows opened the proceedings with a careful review of the work of the year,and in a few well chosen words sounded the call to duty for the year 1896. The statement of the G.S. showed the Order to be steadily progressing, both numerically and financially, though slowly, yet surely. St. Helens reported greatest bet gain in members. The funds for slck and funeral purposes amount to nearly £9OO. The receipts for the year were £330 of which sum £229 had been paid away in sick and accident claims. The sickness had been much heavier than usual, 87 having been on the funds. There had been no deatbi during the year. The G.D. valuation had been made and with the exception of the old uniform members had proved very satis- factory. Average age of members 29 years. The following officers were elected to serve for 1896: G.W.P. William Wheatley, G.W.A. E. Anderton, G. Con Alex. Chalmers, G. Sent. T. Harrison, G. Chap. 0. Pickell, G.f. William Clare, P.G.W.P. William Burrows, G. Patron J. Judson, G.S. William Pearson.</p>
        <p>GREA.T SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of this newly-elected Council was held on Monday evening. There were present : Messrs. G. Thompson, T. Halton,H. Bate, Thomas kilshaw, Wm. Taylor, W. H. L. Gorse, J. Wright (clerk),and G. Clarke (schoolmaster). Mr. Halton raised the question of the postponement of the meeting. He said Mr. Thompson, Mr. Bate and himself were the only members present at the previous meeting of the expiring Council, and they unanimously decided that the next meeting should be held on Thurs- day last. He would now like to know if they were in order, or was this a legitimate meeting. Should they not, in duty bound, have attended the meeting called for *thursday night ? The (Chairman) Councillor Skelton said he had already explained the circumstances to Mr. Halton. Is was no part of the duty of on expiring Council to dictate or ar- range the meeting of an incoming Council. He had received the printed instructions issued by the Local Government Board—the highest authority on this matter existing—and it said that the duty of calling together a new Council devolved upon the Chairman of the late Council, the only time when anyone else could interfere being when the Chairman should fail to obey the orders of the Local Government Board bearing on the mater. —Mr. Halton: I presume you have finished, Mr. Chai- rman ? I beg to differ from you, and feel sure that in your absence any two members of the expiring Council may call a meeting of the new Council.—Mr. Thomp- son : That cannot be done by resolution at the meeting of an expiring Council.—The Chairman: It is a matter scarcely worth discussing.—Mr. Halton asked the Clerk for his ruling.—Tne Chairman : I believe, Mr. Halton, all questions should be addressed through the chair, and I object to this kind of thing taking place. I have shown you how matters stand, and I think any intelli- gent man should thoroughly understand.—Mr. Halton : You don't hold the position of chairman at the present time, Mr. Skelton, and why should I put my questions through you ?—The Chairman: I beg your pardon, sir, I am chairman here until the election of my suc- cessor. Continuing, he said Mr. Halton nad had the question of calling the meeting of the new Council ex- plained to him, and in a manner which any intelligent mind could grasp. Yet he came to the Coun- cil meeting and wanted everything explaining again.— Mr. Halton : I beg your pardon, it has not been fully explained to me. As to the using of the word "intelli- gent," I think you might have left that out.—The Chair- man: I have acted within :my powers as chairman of the expiring Council.—Mr. Thompson : Reallv,gentla- men, I hope you will allow this friction to cease.—Mr. Halton: lam sorry it has occurred.—The Chairman : I did not alter the date of the meeting in any antagonis- tic feeling, but simply because it clashed with another important meeting in this township.—The discussion than dropped, and on the motion of Mr. Thompson, se- conded by air. Gorse, and supported by Mr. Halton, a vote of thanks was accorded the Chairman for his ser- vices during the past year.—ln acknowledging the vote Mr. Skelton said he had always done his best for the welfare of the township. He explained that the reason he wae not present at the Council meeting was that an important meeting of the Warrington Town Council was held the same evening, and be was obliged to attend it. I —Mr. Skelton then left the chair, which was filled by I Mr. Bate.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thovapson proposed that Mr. Skelton be re-elected chairman for the coming year. They would remember that last year they thought the number of members was rather small, and to increase it they had to come to some arrangement by which Mr. Skelton should stand on one side, on condition that after the election he should be elected their chairman. That arrangement he took it had been renew el this year.—Mr. Gorse seconded.—Mr. Kilshaw moved an amendment to the effect that Mr. Bate be chairman.—Mr. Taylor seconded.—Mr. Halton, in supporting it, said he believed in the old theory of having a fresh chairman every year. Young members should be given an opportunity of coming to the front.- -Mr. Thompson hoped Mr. Bate would decline the honour which had been offered to him. Mr. Bate, he thought, knew what was inteeded to be done from the first, and if any alteration had been made in the arrange- ment with regard to the election of chairman it was only right that Mr. Skelton should have been made aware of it, so that he might have come out as a candidate. Toe course which had been adopted was ungentlemanly and dishonourable in the extreme.—Mr. Halton thought Mr. Rate had a perfect right to be made chairman, and he hoped be would accept the honour. He had occupied the posts of waywarden and overseer for a Lumber of years, and it was by no means dishonourable for him now to occupy the post of chairman.—Mr. Thompion : It was the duty of every one here to have given Mr. Skelton proper and honourable intimation of the nature of their intentions, and not deprive Mr. Skelton of a seat on the Council.—Mr. Skelton asked if he bad a right of reply.—Mr. Halton said that he had not. He had no eght in the room.—Mr. Skelton: I am not speaking to you, sir. lam speaking to the Chairman, and from the Chairman I will take the answer.—The Chairman: We cannot allow Mr. Skelton to speak on the present topic. —The Clerk: lam of the same opinion. The business now is to elect a chairman.—The Chairman-: It appears things are not altogether as favourable as they might be for Mr. Skelton. but I cannot help that, I have nothing to do with that. All I can say is that a great many people in the township are inclined for a change.—Mr. Skelton I deny that. (Hear, hear.)—Mr. Thompson: Why didn't you tell Mr. Skelton that before the election was over? It is a most dishonourable thing. Mr. Skelton has been badly treated.—Mr. Skelton: I offered to retire in favour of Mr. Bate on Saturday but he would not hear of it.—Mr. Halton called upon the Chairman to put the amendment to the meeting.— Mr. Thompson: Will you consent then. Mr. Bate, to be the chairman.—Mr. Bate : Yea I will.—Mr. Thompson : I am very much surprised.—The amendment was then put and carried, and Mr. Bate was accordingly elected chairman of the Council. Mr. Kilshaw moved,and Mr. Taylor aeconded,that Mr. Halton be the vice-ehairman•—Mr. Thompson moved that Mr. Gorse be elected to the position, but Mr. Gorse said he would not accept it under any consideration.— Mr. Halton was accordingly elected. • Mr. Halton moved that the chairman, with Mr. Skel- ton and Mr. John Kilshaw, be the overseers for the ensu- ing year. He thought they wanted an extra overseer considering the growth of the parish.—Mr. Skelton de- clined to be one of the officers, and said it was not within the power of the Council to appoint more than two.—The motion ,therefore, fell to the ground, and Mr. Thompson proposed that Mr. Bate and Mr. Taylor be everseers.—Mr. Gorse seconded,and it was carried. Mr. Gorse said he would like to know it anyone was taking the mmutes.—The Clerk : It is all right.—Mr. Glrse said he had not seen any taken.—The Clerk then said that Mr. Clarke was taking the minutes.—Mr. Clarke: No! no! lam not taking anything. lam sitting here as an outsider.—Mr. Gorse hoped the busi- ness would be done in a straightforward manner.—Mr. Halton thought Mr. Wright was perfectly capable of taking minutes himself.—The Clerk: I am not going to take anything of what has passed to-night.—The Chair- man said it was very essential that there should be some minutes taken.—The Clerk then proceeded to take the minutes. It was decided that a precept for £l5 be made on the overseers. It was stated that the cost of the election was £9 15s. sd. Mr. Flalton dilated at some length on the necessity of the London and North-Western Railway Company widening the canal bridge at Sankey Bridges.—After some discussion, in which the opinion WAR expressed that it was a matter for the Highway Board, it was ultimately decided that the Railway Company should be written to on the matter. Mr. Gorse afterwards left the room, saying that he could not remain whilst Mr. Halton acted as chairman and vice-chairman.—Mr.Halton : Obtvery good ; I shall be delighted, I am sure.—Mr. Thompson remarked that the business was disposed of on party lines. It was a case of "follow the leader.—Mr. Halton : That is rather a peculiar charge to make. Do you suppose we arrange it at my house ?—Mr. Thompson: I don't say that, but the business seems to be cut and dried before you come to the room. I should be very pleased if it was trans- acted here only, and not elsewhere. After an animated discussion it was decided that the meetings of the Council should be held once in three months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>SHOCKING SUICIDE AT WATtRINGTON.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING SUICIDE AT WATtRINGTON.</p>
        <p>INQUEST AND VERDICT. On Wednesday evening. Mr. Brighouse,coroner,held an inquest in the Parade Room at the Police Station on the body of William Warburtnn, 63 years of age, who com- mitted suicide at his residence. 58. Pinners'-brow, on Tuesday afternoon, by shooting himself. John Warburton, wiredrawer, 14. West-street, said the deceased, who waz his father, was a wiredrawer,but for the last six months had been unable to follow his employ- ment owing to ill-health. Re had been under the care of Dr. Kisby, as he suffered from bronchitis and asthma. He last saw his father alive on Monday,and he had then no reason to believe he would take away his life. The small cannon now produced, with which it was sup- posed the deceased had killed himself, had been in the Possession of the family as long as witness could recollect; he used to fire it himself when he was a lad. Inspector Sninks said the cannon was kept in the coal hole under the stairs. It had been in the possession of the family for over 80 years. Elizabeth Tattersall (14) said the deceased vas her uncle. She did the housework for him and his mother. Sae lived with them during the day, but went home to APep. Her uncle went out about two o'clock on Tuesday afternoon for a walk. Vie returned about half-past three, and appeared to be all right. He sat on the sofa, and soon afterwards complained of a pain in his head. He asked his mother, who is 84 years of age and is blind, if he could get her tea, but she replied " Not now." Her uncle then said he Neuld go downstairs for a few min- utes. All this conversation took place in the living room upstairs. Inspector Spinks : This is one of the old landmarks of Warrington. The living room is upstairs. Witness, continuing, said that when the deceased had been away ten minutes she heard a loud report. She ran downstairs, and found that her uncle's head was" rest- ing on his shsulder." His clothes appeared to he on fire She at once sent for a medical man. and Dr. Adams shortly afterwards arrived. Her uncle had never said, to her knowledge, that he was tired of life, James Darrnall, a lahourer,emnloyed at Dill am Forge, and living at 54, Pinners'-hrow, said on Tuesday after- noon, at about teo minutes to five he was standing at his house door when he heard a loud report, and a few minutes afterwards the last witness came up to him screaming. She told him what had occnrred, and wit- ness and his brother went into the cell,r, where they found the deceased sitting on a box with his back against the wall. His clothes on his left side were on fire. They put out the flames, when they saw that blood was flowing from a large wound in Warburton's left breast. He was dead. Two of his fingers were broken infl bleeding. Near to the deceased wits the firearm produced. There was a strong smell of powder in the cellar. Sergeant Smith deposed to finding the deceased as described by the last witness. He had known the de- ceased for some years ; he was a most inoffensive man. The piece of lead now produced, weighing 21 ounces, had gone right through the body, had struck the wall behind, and lodged in the man's clothes. The weapon must have been held close to the body when fired. Ernest Henry Napper,assistant to Mr. Hugh Greenough, chemist, Horsemarket-st., said the deceased called at the shop on Tuesdly morning at ten o'cleck, and was sup- plied with some powder. It was sporting powder that was sold to him ; it was a better quality than the pow- derusually soli. There was nothing in deceased's man- ner to excite suspicion. The Coroner having addressed the jury, a verdict to the effect that the deceased committed suicide during temporary insanity was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>EA.RLESTOWN A.ND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EA.RLESTOWN A.ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.</p>
        <p>At the Police Station on Wednesday morning, bafore Mr. J. W. Emmett, Bernard Killryde of St. Helens, was charged with being drunk and disor- derly on Tuesday afternoon in Market-street, and fined 53. and costs. In default he was sent to gaol for 14 days. DRUNKENNESS. _ _ _</p>
        <p>At the Es.rlesto-w-n-- Police Station on Monday morning before Mr. J. W. Emmett, Polly Bowers of no fixed residence, and Annie Cheetham were charged with being drunk and incapable on Satur- day night. Bowers was fined 51. and costs and in default was sent to gaol for 7 days; Cheetham was dischaNed on promising to leave the town. FANCY DRESS BALL AT EARLESTOWN. Quite a novelty,so tar as Earlestown is concerned will be introduced at the Town Hall on Wednesday evening next, when, as will he seen from an adver- tisement, Mrs. Latimer's juvenile fancy dress ball will take place. A very large numbei of children are taking the liveliest interest in the affair, and with the hearty co-operation of their parents, the event should prove highly interesting. The general public will be admitted to the hall,as spectators, on production of ticket, which may be purchased as announced in the advertisement, and as the object for which the ball is being got up is a worthy one, it is hoped a good number will be present. UNITED SUNDAY SCHOOLS !WALKING DAY. A meeting of the representatives of the various Nonconformist Sundky schools in Earlestown, was held in the Lecture Hall of the Wesleyan Chapel on Monday evening,for the purpose of making arrange- ments in connection with the Whit Monday demon- stration. The result of the draw for positions in the procession, was as follows :—Viaduct brass band, Wesleyan Sunday school, Vulcan Wesleya,n, Salva- tion Army, Baptist, Welsh, Sutton-road band, Primitive Methodists. The route w:11 be exactly the same as last year, excepting that the procession will go to the end of Cross-lane. Mr. J. Taylor was elected secretary and Mr. T Clare, conductor. The schools will assemble on the Market Square at 10 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WAHGRA.VH DAY SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>WAHGRA.VH DAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The children who took part in the successful con- cert held at Wargrave day schools a few weeks ago were entertained to tea on Friday evening in the schoolroom. The wants of the children were attended to by the Rector (Rev. .J. Carson), Siesdames .Niolyneux, Slee, Purnell, White, Sweetlove, Shaw, MiBS Carson, Mr. R. Chandley,and the teachers. After disposing of the meal,the chil- dren adjourhed for outdoor games,and on re-assem- bling were presented with fruit. Votes of thanks to the la lies and the Rector were afterwards heartily accorded, and the children dispersed. EARLEST oWN FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening in the Town Hall, Earles- town, an entertainment was given in aid of the funds of the above club, by St. Thomas's Darkies of St. Helens. There was a very fair audience, and a good programme of minstrel songs and choruses was fairlyiwell given.iThe second part of the programme included ;'ll stump speech, and a character song by W. Adamson entitled "The football referee." The whole entertainment concluded with a farce en- titled "Lodgings to let," the characters in which were ably sustained by W. Adamson. T. Nichols, W. Harrison, J. Watkinson,and J. Smith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</head>
        <p>SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of the cadets of temperance was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, on Wednesday night at seven o'clock. The worthy patron presided. After opening, four new members were installed for the present quar- ter. The Britain's Need DiVIAOII held their meet- ing at eight o'clock in the same room Bro. B. Yorke presided. A discuz,sion took place on members being admitted on clearance cards. The representatives to the Grand Division gave their report of the Grand Division meeting on Saturday last. The financial scribe read his quarterly report, end the auditors reported that they examined the books and found aa correct.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>WARGRA,VB CHUBCII.</head>
        <p>WARGRA,VB CHUBCII.</p>
        <p>Special anniversary services in aid of the War- grave and Vulcan day schools were hell in Em- manuel Church, Wargrave, on Sunday. The Itsv.F. A. Bartlett, M.A., vicar of St. Michael's, Liverpool, was announced to conduct the morning service, but owing to indisposition he was unable to be pre- sent, and his place was filled by the &amp;iv. Mr. Cole- grove. There was a good congregation,the anthem being "Unto Thee have I cried," the solo being tastefully rendered by Miss A. Molyneux. The evening service was conducted by the Rev. J. A. N. Sheale, M.A., curate of Parr Mount, and the choir sang Sullivan's anthem, `.I will sing of Thy power." At each service a number of scholars attending the Wargrave schools sang special hymns, having been specially trained by Mr. R. Chandley. In the after- noon a musical service way given, when a number of sacred solos and duets was given by ti.. ch3ir, together with selections on the organ by Mr. E. Leyland, organist. The collections amounted to £l4 10a.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLAR&amp;</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLAR&amp;</p>
        <p>The •=ession of he Hope ot Earlestown lodge of Good Templars on Monday evening,was preceded by a meeting of the Pride of Earlestown juvenile lodge. there was a large attendance of juveniles, Bro. E. Newman, S.J T., presiding. An interesting pro- gramme of songs, recitations, &amp;c., was given by the children, and at the close of a very successful meeting,nine book prizes were presented to various members of the temple as rewards. The adult lodge was afterwards opened, when there was a good at- tendance of members. Bro. G. Clarke presided. Two candidates were initated. The report of the No. 1 Convention held on the previous Saturday, at Tyl- desley was given by Sister E. Wright, V.T., and adopted. An invitation to visit the St. Helens lodge an Tuesday night was accepted, a number of the members intimating their intention of attend- ing. It was resolved to invite the district lodge of South West Lancashire to hold its August Session at Earlestown. The programme of the evening was "A talk about, the stare" by 8r0.13, Barker, sec.,and was of a most interesting character, his remarks bearing particularly upon the moon, and a number of members expressed their pleasure at hearing the address. Thanks were accorded the essayist. NEWTON CHORAL AND ORCHESTRAL SOCIETY'S CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The second concert given by this newly organised society, took place in the Town Hall, Newton, on Saturday evening, when J. More-Sweeton's cantata " King Arthur" was given. In making this selec- tion, the seciety wisely limited the tax on their abilities, and thereby enabled the choir to give a much better interpretation of the work than one produced at the former concert. Toe special fea- ture was the singing of the ladies, who had a great deal of work to do in the choruses, their voices being exceedingly sweet throughout. The male voices were also of great quality, and the choruses as a whole were taken with precision, the attack being especially good. The soloists were Miss F. Hiller (soprano), Mr. George Green (tenor), Mr. W. H. Wallington (baritone). Mr. C. R. Crossley, gave a 'cello solo which had a deep effect on the audience by reason of the reeling he infused in the music. The orchestra showed much improvement, though there was a noticeable lack of wind instruments. Mr. F. H. Crossley conducted with ability, and Mr. A. H. Wykes was an efficient accompanist. There waa only a moderate audience.</p>
        <p>TO MOTHERS.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrup nas oeen usea over fifty years by millions of mothers for their children while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; It produces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain, and the little cherub awakes "as bright as a button." Of all chemists, la. I_d. per bottle.—Ada.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AT HELSBY.—On Monday, at Frodsham, before Mr. Timmas,J.P., Martin McQuinn was charged with be- ing drunk at Helsby. The case was proved by Acting- Sergeant Hunt, and defendant was fined 53. including costs.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.—The meeting of this Coun- cil was held in the Court-room. Frodsham, on Monday evening ; present: Messrs. Thomas Earlam, George Rogers, Joseph Illidge,John Wilkinson, James Edwards, WE. Shore, Joseph G)rker. P. Riley, J. Barrow, W. Renshaw, William Helsby, and William Booth.—Mr. Earlam was proposed as chairman by Mr. Shore, se- conded by Mr. Riley. As an amendment Mr. Earlam proposed Mr. Heywood to be re-elected and this met with a seconder in Mr. Illidge. Mr. Corker them proposed Mr. Shore for the position and this was seconded. On the names being put to the meeting Mr. Reys%ood received seven votes and Mr. Shore three, Mr. Heywood being therefore elected. Messrs. C. E. Linaker and J. W. Both- well were re-elected overseers for the year,and Mr. J. B. Holland was appointLd treasurer to the Council. The committees were then appointed as follows :—Bouu- defies Committee, Messrs. Shore,lllidge, Helsby,Earlam, Edwards. Cork.r, and Riley. Footpaths Committee, Messrs. Illidge, Wilkinson, Booth, Youd. Edwards, and Flenshaw. Charities, Messrs. Edwards, Earlam,Barrow, and Illidge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Five victories in succession is rather a new experience for the Warrington club, and it is somewhat unfortunate that this remarkable return to their old form should come at the fag end of the season. One more match and then the curtain f ills on another season's Rugby football in Warrington. The two latest victories to chronicle are those over Widnes at Chemicalopolis, and the well- known Rangers at Broughton. Both teams are lower in the combiration table than Warrington, but still, seeing that both matches were away from home—which means a great deal—the wirepullers are to be congratulated upon the result.</p>
        <p>The match at Widnes on Saturday was witnessed by perhaps as many Warrington spectators as Widnesians. From start to finish the home team were outplayed at every point, and with the exception of one flash of bril- liant play by Brennan, the Widnes full back, who was chiefly instrumental in scoring the try notched in the first iew nalnutes of the match, the chemical men did not cause the visitors much trouble in keeping their lines intact. Of the Warrington tip.in it was difficult to point to a weak snot except it be the nability of several players to take full advantage of their opportunities to score. Although Warrington won by twelve points to three it would be more representative of the match if the Warrington score were double. To speak of the indi- vidual players. Burton was the flower of the three-quarter line. His judicious kicking was of great service to the visiting team,and called forth repeated cheers. Barber —the Warrington Barber of course—had as his ris a ris Martin (the lately imported player into the Widnes ranks) and the Warrington captain so completely out- played him throughout the match that Martin was pro- bably never so entirely " bottle,' " before. Heesom played a sterling game at back, whilst the two halv-s Higginbottom and Houghton—played together as if they were old mates in that department. The credit for the try scored by Higginbottom was, it is only fair to say, largely due to Houghton. The dropped goal of Higgin- bottom's was a really fine effort. Of the forwards,Daki n and Taylor were the best,both of whom are worthy of a place in the county fifteen.</p>
        <p>After Saturday's display it was generally thought that the match at Broughton on the following Monday would result in another victory for Warrington. This turned out to be the caseibut though Warrington won by seven points to nil, the homesters had very hard lines in not scoring in the first half, when they did most of the pressing. Fligginbottom once again crossed the line,but Taylor failed to increase the points. Burton, however, made amends by dt opping a fine goal. O'Brien,to u3e a familiar expresaion, was" off colour " entirely,but Hee- som played in Lorrimor fashion throughout the m itch.</p>
        <p>To-day, the representatives of Halifax visit the Wil- dersp)ol enclosure, and in some football circles they are considered to have an "easy thing on" in adding a couple of points to their already formidable score. Th. match is a most critical ene, for both Manningham and Hilifax are having a neck and neck struggle for the championship. If Manningham are defeated to-day at Flunslet, and Halifax beat Warrington,the men of Hall. fax town become champions of the Northern League. But there is a glorious uncertainty at out football,and in their present form,Warrington may perhaps wipe away EMMA of the cobwebs from Yorkshire eyes and reveal themselves in their true light b•,fore the football world.</p>
        <p>The combination table will be found appended, and from a glance at the Lancashire section it wit be se,ri that Warrington occupy the middle pnsition—five cluh. being above them and five below. Of the twenty matches played with Lancashire and Cheshire clubs, nine were won, seven lost, and four drawn, making a total of . _</p>
        <p>twenty-two points. The scores when totalled were eleven goals, thirteen tries by Warrington, to seven goals, eighteen tries by their opponents, or 86 points for Warrington against 84. This result, considering the exceptional difficulties met with at the beginning of the season, must be considered satisfactory.</p>
        <p>The match between Runcorn and Halifax on Mon- day night was another instance of the easy manner in which a match, almost hopeless may be snatched out of the fire. All through the game Runcorn had the maim part of play and in the first half Myers dropped a splendid goal. Halifax though overpla3 ed managed to get into the home quarters during the second half, and their back kicking over the line, a forwsrd dropped on the ball while a Runcorn player stood by and looked at him. A more simple try could not be scored. The ball went far enough to put Runcorn off their guard but not far enough to be dead and a Halifax forward by taking the only opportunity afforded his team scored in a posi- tion which made the conversion of the try an eas% matter. If Halifax come out now at the top of the Northern League they owe their success in a great measure to the luck the team ha.' at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire ands Chesbireisection is as fellows : Score Score Pts. Phi. Won Lost Drn For. Ag'st. Runcorn Wigan Oldham lB ... 11 ... 5 ... 2 ...168 ... 67 ... 21 Tyldesley Leigh Warrington Widnes *St. Helens Stockport Rochdale H l9 -. 3 ... 11 ... 5 ...42 ...143 ... H Bg'ht'n Ran lB ... 1 ... 11 ... 6 ... 32 ... 91 ... 8</p>
        <p>The Yorkshire section is as follows : Score Score Ils. Pld. Won. Lost. Drn For. Meat. Bfsrominemmi. 19 ... 16 .. 3 .. 0 -.150 .. 63 -. 31 Halifax Hunslat l9 -. 12 ... 6 2 ..119 ... 72 .- 26 Hull 2O ... 10 -. 9 -. 1 -.124 67 -. 21 Irghnuse Ibma 2O -. 9 -. 8 -. 3 -. 5 -. 60 -. 21 Leeds 2O -. 9 ... 9 -. 2 -. 83 -.118 -. 20 Bradford 2O 6 .. 9 lAversedge 2O 7 -. 11 -. 2 .-112 ..138 ~ 16 Wakefi'd Tray 18 -. 6 -. 12 1 .. 50 -.152 -. 11 Batley l9 -. 4 -. 12 3 -. 46 .-126 -. 11 Huddersfield- l9 -. 5 .. 13 .. 1 ... 75 -.107 .. 11</p>
        <p>The Northern Union is as follows : Score. Score. Phi. Won. Lost. Drn. For. Ag'st. Pta. anningham 41 ... 32 ... 9 ... 0 —343 ...158 ... 64 Halifax Oldham Runcorn 4l ... 23 ... 11 ... 8 ...306 ...139 ... 54 Beghouse Ran 4l ... 22 ... 11 ... 8 ...241 ...123 ... 52 1-1 unslet Hull Tyldesley Leigh Wigan 4l ... 19 ... 15 ... 7 ...245 ...139 ... 45 Viradford 4l ... 17 ... 15 ... 9 -.233 ...175 ... 43 Leeds Warrington *St.Helens 4O ... 14 ... 18 ... 8 ...166 ...220 Widnes Liversedge 4l 14 ... 23 ... 4 ...248 ...352 32 Stockport Wakefield T 40 ... 12 24 ... 4 ...129 3n2 ... 28 Batley 4O ... 10 ... 23 ... 7 ...115 ...290 ... 27 tßr'ght'n Ran. 40 ... 8 ... 24 ... 8 ...165 ...215 ... 24 Huddersfield... 41 ... 10 ... 27 ... 4 ...186 ...250 ... 24 Rochdale H 4l ... 4 ... 29 ... 8 -. 78 -.34 ... 16 * St Helens 2 points deductvd for breach of Rule 22. t The game between these clubs on Raster Monday has been awarded as a draw, and is so counted in this table.</p>
        <p>The two thousand persons who attended at the Wilderspool enclosure on Saturday to witness the contest between Widnes" A "and Warrington" A," expected to see a good gameeas the match decided whether Warring- ton would hold the championship. Both teams were well represented and as Warrington had beaten Widnes on the latter's ground it was thought that they should have no difficulty in doing so at home. But, alas, for expectations the match from all points of view was a poor one and the homesters instead of playing like "the best A ' team in Lancashire," as they are fondly called, gave an exhibition of extremely faulty tactics. rhe consequence was that they were beateniby a placed goal to a penalty goal.</p>
        <p>Play was noticeably bad at half-back,Clarke and Foden instead of feeding the threequarters chose to kick up field or make ineffective rushes which were always checked. The tbreequarter line was certainly strong enough, and if it had been given opportunities would very probably have altered the position of affairs. Only now and then did they gotthe ball however, and even tkeli the chances were against them. Forward, the two teams were fairly well matched in strength, though the home eight were superior in working the ball out. At full back for Warrington,Ashurst played a weak game.</p>
        <p>The victors certainly had the best of three parts of the game and were entitled to their win. They had a very smart threequarter line, thief among whom was !lamp- son.late of St. Mary's, who scored the try,and converted it from a rather acute angle. Some of the passing move- ments among the backs were well combined and more than once they looked very like scoring.</p>
        <p>CYCLINO. The splendid weather of Saturday last. tempted 00111 40 members of the Warrington Cycling Club into tn. ing out for a circblar run. Starting from headquartol the partyftravelled via., Arley, Budworth, and Pickiners's to Plumbley. Here an association 'football matcb played between teams selected by the captain 0.7. secretary ,the captain's team winning by four goals tot, Afterwards tea was partaken of at the Sal,.4„erts. Plumbley, and the return journey was made ford, after a most enjoyable afternoon.</p>
        <p>LOCAL CLA.INI AGAINST THE L. &amp; NY` RAILWAY COMPANY.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court, on ThursdaY.bePis his Hotiour Judge Wynne lefoulkes, a case was hear(' which Mr. Edwar Leigh, hay dealer, claimed the of £2 18s. 61. from the London and North-Western .„/ way Company, for loss sustained by the delay in deli/7_ of a lead of hay by the defendant company. Mr. C. Preston (Messrs. Grundy, Kershaw, and Co., chester), represented the plaintiff, and Mr. Fenna,gaw" tor, appeared on behalf of the Railway Company. is In opening the case, Mr. Preston said the baY question was loaded at Heatley Station, b7.„01. Clarke farmer, Heatley, on the Ist December, I°'"' and forwarding instructions were sent to the CI; pany on the following day. The hay did net resni its destination at Guide Bridge until the 21st..:ig the same month.—llis Honour: I have an jute': in the defendant company. Fleve you any objectil to my trying t.Via case.—Mr. Prestion . (Xi no, Honour.—Continuing Mr. Preston said that in can quenee of the delay in delivery of the hay, the plai!';, lost the order he had for it, and hid to dispose of if best he could. The claim represented the differecCe_Aw." tween whae he realised and what he would have receirt" had the company made prompt delivery. Mr. Leigh stated that he gave an order to Mr. of Heaney for fifty cwts of hay on the Ist Decetobebl expecting that it would arrive at the Guide Britt., station shortly afterwards. He called at the station ea; day for a week, but finding that it did not arrive, wrote to Clarke who 'replied that the hay lay at Header station awaiting his removal. About the 13th or 141 December, witness had a conversation with Clarke aboc the hay. He did not receive the hay until the 21st De' cember, and the Coiliery Company from whom be re; ceived the order then refused to have it. Witness to Dukinfield and disposed of it at 70. a stone by ate contract. That was a loss on the contract price £2 10e., and sz. ior cirtage trom Gaiae Bridge.—CroSt examined by Mr. Fenna, witness said he was not av/P., ot any special contract having been entered into betWer. Mr. Clarke and the tiailway Company. He did not givv notice to the railway company that he was under a OP tract to deliver the hay. The price of hay on the December was B±l. a stone. The reason he had net ri stituted proceeeings befere was that he fully expean the company to pay him. He had written more twenty times to them, and at last was compelled to proce-sclings. Richard Edward Clarke, Warburton Park, HeatleP said he received instructions on the Ist December to for ward the hay, and his men loaded two tons, ten cam' the same thy. He considered that the hay should ne_7l reached its destination in a day or two. Witness ho,7g. from Mr. Leigh on the 10th December that he had IS received the hay, and he went to the station and fon.„J'' that it was still there. He did not remember rf.• stationmaster giving a reason why it did not go. Bee,' , ceived 41. a stone from he plaintiff for it.—Cross-e"' ' ' amined by Mr. Fetina,witness admitted that the statiolr master at Heatley told him that the reason for not sou' , in. off ttie hay was that he had not received forwardin,,,S instructions, and the company did not know where send it to. Be admitted that he signed the special c' sianinent nete produce:]. For the defence, Mr. Fenna, dwelt largely on the ; that the sender, Mr. Clarke, had signed a special exPr tract which stated on the note that it " relieved tbb comp iny from all liability by loss, dimaae,mis•delivft delay or detention."—His Honour said it looked to nt ' as if the contract was unreasomable because it absolv.°.- the company iron) all acts of negligence.--the Ferl.L"' quoted a decision in the action of White against rs, Great Western Railway Company, in which the seOM under that contract rendered himself liable to all 1u; occasioned by delay.—Mr. Fenna was proceeding ,yd speak with regard to the noint of loss of profit, when r; iioueur said it was a mere question between the venoLl and the purchaser and whether the responsibility wl geverned by the contract entered into on the 11'" December, Mr. Preston, for the plaintiff, contended that el stationmaster who was taken ill at the time, forgot about the hay, and then handed the special contraCt ; Mr. Clarke to sign so as to absolve the companY f.r2o. blame. Even supposing that instructions were net to forward the goods until the 11th December, there</p>
