      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-04-25_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>The rules and fixtures of the Warrington Cricket League have just been issued in printed form. Among the laws of the League it is laid down that the League shall comprise six clubs for the time being; that the Committee shall provide eleven medals which will be handed over to the champion club as soon as the same is ascertained. The scoring will be reckoned two points for a win and one point for a draw, and the club with the highest number of points at the end of the season will be the champion club. Rule 25 says the club at the bottom of the League at the end of the season must r(- tirc., but shall be eligible for re-election. There is bound to be one at the bottom, but which of the six will it be? The small number of teams will make that pretty well clear before the season is terminat sd. so that the prl- spective kind-most club will be able to accustom itself to its impending fate before the doom is sealed. The Hat of fixtures for each club shows that some good matches will be played, and also that players will have some pleasant outs. A successful season seems to be almost assured, and once started the League ought to thrive.</p>
